var e={firstLinearPair:{title:`I.13 · the first pair totals two right angles`,text:`ΑΕ stands on ΓΔ. Its two adjacent angles ΓΕΑ and ΑΕΔ therefore equal two right angles together. The intersecting lines extend through Ε; no line is merely touching another at an endpoint.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:13},secondLinearPair:{title:`I.13 · the second pair has the same total`,text:`ΔΕ stands on ΑΒ. The pair ΑΕΔ and ΔΕΒ also totals two right angles. ΑΕΔ is common to both pairs, even when the two lines meet obliquely.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:13},compareLinearPairs:{title:`Common notion 1 · equal totals`,text:`Both pairs equal two right angles, so the pairs equal one another. This compares totals, not the individual adjacent angles.`,kind:`deduction`},verticalRemainders:{title:`Common notion 3 · subtract the shared angle`,text:`Remove ΑΕΔ from both pairs. The two remaining angles, ΓΕΑ and ΒΕΔ, are vertically opposite and equal. Repeat with the other shared angle to obtain the second opposite pair.`,kind:`deduction`},exteriorMidpoint:{title:`I.10 · bisect the side ΑΓ`,text:`Construct the midpoint Ε with I.10. The plate computes it by intersecting the angle bisector with the given segment, rather than assigning an arithmetic midpoint as a construction permission.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:10},exteriorCopy:{title:`Extend ΒΕ and lay off ΕΖ equal to ΒΕ`,text:`Continue ΒΕ beyond Ε and choose Ζ with ΕΖ = ΒΕ; join ΖΓ and extend ΑΓ past Γ to Η. The source gives these commands without a separate citation for copying the length. Here I.2 supplies a length at Ε, then a circle at Ε cuts the continuation. The copied length is not silently assumed to be a new compass permission.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:2},exteriorPairs:{title:`The midpoint and the copied length`,text:`ΑΕ = ΕΓ from I.10, and ΒΕ = ΕΖ by construction. These are the two corresponding side pairs of ΑΒΕ and ΓΖΕ. The angle needed next is the one included between them.`,kind:`deduction`},exteriorVertical:{title:`I.15 · the included angles are vertically opposite`,text:`The lines ΑΓ and ΒΖ cross at Ε. Their vertically opposite angles ΑΕΒ and ΖΕΓ are equal by I.15. We now have two sides and their included angle, not two sides and an arbitrary angle.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:15},exteriorSAS:{title:`I.4 · transfer the opposite interior angle`,text:`The two side pairs and the equal included angles give ΑΒ = ΖΓ and equality of the triangles and remaining corresponding angles by I.4. In particular ΒΑΕ = ΕΓΖ. This places a copy of the interior angle inside the exterior angle.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:4},exteriorPart:{title:`Common notion 5 · the exterior contains a proper part`,text:`Ray ΓΖ lies strictly inside exterior angle ΕΓΔ in this nondegenerate plane triangle. Thus ΕΓΔ is larger than ΕΓΖ, which equals ΒΑΕ. The strict containment, not an assumed triangle-angle sum, supplies the inequality.`,kind:`deduction`},exteriorSecond:{title:`Repeat the construction for the other interior angle`,text:`The source says to bisect ΒΓ and reason similarly. The plate makes that omitted drawing explicit: Θ is the midpoint of ΒΓ, and ΑΘ is extended to Κ with ΘΚ = ΘΑ. These two labels are modern additions. I.15 and I.4 give ΘΓΚ = ΑΒΓ inside ΒΓΗ; I.15 also gives ΒΓΗ = ΑΓΔ. The same exterior angle is therefore larger than the other opposite interior angle too.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:15}},t={15:{id:15,numeral:`I.15`,greekNumeral:`ιεʹ`,title:`Subtract a shared angle to find the opposite pair`,shortTitle:`Vertical angles`,introduction:`Two crossing lines make two different linear pairs. Subtract their shared angle and the vertically opposite remainders must be equal. ‘Vertical’ here means opposite at the vertex, not upright on the screen.`,statement:{grc:`Ἐὰν δύο εὐθεῖαι τέμνωσιν ἀλλήλας, τὰς κατὰ κορυφὴν γωνίας ἴσας ἀλλήλαις ποιοῦσιν.`,lat:`Si duae rectae inter se secant, angulos ad uerticem positos inter se aequales efficiunt.`,en:`If two straight lines cut one another, they make the vertically opposite angles equal to one another.`,greekPages:[40],latinPages:[41]},steps:[{title:`Let two straight lines cross at Ε`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Δύο γὰρ εὐθεῖαι αἱ ΑΒ, ΓΔ τεμνέτωσαν ἀλλήλας κατὰ τὸ Ε σημεῖον· λέγω, ὅτι ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ μὲν ὑπὸ ΑΕΓ γωνία τῇ ὑπὸ ΔΕΒ, ἡ δὲ ὑπὸ ΓΕΒ τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΕΔ.`,lat:`Nam duae rectae ΑΒ, ΓΔ inter se secent in puncto Ε. dico, esse ∠ ΑΕΓ = ΔΕΒ et ∠ ΓΕΒ = ΑΕΔ.`,en:`Let ΑΒ and ΓΔ cut one another at Ε. I claim that ΑΕΓ equals ΔΕΒ and ΓΕΒ equals ΑΕΔ.`,greekPages:[40],latinPages:[41],language:`ἡ μέν … ἡ δέ names the first angle and then the second. In every three-letter angle name, Ε in the middle identifies the common vertex. Opposite pairs do not share a ray.`},{title:`Use the linear pair on ΓΔ`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`firstLinearPair`,grc:`Ἐπεὶ γὰρ εὐθεῖα ἡ ΑΕ ἐπ᾽ εὐθεῖαν τὴν ΓΔ ἐφέστηκε γωνίας ποιοῦσα τὰς ὑπὸ ΓΕΑ, ΑΕΔ, αἱ ἄρα ὑπὸ ΓΕΑ, ΑΕΔ γωνίαι δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς ἴσαι εἰσίν.`,lat:`nam quoniam recta ΑΕ super rectam ΓΔ erecta est angulos efficiens ΓΕΑ, ΑΕΔ, anguli ΓΕΑ, ΑΕΔ duobus rectis aequales sunt.`,en:`ΑΕ stands on ΓΔ, making ΓΕΑ and ΑΕΔ. Thus these two angles together equal two right angles.`,greekPages:[40],latinPages:[41],language:`ποιοῦσα / efficiens describes ΑΕ making the angles. δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς / duobus rectis compares their sum with two right angles, just as in I.13.`},{title:`Use the linear pair on ΑΒ`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`secondLinearPair`,grc:`πάλιν, ἐπεὶ εὐθεῖα ἡ ΔΕ ἐπ᾽ εὐθεῖαν τὴν ΑΒ ἐφέστηκε γωνίας ποιοῦσα τὰς ὑπὸ ΑΕΔ, ΔΕΒ, αἱ ἄρα ὑπὸ ΑΕΔ, ΔΕΒ γωνίαι δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς ἴσαι εἰσίν.`,lat:`rursus quoniam recta ΔΕ super rectam ΑΒ erecta est angulos efficiens ΑΕΔ, ΔΕΒ, anguli ΑΕΔ, ΔΕΒ duobus rectis aequales sunt.`,en:`Again, ΔΕ stands on ΑΒ, making ΑΕΔ and ΔΕΒ. This second pair also equals two right angles together.`,greekPages:[40],latinPages:[41],language:`πάλιν / rursus repeats I.13 with a different line standing on a different base. ΑΕΔ occurs in both pairs; it will be removed from both.`},{title:`The two pairs have the same total`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`compareLinearPairs`,grc:`ἐδείχθησαν δὲ καὶ αἱ ὑπὸ ΓΕΑ, ΑΕΔ δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς ἴσαι· αἱ ἄρα ὑπὸ ΓΕΑ, ΑΕΔ ταῖς ὑπὸ ΑΕΔ, ΔΕΒ ἴσαι εἰσίν.`,lat:`sed demonstratum est, etiam angulos ΓΕΑ, ΑΕΔ duobus rectis aequales esse. quare ΓΕΑ + ΑΕΔ = ΑΕΔ + ΔΕΒ.`,en:`ΓΕΑ and ΑΕΔ were also shown equal to two right angles together. Hence ΓΕΑ plus ΑΕΔ equals ΑΕΔ plus ΔΕΒ.`,greekPages:[40],latinPages:[41],language:`ἐδείχθησαν / demonstratum est refers back to the first pair. Greek names the paired angles in prose; the Latin now makes the equality of sums explicit with plus signs.`},{title:`Subtract ΑΕΔ and compare the remainders`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`verticalRemainders`,grc:`κοινὴ ἀφῃρήσθω ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΕΔ· λοιπὴ ἄρα ἡ ὑπὸ ΓΕΑ λοιπῇ τῇ ὑπὸ ΒΕΔ ἴση ἐστίν·`,lat:`subtrahatur, qui communis est, ∠ ΑΕΔ. itaque ΓΕΑ = ΒΕΔ.`,en:`Subtract the common angle ΑΕΔ. The remaining angle ΓΕΑ therefore equals the remaining angle ΒΕΔ.`,greekPages:[40],latinPages:[41],language:`λοιπή … λοιπῇ marks the two remainders after subtraction. ΒΕΔ reverses the outer letters of ΔΕΒ and names the same angle.`},{title:`Apply the same reasoning to the other pair`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`verticalRemainders`,grc:`ὁμοίως δὴ δειχθήσεται, ὅτι καὶ αἱ ὑπὸ ΓΕΒ, ΔΕΑ ἴσαι εἰσίν.`,lat:`similiter demonstrabimus, esse etiam ∠ ΓΕΒ = ΔΕΑ.`,en:`In the same way, ΓΕΒ and ΔΕΑ will also be shown equal.`,greekPages:[40],latinPages:[41],language:`ὁμοίως / similiter means ‘in the same way’. The source abbreviates the repeated argument. The plate changes the emphasized pair so that ‘also’ refers to a visibly different pair.`},{title:`Both vertically opposite pairs are equal`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`finish`,grc:`Ἐὰν ἄρα δύο εὐθεῖαι τέμνωσιν ἀλλήλας, τὰς κατὰ κορυφὴν γωνίας ἴσας ἀλλήλαις ποιοῦσιν· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo si duae rectae inter se secant, angulos ad uerticem positos inter se aequales efficiunt; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Therefore two straight lines cutting one another make the vertically opposite angles equal. This is what had to be shown.`,greekPages:[40],latinPages:[41],language:`κατὰ κορυφήν / ad uerticem describes opposition at the vertex. Neither expression says that the lines must be horizontal or vertical; flip the plate or change the angle to check the placement.`}],editionNote:{title:`A corollary printed only in the Greek`,grc:`[Πόρισμα. Ἐκ δὴ τούτου φανερὸν ὅτι, ἐὰν δύο εὐθεῖαι τέμνωσιν ἀλλήλας, τὰς πρὸς τῇ τομῇ γωνίας τέτρασιν ὀρθαῖς ἴσας ποιήσουσιν.]`,en:`[Corollary. It is clear from this that, if two straight lines cut one another, they make the angles at the intersection equal to four right angles together.]`,greekPages:[42],note:`Heiberg brackets this whole corollary on Greek p. 42. His facing Latin begins I.16 and provides no corresponding translation. The English here is our reading translation; no missing Latin has been supplied. The critical apparatus records differing witnesses.`},insight:{title:`Equality by subtraction`,text:`The equality does not depend on how the crossing looks. Two linear pairs share a total and an angle, so their remainders are equal. The diagram uses modern degree measurements only to check the current drawing. The bracketed Greek corollary below totals the four angles, while preserving this edition's missing Latin counterpart.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI15.html`},question:{prompt:`Which operation makes the opposite angles equal?`,options:[`Subtract the shared angle from two equal linear-pair totals`,`Assume all four angles are right`,`Add the two opposite angles and call their sum 180°`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. Common notion 3 makes the remainders equal.`,`The lines may meet obliquely. Only a perpendicular crossing gives four right angles.`,`Opposite angles are equal, but their sum need not equal two right angles.`]}},16:{id:16,numeral:`I.16`,greekNumeral:`ιϛʹ`,title:`Put an interior angle inside an exterior angle`,shortTitle:`The exterior angle`,introduction:`Bisect a side, extend a joining line, and use vertically opposite angles to copy an interior angle into the exterior. The whole is larger than its proper part. Repeat for the other interior angle.`,statement:{grc:`Παντὸς τριγώνου μιᾶς τῶν πλευρῶν προσεκβληθείσης ἡ ἐκτὸς γωνία ἑκατέρας τῶν ἐντὸς καὶ ἀπεναντίον γωνιῶν μείζων ἐστίν.`,lat:`In quouis triangulo uno latere producto angulus extrinsecus positus utrouis angulo interiore et opposito maior est.`,en:`If one side of any triangle is extended, the exterior angle is greater than either of the interior and opposite angles.`,greekPages:[42],latinPages:[43]},steps:[{title:`Extend ΒΓ past Γ to Δ`,phase:`Given`,reason:`p2`,grc:`Ἔστω τρίγωνον τὸ ΑΒΓ, καὶ προσεκβεβλήσθω αὐτοῦ μία πλευρὰ ἡ ΒΓ ἐπὶ τὸ Δ· λέγω, ὅτι ἡ ἐκτὸς γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΓΔ μείζων ἐστὶν ἑκατέρας τῶν ἐντὸς καὶ ἀπεναντίον τῶν ὑπὸ ΓΒΑ, ΒΑΓ γωνιῶν.`,lat:`Sit triangulus ΑΒΓ, et producatur unum latus eius ΒΓ ad Δ punctum. dico esse ∠ ΑΓΔ > ΓΒΑ et ΑΓΔ > ΒΑΓ.`,en:`Let ΑΒΓ be a triangle and extend its side ΒΓ to Δ. I claim that exterior angle ΑΓΔ is greater than each opposite interior angle ΓΒΑ and ΒΑΓ.`,greekPages:[42],latinPages:[43],language:`ἑκατέρας / utrouis means each of the two separately. The claim is an inequality with each interior angle, not an equality with their sum. The adjacent interior angle at Γ is excluded by ἀπεναντίον / opposito.`},{title:`Bisect ΑΓ at Ε`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`exteriorMidpoint`,grc:`Τετμήσθω ἡ ΑΓ δίχα κατὰ τὸ Ε,`,lat:`secetur ΑΓ in duas partes aequales in Ε,`,en:`Bisect ΑΓ at Ε.`,greekPages:[42],latinPages:[43],language:`τετμήσθω / secetur commands a cut; δίχα / in duas partes aequales specifies equal halves. This is I.10, now available as a proved construction.`},{title:`Join, extend, copy, and join again`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`exteriorCopy`,grc:`καὶ ἐπιζευχθεῖσα ἡ ΒΕ ἐκβεβλήσθω ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας ἐπὶ τὸ Ζ, καὶ κείσθω τῇ ΒΕ ἴση ἡ ΕΖ, καὶ ἐπεζεύχθω ἡ ΖΓ, καὶ διήχθω ἡ ΑΓ ἐπὶ τὸ Η.`,lat:`et ducta ΒΕ producatur in directum ad Ζ, et ponatur ΕΖ = ΒΕ, et ducatur ΖΓ, et educatur ΑΓ ad Η.`,en:`Join ΒΕ and extend it straight to Ζ, laying off ΕΖ equal to ΒΕ. Join ΖΓ and continue ΑΓ to Η.`,greekPages:[42],latinPages:[43],language:`ἐπιζευχθεῖσα / ducta gives the joining as already done before the extension command. ἐκβεβλήσθω, κείσθω, ἐπεζεύχθω and διήχθω form a sequence of distinct operations, not one instruction to draw the final shape.`},{title:`Collect the two equal side pairs`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`exteriorPairs`,grc:`Ἐπεὶ οὖν ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ μὲν ΑΕ τῇ ΕΓ, ἡ δὲ ΒΕ τῇ ΕΖ, δύο δὴ αἱ ΑΕ, ΕΒ δυσὶ ταῖς ΓΕ, ΕΖ ἴσαι εἰσὶν ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ·`,lat:`iam quoniam ΑΕ = ΕΓ et ΒΕ = ΕΖ, duae rectae ΑΕ, ΕΒ duabus ΓΕ, ΕΖ aequales sunt altera alteri.`,en:`ΑΕ equals ΕΓ, and ΒΕ equals ΕΖ. Thus the two sides ΑΕ, ΕΒ equal ΓΕ, ΕΖ respectively.`,greekPages:[42],latinPages:[43],language:`ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ / altera alteri keeps the correspondence ordered: ΑΕ with ΓΕ, ΕΒ with ΕΖ. It does not say all four lengths are equal.`},{title:`Use I.15 at the crossing Ε`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`exteriorVertical`,grc:`καὶ γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΕΒ γωνίᾳ τῇ ὑπὸ ΖΕΓ ἴση ἐστίν· κατὰ κορυφὴν γάρ·`,lat:`et ∠ ΑΕΒ = ΖΕΓ (nam ad uerticem eius est).`,en:`Angle ΑΕΒ equals ΖΕΓ, for they are vertically opposite.`,greekPages:[42],latinPages:[43],language:`γάρ / nam supplies the reason, compressed into ‘vertically opposite’. The previous lesson turns that short phrase into a proved equality. Ε is the vertex of both included angles.`},{title:`Apply side–angle–side`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`exteriorSAS`,grc:`βάσις ἄρα ἡ ΑΒ βάσει τῇ ΖΓ ἴση ἐστίν, καὶ τὸ ΑΒΕ τρίγωνον τῷ ΖΕΓ τριγώνῳ ἐστὶν ἴσον, καὶ αἱ λοιπαὶ γωνίαι ταῖς λοιπαῖς γωνίαις ἴσαι εἰσὶν ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ, ὑφ᾽ ἃς αἱ ἴσαι πλευραὶ ὑποτείνουσιν·`,lat:`itaque basis ΑΒ basi ΖΓ aequalis est et △ ΑΒΕ = ΖΕΓ, et reliqui anguli reliquis aequales sunt alter alteri, sub quibus aequalia latera subtendunt.`,en:`Therefore ΑΒ equals ΖΓ, the triangles ΑΒΕ and ΖΕΓ are equal, and their remaining angles are equal respectively where the equal sides subtend them.`,greekPages:[42],latinPages:[43],language:`λοιπαί / reliqui now means the remaining angles after the included one. The source's triangle naming does not list vertices in matching correspondence; use the stated side pairs to read the correspondence Α ↔ Γ, Β ↔ Ζ, Ε ↔ Ε.`},{title:`Identify the copied interior angle`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`exteriorSAS`,grc:`ἴση ἄρα ἐστὶν ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΑΕ τῇ ὑπὸ ΕΓΖ.`,lat:`itaque ∠ ΒΑΕ = ΕΓΖ.`,en:`In particular, ΒΑΕ equals ΕΓΖ.`,greekPages:[42],latinPages:[43],language:`ἄρα / itaque draws out the one corresponding angle needed next. Because Ε lies on ΑΓ, ΒΑΕ is the original interior angle ΒΑΓ.`},{title:`The exterior angle contains that angle as a proper part`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`exteriorPart`,grc:`μείζων δέ ἐστιν ἡ ὑπὸ ΕΓΔ τῆς ὑπὸ ΕΓΖ· μείζων ἄρα ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΓΔ τῆς ὑπὸ ΒΑΕ.`,lat:`uerum ∠ ΕΓΔ > ΕΓΖ. quare ∠ ΑΓΔ > ΒΑΕ.`,en:`But ΕΓΔ is greater than its part ΕΓΖ. Therefore exterior angle ΑΓΔ is greater than ΒΑΕ.`,greekPages:[42,44],latinPages:[43,45],language:`μείζων with the genitive τῆς means ‘greater than’. The sentence crosses the page in both languages. ΕΓΔ and ΑΓΔ name the same exterior angle because Ε lies on ΓΑ.`},{title:`Bisect ΒΓ and repeat for the other interior angle`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`exteriorSecond`,grc:`Ὁμοίως δὴ τῆς ΒΓ τετμημένης δίχα δειχθήσεται καὶ ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΓΗ, τουτέστιν ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΓΔ, μείζων καὶ τῆς ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ.`,lat:`similiter recta ΒΓ in duas partes aequales secta demonstrabitur etiam ∠ ΒΓΗ > ΑΒΓ, h. e. ∠ ΑΓΔ > ΑΒΓ.`,en:`Similarly, after bisecting ΒΓ, ΒΓΗ will be shown greater than ΑΒΓ too. That is, ΑΓΔ is greater than ΑΒΓ.`,greekPages:[44],latinPages:[45],language:`ὁμοίως / similiter calls for a second construction, not merely a second numerical check of the first one. τουτέστιν / h. e. identifies ΒΓΗ with the equal vertically opposite angle ΑΓΔ. The plate's Θ and Κ make the abbreviated second construction visible; those labels are our additions.`},{title:`The exterior is greater than each opposite interior angle`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`finish`,grc:`Παντὸς ἄρα τριγώνου μιᾶς τῶν πλευρῶν προσεκβληθείσης ἡ ἐκτὸς γωνία ἑκατέρας τῶν ἐντὸς καὶ ἀπεναντίον γωνιῶν μείζων ἐστίν· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo in quouis triangulo uno latere producto angulus extrinsecus positus utrouis angulo interiore et opposito maior est; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Therefore, when one side of any triangle is extended, the exterior angle is greater than each of the opposite interior angles. This is what had to be shown.`,greekPages:[44],latinPages:[45],language:`παντός / quouis makes the claim general. The proof uses the earlier constructions, I.4, I.15 and the whole–part principle. It has not used the parallel postulate or a triangle-angle-sum theorem.`}],insight:{title:`A strict inequality before the parallel postulate`,text:`I.16 proves that the exterior angle is greater than either opposite interior angle. It does not yet prove the later equality with their sum. The key move is construction: copy one interior angle into a proper part of the exterior, then repeat with the other side bisected. At step 9 the plate draws that second construction explicitly.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI16.html`},question:{prompt:`Why does the proof bisect ΒΓ after already bisecting ΑΓ?`,options:[`To place a copy of the other opposite interior angle inside ΒΓΗ, which equals ΑΓΔ`,`To make all three interior angles equal`,`To assume the exterior angle equals the sum of the opposite interior angles`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. The repeated construction supplies the second inequality; I.15 relates the two exterior angles.`,`The triangle is general, with no equal-angle assumption.`,`That stronger equality belongs to a later theorem. It is not a premise of I.16.`]}}},n={i3ForAngle:{title:`I.3 · obtain equal lengths on the two rays`,text:`Choose Δ on ΑΒ closer to Α than the available endpoint Γ. I.3 then cuts ΑΕ equal to ΑΔ from ΑΓ. The model actually calls that earlier construction; it does not choose Ε by visually reflecting Δ.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:3},i1ForAngle:{title:`I.1 · construct an equilateral triangle on ΔΕ`,text:`The two circles of I.1 supply two possible vertices. Choose Ζ on the side of ΔΕ opposite Α, as in the printed figure. This keeps ΑΖ inside the given angle. The alternative crossing can work, collapse at Α, or put the ray outside the angle; inspect those cases with the plate controls.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:1},commonBisector:{title:`The obtained equal lengths and one common side`,text:`I.3 supplied ΑΔ = ΑΕ. ΑΖ belongs to both triangles: comparing it with itself does not require another construction. These are two of the three side pairs needed for I.8.`,kind:`given`},equilateralBase:{title:`The remaining equal sides come from I.1`,text:`ΔΖ and ΕΖ are sides of the equilateral triangle ΔΕΖ. Together with ΑΔ = ΑΕ and common ΑΖ, this supplies every side pair of triangles ΔΑΖ and ΕΑΖ.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:1},i8:{title:`I.8 · three side pairs give the equal angles`,text:`Compare ΔΑΖ with ΕΑΖ: ΔΑ = ΕΑ, ΑΖ is common, and ΔΖ = ΕΖ. I.8 gives ∠ΔΑΖ = ∠ΕΑΖ. The chosen position of Ζ places these adjacent angles inside the given angle, so their equality really bisects it.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:8},i1ForMidpoint:{title:`I.1 · equal sides above the given base`,text:`Construct the equilateral triangle ΑΒΓ. In particular, ΑΓ = ΒΓ. Its two original circles also provide the other intersection, which can be reused when applying I.9.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:1},i9:{title:`I.9 · bisect the angle at Γ`,text:`Apply the angle-bisector construction at Γ. Here Α and Β are already available points at equal distances from Γ. The other intersection of I.1’s circles completes the equilateral triangle needed by I.9. Join Γ to that intersection; Δ is where the resulting line crosses ΑΒ.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:9},midpointPairs:{title:`Equal equilateral sides and the common bisector`,text:`I.1 supplies ΑΓ = ΒΓ, while ΓΔ is a common side of ΑΓΔ and ΒΓΔ. The two side pairs have been identified without assuming anything about ΑΔ and ΒΔ.`,kind:`given`},bisectedAngle:{title:`The angle equality is the result of I.9`,text:`I.9 gives ∠ΑΓΔ = ∠ΒΓΔ at Γ. It is the included angle between the two equal side pairs, exactly the angle needed to apply I.4.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:9},sasMidpoint:{title:`I.4 · equal bases of the two smaller triangles`,text:`ΑΓ = ΒΓ, ΓΔ is common, and ∠ΑΓΔ = ∠ΒΓΔ. I.4 therefore gives the equal bases ΑΔ and ΒΔ. The midpoint is a consequence of these constructions and equalities, not a coordinate assigned in advance.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:4}},r={9:{id:9,numeral:`I.9`,greekNumeral:`θʹ`,title:`Construct an angle’s equal halves`,shortTitle:`Bisect an angle`,introduction:`Choose equal lengths on the two rays and construct an equilateral triangle between them. Three side pairs then prove the equality of the two angle parts. The choice of circle intersection matters.`,statement:{grc:`Τὴν δοθεῖσαν γωνίαν εὐθύγραμμον δίχα τεμεῖν.`,lat:`Datum angulum rectilineum in duas partes aequales diuidere.`,en:`Divide a given rectilinear angle into two equal parts.`,greekPages:[28],latinPages:[29]},steps:[{title:`Start with the angle to be divided`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω ἡ δοθεῖσα γωνία εὐθύγραμμος ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΑΓ. δεῖ δὴ αὐτὴν δίχα τεμεῖν.`,lat:`Sit datus angulus rectilineus ΒΑΓ: oportet igitur eum in duas partes aequales diuidere.`,en:`Let ΒΑΓ be the given rectilinear angle. It is to be divided into two equal parts.`,greekPages:[28],latinPages:[29],language:`δίχα means ‘in two’; the construction requires equal halves, made explicit by Latin in duas partes aequales. τεμεῖν and diuidere are infinitives following the impersonal δεῖ and oportet: ‘it is necessary to divide’.`},{title:`Obtain equal lengths on the two rays`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`i3ForAngle`,grc:`Εἰλήφθω ἐπὶ τῆς ΑΒ τυχὸν σημεῖον τὸ Δ, καὶ ἀφῃρήσθω ἀπὸ τῆς ΑΓ τῇ ΑΔ ἴση ἡ ΑΕ,`,lat:`sumatur in ΑΒ quoduis punctum Δ, et ab ΑΓ rectae ΑΔ aequalis abscindatur ΑΕ,`,en:`Choose a point Δ on ΑΒ, and cut ΑΕ from ΑΓ equal to ΑΔ.`,greekPages:[28],latinPages:[29],language:`τυχὸν / quoduis allows a chosen point. The practical plate keeps it closer to Α than either displayed ray endpoint, so I.3 can cut the required part. ἀπὸ τῆς ΑΓ identifies the segment being cut; τῇ ΑΔ identifies the length to match.`},{title:`Join the two obtained points`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p1`,grc:`καὶ ἐπεζεύχθω ἡ ΔΕ,`,lat:`et ducatur ΔΕ,`,en:`Join Δ and Ε.`,greekPages:[28],latinPages:[29],language:`The repeated καί / et joins consecutive operations. ΔΕ becomes the base on which the next operation invokes I.1.`},{title:`Construct the equilateral triangle`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`i1ForAngle`,grc:`καὶ συνεστάτω ἐπὶ τῆς ΔΕ τρίγωνον ἰσόπλευρον τὸ ΔΕΖ,`,lat:`et in ΔΕ construatur triangulus aequilaterus ΔΕΖ,`,en:`Construct the equilateral triangle ΔΕΖ on ΔΕ.`,greekPages:[28],latinPages:[29],language:`ἐπὶ τῆς ΔΕ / in ΔΕ means ‘on ΔΕ’ as a base. The words name the equilateral construction; the printed figure supplies the intended side for Ζ. The plate states that choice explicitly rather than silently treating the two crossings as interchangeable.`},{title:`Join the vertex to Ζ and state the claim`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p1`,grc:`καὶ ἐπεζεύχθω ἡ ΑΖ· λέγω, ὅτι ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΑΓ γωνία δίχα τέτμηται ὑπὸ τῆς ΑΖ εὐθείας.`,lat:`et ducatur ΑΖ. dico, angulum ΒΑΓ recta ΑΖ in duas partes aequales diuisum esse.`,en:`Join Α to Ζ. I claim that ΑΖ has divided angle ΒΑΓ into equal halves.`,greekPages:[28],latinPages:[29],language:`τέτμηται and diuisum esse present the proposed completed result. Because they follow λέγω / dico, they announce what the following proof must establish; drawing the line has not yet proved the equality.`},{title:`Identify two corresponding side pairs`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`commonBisector`,grc:`Ἐπεὶ γὰρ ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΑΔ τῇ ΑΕ, κοινὴ δὲ ἡ ΑΖ, δύο δὴ αἱ ΔΑ, ΑΖ δυσὶ ταῖς ΕΑ, ΑΖ ἴσαι εἰσὶν ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ.`,lat:`nam cum ΑΔ = ΑΕ, et ΑΖ communis sit, duae rectae ΔΑ, ΑΖ duabus ΕΑ, ΑΖ aequales sunt altera alteri;`,en:`ΑΔ equals ΑΕ and ΑΖ is common. Thus ΔΑ and ΑΖ equal ΕΑ and ΑΖ respectively.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31],language:`ΔΑ names the same segment as ΑΔ. The reversal follows the order of the compared triangles, ΔΑΖ and ΕΑΖ. κοινή / communis identifies a single side used in both triangles, not two separately constructed equal lines.`},{title:`Supply the third side pair`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`equilateralBase`,grc:`καὶ βάσις ἡ ΔΖ βάσει τῇ ΕΖ ἴση ἐστίν·`,lat:`et basis ΔΖ basi ΕΖ aequalis est.`,en:`The base ΔΖ also equals the base ΕΖ.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31],language:`βάσις / basis now names the side opposite the angle being compared in each smaller triangle. ‘Base’ is a role in the argument, not necessarily the horizontal line in the picture.`},{title:`Use I.8 to obtain the equal angle parts`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`i8`,grc:`γωνία ἄρα ἡ ὑπὸ ΔΑΖ γωνίᾳ τῇ ὑπὸ ΕΑΖ ἴση ἐστίν.`,lat:`itaque ∠ ΔΑΖ = ΕΑΖ.`,en:`Therefore angle ΔΑΖ equals angle ΕΑΖ.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31],language:`Both angle names have Α in the middle. The outer letters change from Δ to Ε while Ζ remains common, matching the two sides of the dividing ray. ἄρα / itaque signals the consequence of the three established side pairs.`},{title:`The angle has been bisected`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`finish`,grc:`Ἡ ἄρα δοθεῖσα γωνία εὐθύγραμμος ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΑΓ δίχα τέτμηται ὑπὸ τῆς ΑΖ εὐθείας· ὅπερ ἔδει ποιῆσαι.`,lat:`Ergo datus angulus rectilineus ΒΑΓ recta ΑΖ in duas partes aequales diuisus est; quod oportebat fieri.`,en:`The given rectilinear angle ΒΑΓ has therefore been divided into equal halves by ΑΖ. The requested construction is complete.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31],language:`The given object returns with δοθεῖσα / datus, and the completed division with τέτμηται / diuisus est. ποιῆσαι / fieri closes a construction problem; compare δεῖξαι / demonstrandum in the preceding theorems.`}],insight:{title:`Equal angles still need the right position`,text:`I.1 gives two circle intersections. The one across ΔΕ from Α always gives an internal dividing ray. For the other crossing, an angle below 60° still gives an internal ray; at 60° the crossing is Α itself; above 60° it gives the opposite ray. In that last case the two measured angles are equal, but they are not the two parts of the original angle. The printed figure encodes a position choice that the short command does not spell out.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI9.html`},question:{prompt:`At a 90° given angle, what goes wrong with the other circle crossing?`,options:[`The ray goes outside the given angle, so equal angles alone do not show its bisection`,`ΔΖ and ΕΖ stop being equal`,`I.8 needs a measured 45° angle as a premise`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. That crossing produces the opposite ray: the two measured angles are 135°, not the two 45° parts inside the given angle.`,`Both circle crossings are equidistant from Δ and Ε. The failure is their position relative to the given angle.`,`I.8 uses three side equalities and proves the angle equality. No 45° premise or protractor is used.`]}},10:{id:10,numeral:`I.10`,greekNumeral:`ιʹ`,title:`Earn a segment’s midpoint`,shortTitle:`Bisect a segment`,introduction:`An equilateral triangle and the angle bisector now divide a segment. The midpoint is found by intersecting constructed lines, then proved by comparing two triangles.`,statement:{grc:`Τὴν δοθεῖσαν εὐθεῖαν πεπερασμένην δίχα τεμεῖν.`,lat:`Datam rectam terminatam in duas partes aequales diuidere.`,en:`Divide a given finite straight line into two equal parts.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31]},steps:[{title:`Take the finite segment to be divided`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω ἡ δοθεῖσα εὐθεῖα πεπερασμένη ἡ ΑΒ· δεῖ δὴ τὴν ΑΒ εὐθεῖαν πεπερασμένην δίχα τεμεῖν.`,lat:`Sit data recta terminata ΑΒ. oportet igitur rectam terminatam ΑΒ in duas partes aequales diuidere.`,en:`Let ΑΒ be the given finite straight line. It is to be divided into two equal parts.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31],language:`πεπερασμένη / terminata identifies a bounded segment. A midpoint belongs to that finite length; it is not the middle of an infinite supporting line. Compare postulate 2, which starts from a finite line and extends it.`},{title:`Construct the equilateral triangle on it`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`i1ForMidpoint`,grc:`Συνεστάτω ἐπ’ αὐτῆς τρίγωνον ἰσόπλευρον τὸ ΑΒΓ,`,lat:`construatur in ea triangulus aequilaterus ΑΒΓ,`,en:`Construct the equilateral triangle ΑΒΓ on ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31],language:`αὐτῆς / ea refers back to the given segment. The new construction does not introduce another arbitrary base. I.1 has already established how to make this triangle.`},{title:`Bisect the angle at Γ`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`i9`,grc:`καὶ τετμήσθω ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΓΒ γωνία δίχα τῇ ΓΔ εὐθείᾳ·`,lat:`et angulus ΑΓΒ recta ΓΔ in duas partes aequales diuidatur.`,en:`Bisect angle ΑΓΒ with the straight line ΓΔ.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31],language:`τετμήσθω is the requested division: ‘let it have been cut’. The dative τῇ ΓΔ εὐθείᾳ names the dividing line; Latin recta ΓΔ is ablative. Δ lies where that line meets the given base in the figure.`},{title:`State the proposed midpoint`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`given`,grc:`λέγω, ὅτι ἡ ΑΒ εὐθεῖα δίχα τέτμηται κατὰ τὸ Δ σημεῖον.`,lat:`dico, rectam ΑΒ in puncto Δ in duas partes aequales diuisam esse.`,en:`I claim that Δ divides ΑΒ into two equal parts.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31],language:`κατὰ τὸ Δ σημεῖον / in puncto Δ locates the division at a point. The source distinguishes this claim from the construction of ΓΔ: the equality of ΑΔ and ΒΔ remains to be proved.`},{title:`Identify the equal sides around Γ`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`midpointPairs`,grc:`Ἐπεὶ γὰρ ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΑΓ τῇ ΓΒ, κοινὴ δὲ ἡ ΓΔ, δύο δὴ αἱ ΑΓ, ΓΔ δύο ταῖς ΒΓ, ΓΔ ἴσαι εἰσὶν ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ·`,lat:`nam cum ΑΓ = ΓΒ, et ΓΔ communis sit, duae rectae ΑΓ, ΓΔ duabus ΒΓ, ΓΔ aequales sunt altera alteri;`,en:`ΑΓ equals ΓΒ, and ΓΔ is common. Thus ΑΓ and ΓΔ equal ΒΓ and ΓΔ respectively.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31],language:`ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ / altera alteri again means ‘each to each’, in the stated order. One pair comes from the equilateral triangle; the second is the same segment ΓΔ used twice.`},{title:`Use the angle equality just constructed`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`bisectedAngle`,grc:`καὶ γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΓΔ γωνίᾳ τῇ ὑπὸ ΒΓΔ ἴση ἐστίν·`,lat:`et ∠ ΑΓΔ = ΒΓΔ.`,en:`And the angles ΑΓΔ and ΒΓΔ are equal.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31],language:`Γ is the middle letter of both angle names, identifying the common vertex. These are the two parts of the angle bisected by I.9, not the angles at the proposed midpoint Δ.`},{title:`Conclude that the two base parts are equal`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`sasMidpoint`,grc:`βάσις ἄρα ἡ ΑΔ βάσει τῇ ΒΔ ἴση ἐστίν.`,lat:`quare ΑΔ = ΒΔ.`,en:`Therefore base ΑΔ equals base ΒΔ.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31],language:`ἄρα / quare draws the conclusion from I.4’s two sides and included angle. ΑΔ and ΒΔ are called bases of the smaller compared triangles; they together make the original given segment.`},{title:`The finite line has been bisected`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`finish`,grc:`Ἡ ἄρα δοθεῖσα εὐθεῖα πεπερασμένη ἡ ΑΒ δίχα τέτμηται κατὰ τὸ Δ· ὅπερ ἔδει ποιῆσαι.`,lat:`Ergo data recta terminata ΑΒ in puncto Δ in duas partes aequales diuisa est; quod oportebat fieri.`,en:`The given finite line ΑΒ has therefore been divided into equal halves at Δ. The requested construction is complete.`,greekPages:[30],latinPages:[31],language:`δίχα τέτμηται / in duas partes aequales diuisa est repeats the task as a completed result. The same language concluded the division of an angle in I.9; the object divided is now a segment.`}],insight:{title:`Two circles do more work than they first seem to`,text:`The two circles used for I.1 have a second intersection. When I.9 is applied at Γ, the already equal segments ΓΑ and ΓΒ let Α and Β serve as its two chosen points. The second crossing completes the needed equilateral triangle without drawing new circles. Joining the two crossings gives the line whose intersection with ΑΒ is Δ. The auxiliary label Ζ₉ identifies the point’s role in I.9; it is not a label added to the source text of I.10.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI10.html`},question:{prompt:`Why is Δ the midpoint, beyond where it appears in the drawing?`,options:[`Every line from an equilateral triangle’s vertex bisects its base`,`I.4 makes ΑΔ and ΒΔ equal from the constructed sides and included angles`,`The coordinate system defines Δ as the average of Α and Β`],correct:1,explanations:[`An arbitrary line from Γ need not meet the base at its midpoint. The angle bisection is essential.`,`Yes. The two equilateral sides, common ΓΔ and equal half-angles supply I.4’s hypotheses, which give the equal base parts.`,`The model intersects a constructed bisector with ΑΒ. Agreement with the arithmetic midpoint is an independent check, not how Δ is assigned.`]}}},i={inequalityExtend:{title:`Postulate 2 · continue the side`,text:`Continue the specified side along its own straight line. This supplies an exterior angle; it does not assume the later theorem about the sum of a triangle’s angles.`,kind:`construction`},inequalityExterior:{title:`I.16 · an exterior angle exceeds the opposite interior angle`,text:`Apply the already proved exterior-angle inequality to the named triangle. Compare the interior angle away from the exterior vertex, not the adjacent angle.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:16},inequalityAdd:{title:`Add the same angle to unequal angles`,text:`If one magnitude exceeds another, adding the same magnitude to both preserves the inequality. This order rule is used here without a separate proof. It is not one of the usual five common notions; Heiberg’s expanded list includes a related unequal-addition formulation.`,kind:`assumption`},inequalityStraight:{title:`I.13 · the adjacent pair equals two right angles`,text:`ΑΓ stands on the straight line ΒΔ. The adjacent exterior and interior angles at Γ total two right angles. Replace that total in the inequality just established; no three-angle sum is needed.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:13},inequalityRepeat:{title:`Repeat the argument with the other pair`,text:`The source says similarly. The selector expands the two remaining cases by reassigning the roles of Α, Β and Γ. Δ is always the new extension point. These extra drawings are modern teaching expansions, not additional source diagrams.`,kind:`deduction`},inequalityCut:{title:`I.3 · cut off ΑΔ equal to ΑΒ`,text:`The hypothesis ΑΓ > ΑΒ ensures that I.3 can place Δ strictly inside ΑΓ. Join ΒΔ by postulate 1. The computed cut uses the earlier copying construction, rather than treating the given inequality as a measured guess.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:3},inequalityIsosceles:{title:`I.5 · equal sides give equal base angles`,text:`Identify the two equal sides of the small triangle. Their opposite angles are equal by I.5; these are the angles subsequently compared with a larger angle.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:5},inequalityTransfer:{title:`Substitute the equal angle`,text:`ΑΔΒ exceeds ΑΓΒ, while ΑΔΒ equals ΑΒΔ. Substitution therefore gives ΑΒΔ > ΑΓΒ.`,kind:`deduction`},inequalityWhole:{title:`Common notion 5 · the whole exceeds its part`,text:`The ray through the interior point Δ divides ΑΒΓ into two positive angles. ΑΒΓ exceeds its proper part ΑΒΔ, which already exceeds ΑΓΒ. The word πολλῷ strengthens the comparison without assigning a numerical difference.`,kind:`deduction`},inequalityCases:{title:`Exclude the other two possible orders`,text:`For two lengths exactly one of greater, equal or less holds. If ΑΓ were not greater than ΑΒ, it would be equal or less. Each alternative must be tested against the GIVEN angle inequality; a converse is not proved merely by reversing a sentence.`,kind:`assumption`},inequalityEqualCase:{title:`I.5 · the equal-side alternative contradicts the given angles`,text:`If ΑΓ = ΑΒ, I.5 forces ΑΒΓ = ΑΓΒ. That conflicts with the given ΑΒΓ > ΑΓΒ. The plate temporarily draws the equal-side case and explicitly withdraws the original angle premise from that altered drawing.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:5},inequalityLessCase:{title:`I.18 · the shorter-side alternative reverses the given order`,text:`If ΑΓ < ΑΒ, apply I.18 to the longer side ΑΒ: its opposite angle ΑΓΒ exceeds ΑΒΓ. This again contradicts the given order. The altered triangle illustrates the consequence of this supposition, not a triangle satisfying every premise.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:18},inequalityEliminate:{title:`Only the greater-side case remains`,text:`Equality and the shorter-side alternative have both contradicted the given angle order. The remaining possibility is ΑΓ > ΑΒ. The plate returns to the original given triangle.`,kind:`deduction`},inequalityCopy:{title:`Copy ΓΑ onto the continuation at Α, then join ΔΓ`,text:`Euclid asks for ΑΔ = ΓΑ on the continuation of ΒΑ. Here I.2 supplies a length at Α, and a circle at Α cuts that ray at Δ. Join ΔΓ by postulate 1. The auxiliary I.2 work is collapsed; the earlier lesson exposes it.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:2},inequalityAnglePart:{title:`Common notion 5 · ΒΓΔ contains ΑΓΔ`,text:`Ray ΓΑ lies strictly inside angle ΒΓΔ. Thus ΒΓΔ exceeds ΑΓΔ, which equals ΑΔΓ by I.5. Since Β, Α and Δ are in that order on a line, rays ΔΑ and ΔΒ coincide: ΑΔΓ is also ΒΔΓ.`,kind:`deduction`},inequalityLonger:{title:`I.19 · use the angle order in triangle ΔΓΒ`,text:`In ΔΓΒ, angle ΒΓΔ exceeds ΒΔΓ. The side opposite the greater angle, ΔΒ, therefore exceeds ΒΓ. This is why I.19 was needed before the triangle inequality.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:19},inequalitySum:{title:`Replace the copied segment in the whole length`,text:`Α lies between Β and Δ, so ΔΒ = ΒΑ + ΑΔ. Replace ΑΔ by its constructed equal ΑΓ. Since ΔΒ > ΒΓ, we obtain ΒΑ + ΑΓ > ΒΓ. Heiberg spells out the segment addition in a Latin footnote; it is explained here rather than inserted into Euclid’s text.`,kind:`deduction`}},a={17:{id:17,numeral:`I.17`,greekNumeral:`ιζʹ`,title:`Keep any two angles below two right angles`,shortTitle:`Two-angle bound`,introduction:`Use an exterior angle and a straight pair to bound two interior angles. The later theorem about all three angles is not needed.`,statement:{grc:`Παντὸς τριγώνου αἱ δύο γωνίαι δύο ὀρθῶν ἐλάσσονές εἰσι πάντῃ μεταλαμβανόμεναι.`,lat:`Cuiusuis trianguli duo anguli duobus rectis minores sunt quoquo modo coniuncti.`,en:`In any triangle, any two angles taken together are less than two right angles.`,greekPages:[44],latinPages:[45]},steps:[{title:`Choose any triangle`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω τρίγωνον τὸ ΑΒΓ· λέγω, ὅτι τοῦ ΑΒΓ τριγώνου αἱ δύο γωνίαι δύο ὀρθῶν ἐλάττονές εἰσι πάντῃ μεταλαμβανόμεναι.`,lat:`Sit triangulus ΑΒΓ. dico, angulos duos trianguli ΑΒΓ duobus rectis minores esse quoquo modo coniunctos.`,en:`Let ΑΒΓ be a triangle. I say that any two of its angles, taken together, are less than two right angles.`,greekPages:[44],latinPages:[45],language:`αἱ δύο γωνίαι is the subject. δύο ὀρθῶν is genitive after the comparative ἐλάσσονες: less than two right angles. Latin expresses the comparison with the ablative duobus rectis.`},{title:`Extend ΒΓ to Δ`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`inequalityExtend`,grc:`Ἐκβεβλήσθω γὰρ ἡ ΒΓ ἐπὶ τὸ Δ.`,lat:`producatur enim ΒΓ ad Δ.`,en:`For let ΒΓ be extended to Δ.`,greekPages:[44],latinPages:[45],language:`Ἐκβεβλήσθω commands the completed extension. Latin producatur is a passive subjunctive with instructional force; ad Δ gives its destination.`},{title:`Compare exterior and opposite interior`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityExterior`,grc:`Καὶ ἐπεὶ τριγώνου τοῦ ΑΒΓ ἐκτός ἐστι γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΓΔ, μείζων ἐστὶ τῆς ἐντὸς καὶ ἀπεναντίον τῆς ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ.`,lat:`et quoniam in triangulo ΑΒΓ extrinsecus positus est angulus ΑΓΔ, maior est angulo interiore et opposito ΑΒΓ.`,en:`Since ΑΓΔ is an exterior angle of triangle ΑΒΓ, it is greater than the interior and opposite angle ΑΒΓ.`,greekPages:[44],latinPages:[45],language:`ἐντὸς καὶ ἀπεναντίον and interiore et opposito describe the same angle. Both qualifications matter: the adjacent interior angle is not the one I.16 compares.`},{title:`Add the angle at Γ to both`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityAdd`,grc:`κοινὴ προσκείσθω ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΓΒ· αἱ ἄρα ὑπὸ ΑΓΔ, ΑΓΒ τῶν ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ, ΒΓΑ μείζονές εἰσιν.`,lat:`communis adiciatur ΑΓΒ. itaque ΑΓΔ + ΑΓΒ > ΑΒΓ + ΒΓΑ.`,en:`Let ΑΓΒ be added in common. Therefore ΑΓΔ and ΑΓΒ together are greater than ΑΒΓ and ΒΓΑ together.`,greekPages:[44],latinPages:[45],language:`κοινή / communis says that the same angle is added to both sides. ΒΓΑ and ΑΓΒ name the same angle with the endpoint order reversed; the vertex remains Γ.`},{title:`Replace the straight pair`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityStraight`,grc:`ἀλλ᾽ αἱ ὑπὸ ΑΓΔ, ΑΓΒ δύο ὀρθαῖς ἴσαι εἰσίν· αἱ ἄρα ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ, ΒΓΑ δύο ὀρθῶν ἐλάσσονές εἰσιν.`,lat:`uerum ΑΓΔ + ΑΓΒ duobus rectis aequales sunt. itaque ΑΒΓ + ΒΓΑ duobus rectis minores sunt.`,en:`But ΑΓΔ and ΑΓΒ together equal two right angles. Therefore ΑΒΓ and ΒΓΑ together are less than two right angles.`,greekPages:[44],latinPages:[45],language:`ὀρθαῖς is dative with ἴσαι; ὀρθῶν is genitive with ἐλάσσονες. The reference quantity stays the same while its grammatical role changes.`},{title:`Unpack “similarly” for the other pairs`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityRepeat`,grc:`ὁμοίως δὴ δείξομεν, ὅτι καὶ αἱ ὑπὸ ΒΑΓ, ΑΓΒ δύο ὀρθῶν ἐλάσσονές εἰσι καὶ ἔτι αἱ ὑπὸ ΓΑΒ, ΑΒΓ.`,lat:`similiter demonstrabimus, etiam ΒΑΓ + ΑΓΒ et praeterea ΓΑΒ + ΑΒΓ duobus rectis minores esse.`,en:`Similarly we shall show that ΒΑΓ and ΑΓΒ together, and also ΓΑΒ and ΑΒΓ together, are less than two right angles.`,greekPages:[44],latinPages:[45],language:`δείξομεν and demonstrabimus are future: we shall show. The short sentence delegates two repetitions. Choose each pair on the plate to make those repetitions visible.`},{title:`Conclude the two-angle bound`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`inequalityRepeat`,grc:`Παντὸς ἄρα τριγώνου αἱ δύο γωνίαι δύο ὀρθῶν ἐλάσσονές εἰσι πάντῃ μεταλαμβανόμεναι· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo cuiusuis trianguli duo anguli duobus rectis minores sunt quoquo modo coniuncti; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Therefore, in any triangle, any two angles taken together are less than two right angles. This is what had to be shown.`,greekPages:[44],latinPages:[45],language:`πάντῃ μεταλαμβανόμεναι / quoquo modo coniuncti means that every choice of two is covered. The conclusion is a strict bound for two angles, not yet an equality for all three.`}],insight:{title:`A bound earned without the angle-sum theorem`,text:`I.16 supplies the strict comparison; adding a common angle preserves it; I.13 identifies the larger total as two right angles. The degree readout is a modern check of the drawing. It does not serve as a premise or replace these three steps.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI17.html`},question:{prompt:`Which result turns the larger pair into two right angles?`,options:[`I.13, because the adjacent pair stands on a straight line`,`The later sum of all three interior angles`,`The numerical readout alone`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. I.13 gives the straight-pair total, after I.16 establishes the inequality.`,`That later theorem is not used here.`,`Measurements check this sketch; the argument needs the earlier proposition.`]}},18:{id:18,numeral:`I.18`,greekNumeral:`ιηʹ`,title:`Find the greater angle from the greater side`,shortTitle:`Side to angle`,introduction:`Cut the longer side to build a small isosceles triangle. An exterior angle then carries a strict inequality into the original triangle.`,statement:{grc:`Παντὸς τριγώνου ἡ μείζων πλευρὰ τὴν μείζονα γωνίαν ὑποτείνει.`,lat:`In quouis triangulo maius latus sub maiore angulo subtendit.`,en:`In any triangle, the greater side lies opposite the greater angle.`,greekPages:[46],latinPages:[47]},steps:[{title:`Start with ΑΓ greater than ΑΒ`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω γὰρ τρίγωνον τὸ ΑΒΓ μείζονα ἔχον τὴν ΑΓ πλευρὰν τῆς ΑΒ· λέγω, ὅτι καὶ γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ μείζων ἐστὶ τῆς ὑπὸ ΒΓΑ.`,lat:`Sit enim triangulus ΑΒΓ habens ΑΓ > ΑΒ. dico, etiam esse ∠ ΑΒΓ > ΒΓΑ.`,en:`Let ΑΒΓ be a triangle whose side ΑΓ is greater than ΑΒ. I say that angle ΑΒΓ is also greater than ΒΓΑ.`,greekPages:[46],latinPages:[47],language:`μείζονα ἔχον / habens introduces the given side order. The angle order after λέγω / dico is the claim still to be proved.`},{title:`Cut ΑΔ equal to ΑΒ and join ΒΔ`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`inequalityCut`,grc:`Ἐπεὶ γὰρ μείζων ἐστὶν ἡ ΑΓ τῆς ΑΒ, κείσθω τῇ ΑΒ ἴση ἡ ΑΔ, καὶ ἐπεζεύχθω ἡ ΒΔ.`,lat:`nam quoniam ΑΓ > ΑΒ, ponatur ΑΔ = ΑΒ, et ducatur ΒΔ.`,en:`Since ΑΓ is greater than ΑΒ, let ΑΔ be laid off equal to ΑΒ, and let ΒΔ be joined.`,greekPages:[46],latinPages:[47],language:`κείσθω / ponatur is a command to place, not an assertion about an arbitrary point. The prior inequality puts Δ inside ΑΓ. Equal or shorter ΑΓ would not support this construction.`},{title:`Use the exterior angle at Δ`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityExterior`,grc:`Καὶ ἐπεὶ τριγώνου τοῦ ΒΓΔ ἐκτός ἐστι γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΔΒ, μείζων ἐστὶ τῆς ἐντὸς καὶ ἀπεναντίον τῆς ὑπὸ ΔΓΒ·`,lat:`et quoniam in triangulo ΒΓΔ extrinsecus positus est ∠ ΑΔΒ, erit ∠ ΑΔΒ > ΔΓΒ, qui interior est et oppositus.`,en:`Since ΑΔΒ is an exterior angle of triangle ΒΓΔ, it is greater than the interior and opposite angle ΔΓΒ.`,greekPages:[46],latinPages:[47],language:`The triangle is now ΒΓΔ, not the original ΑΒΓ. Track its name: ΑΔ extends ΓΔ, making ΑΔΒ exterior to this smaller triangle.`},{title:`Identify the equal base angles`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityIsosceles`,grc:`ἴση δὲ ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΔΒ τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΒΔ, ἐπεὶ καὶ πλευρὰ ἡ ΑΒ τῇ ΑΔ ἐστιν ἴση·`,lat:`sed ∠ ΑΔΒ = ΑΒΔ, quoniam etiam ΑΒ = ΑΔ.`,en:`But ΑΔΒ equals ΑΒΔ, since side ΑΒ also equals ΑΔ.`,greekPages:[46],latinPages:[47],language:`ἐπεί / quoniam gives the reason for the angle equality: the two sides were made equal. The proof changes its focus to isosceles triangle ΑΒΔ.`},{title:`Transfer the strict comparison`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityTransfer`,grc:`μείζων ἄρα καὶ ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΒΔ τῆς ὑπὸ ΑΓΒ·`,lat:`itaque etiam ∠ ΑΒΔ > ΑΓΒ.`,en:`Therefore ΑΒΔ too is greater than ΑΓΒ.`,greekPages:[46],latinPages:[47],language:`ἄρα / itaque marks a consequence. ΔΓΒ has become ΑΓΒ because ΓΔ and ΓΑ are the same ray; the vertex Γ has not moved.`},{title:`Pass from the part to the whole angle`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityWhole`,grc:`πολλῷ ἄρα ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ μείζων ἐστὶ τῆς ὑπὸ ΑΓΒ.`,lat:`quare multo magis ∠ ΑΒΓ > ΑΓΒ.`,en:`All the more, therefore, ΑΒΓ is greater than ΑΓΒ.`,greekPages:[46],latinPages:[47],language:`πολλῷ / multo is a dative or ablative of degree of difference. Here ‘all the more’ stresses that the whole exceeds a part already greater than the comparison angle; no numerical multiplier is specified.`},{title:`Conclude from side order to angle order`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`inequalityWhole`,grc:`Παντὸς ἄρα τριγώνου ἡ μείζων πλευρὰ τὴν μείζονα γωνίαν ὑποτείνει· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo in quouis triangulo maius latus sub maiore angulo subtendit; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Therefore, in any triangle, the greater side lies opposite the greater angle. This is what had to be shown.`,greekPages:[46],latinPages:[47],language:`ἡ μείζων πλευρά is nominative, the subject of ὑποτείνει. τὴν μείζονα γωνίαν is accusative. Compare the next proposition, which begins with the angle as the given information.`}],insight:{title:`The greater side creates its own witness`,text:`The cut point Δ supplies both an isosceles triangle and an exterior angle. If ΑΓ equals ΑΒ, Δ would coincide with Γ and triangle ΒΓΔ would collapse. If ΑΓ is shorter, no such interior cut is possible. Move the side control through equality to see exactly where this proof stops applying.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI18.html`},question:{prompt:`Why is ΑΓ > ΑΒ required before choosing Δ?`,options:[`It puts Δ strictly inside ΑΓ, so ΒΓΔ is a triangle`,`It means every angle is acute`,`It lets us assume the desired angle inequality`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. The internal cut supports both the exterior-angle step and the proper-part comparison.`,`The source places no acute-angle restriction on the triangle.`,`The side order is given; the angle order must still be proved.`]}},19:{id:19,numeral:`I.19`,greekNumeral:`ιθʹ`,title:`Recover the greater side from the greater angle`,shortTitle:`Angle to side`,introduction:`Prove the converse by excluding equality and the reverse order. Watch what each temporary supposition does to the given angles.`,statement:{grc:`Παντὸς τριγώνου ὑπὸ τὴν μείζονα γωνίαν ἡ μείζων πλευρὰ ὑποτείνει.`,lat:`In quouis triangulo sub maiore angulo maius latus subtendit.`,en:`In any triangle, the side opposite the greater angle is greater.`,greekPages:[46],latinPages:[47]},steps:[{title:`Begin with the angle inequality`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω τρίγωνον τὸ ΑΒΓ μείζονα ἔχον τὴν ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ γωνίαν τῆς ὑπὸ ΒΓΑ· λέγω, ὅτι καὶ πλευρὰ ἡ ΑΓ πλευρᾶς τῆς ΑΒ μείζων ἐστίν.`,lat:`Sit triangulus ΑΒΓ habens ∠ ΑΒΓ > ΒΓΑ. dico, etiam esse ΑΓ > ΑΒ.`,en:`Let ΑΒΓ be a triangle with angle ΑΒΓ greater than ΒΓΑ. I say that side ΑΓ is also greater than ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[46],latinPages:[47],language:`The given and the goal have exchanged roles since I.18. μείζονα ἔχον now introduces an angle inequality. The shape is a numerical realization of that given, not a proof of the converse.`},{title:`State the two alternatives`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityCases`,grc:`Εἰ γὰρ μή, ἤτοι ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΑΓ τῇ ΑΒ ἢ ἐλάσσων·`,lat:`nam si minus, aut ΑΓ = ΑΒ aut ΑΓ < ΑΒ.`,en:`For if not, ΑΓ is either equal to ΑΒ or less than it.`,greekPages:[46],latinPages:[47],language:`Εἰ γὰρ μή / nam si minus means ‘for if not’ here. Latin minus does not itself assert the shorter-side case: the following aut … aut gives both alternatives.`},{title:`Reject equal sides`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityEqualCase`,grc:`ἴση μὲν οὖν οὐκ ἔστιν ἡ ΑΓ τῇ ΑΒ· ἴση γὰρ ἂν ἦν καὶ γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΓΒ· οὐκ ἔστι δέ· οὐκ ἄρα ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΑΓ τῇ ΑΒ.`,lat:`iam non est ΑΓ = ΑΒ. tum enim esset ∠ ΑΒΓ = ΑΓΒ; uerum non est. itaque non est ΑΓ = ΑΒ.`,en:`ΑΓ is not equal to ΑΒ: then angle ΑΒΓ would also equal ΑΓΒ. But it does not. Therefore ΑΓ is not equal to ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[46],latinPages:[47],language:`ἂν ἦν and esset express what would follow under the supposition. The equal angles belong to the hypothetical case; they are not new facts about the original given triangle.`},{title:`Reject the shorter side`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityLessCase`,grc:`οὐδὲ μὴν ἐλάσσων ἐστὶν ἡ ΑΓ τῆς ΑΒ· ἐλάσσων γὰρ ἂν ἦν καὶ γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ τῆς ὑπὸ ΑΓΒ· οὐκ ἔστι δέ· οὐκ ἄρα ἐλάσσων ἐστὶν ἡ ΑΓ τῆς ΑΒ.`,lat:`neque uero ΑΓ < ΑΒ. tum enim esset ∠ ΑΒΓ < ΑΓΒ. uerum non est. itaque non est ΑΓ < ΑΒ.`,en:`Nor is ΑΓ less than ΑΒ: then angle ΑΒΓ would also be less than ΑΓΒ. But it is not. Therefore ΑΓ is not less than ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[46,48],latinPages:[47,49],language:`οὐδὲ μήν / neque uero introduces the second rejection. I.18 is applied with the side roles reversed. The repeated οὐκ ἔστι δέ / uerum non est points back to the given angle order.`},{title:`Keep the only remaining order`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityEliminate`,grc:`ἐδείχθη δέ, ὅτι οὐδὲ ἴση ἐστίν. μείζων ἄρα ἐστὶν ἡ ΑΓ τῆς ΑΒ.`,lat:`demonstratum autem est, ne aequalem quidem esse. quare ΑΓ > ΑΒ.`,en:`It was also shown that ΑΓ is not equal to ΑΒ. Therefore ΑΓ is greater than ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[48],latinPages:[49],language:`ἐδείχθη / demonstratum est recalls an established rejection. ne … quidem reinforces ‘not even equal’. Both failed alternatives are needed before greater follows.`},{title:`Conclude the converse`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`inequalityEliminate`,grc:`Παντὸς ἄρα τριγώνου ὑπὸ τὴν μείζονα γωνίαν ἡ μείζων πλευρὰ ὑποτείνει· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo in quouis triangulo sub maiore angulo maius latus subtendit; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Therefore, in any triangle, the side opposite the greater angle is greater. This is what had to be shown.`,greekPages:[48],latinPages:[49],language:`ὑπὸ τὴν μείζονα γωνίαν comes before the subject ἡ μείζων πλευρά. The angle is the starting information. Latin likewise moves sub maiore angulo before maius latus.`}],insight:{title:`A converse needs an argument of its own`,text:`I.18 goes from sides to angles. I.19 goes back from angles to sides by testing every other side order. The changed sketches in steps 3 and 4 show why those alternatives cannot coexist with the original angle premise. The proof then returns to that original triangle.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI19.html`},question:{prompt:`What is true of the equal-side sketch in step 3?`,options:[`It also satisfies the original greater-angle premise`,`It illustrates the supposition’s consequence and loses the original angle premise`,`It disproves I.5`],correct:1,explanations:[`Equal sides force equal opposite angles, so the strict given inequality cannot remain.`,`Yes. That incompatibility is the contradiction; the plate labels it explicitly.`,`It uses I.5 to reject the supposition, not to reject I.5.`]}},20:{id:20,numeral:`I.20`,greekNumeral:`κʹ`,title:`Turn two sides into one longer straight length`,shortTitle:`Triangle inequality`,introduction:`Extend a side and copy the other onto it. The earlier angle–side comparison then proves the triangle inequality without assuming that a straight route is shortest.`,statement:{grc:`Παντὸς τριγώνου αἱ δύο πλευραὶ τῆς λοιπῆς μείζονές εἰσι πάντῃ μεταλαμβανόμεναι.`,lat:`In quouis triangulo duo latera reliquo maiora sunt quoquo modo coniuncta.`,en:`In any triangle, any two sides taken together are greater than the remaining side.`,greekPages:[48],latinPages:[49]},steps:[{title:`Name all three comparisons`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω γὰρ τρίγωνον τὸ ΑΒΓ· λέγω, ὅτι τοῦ ΑΒΓ τριγώνου αἱ δύο πλευραὶ τῆς λοιπῆς μείζονές εἰσι πάντῃ μεταλαμβανόμεναι, αἱ μὲν ΒΑ, ΑΓ τῆς ΒΓ, αἱ δὲ ΑΒ, ΒΓ τῆς ΑΓ, αἱ δὲ ΒΓ, ΓΑ τῆς ΑΒ.`,lat:`Sit enim triangulus ΑΒΓ. dico, in triangulo ΑΒΓ duo latera reliquo maiora esse quoquo modo coniuncta, ΒΑ + ΑΓ > ΒΓ, ΑΒ + ΒΓ > ΑΓ, ΒΓ + ΓΑ > ΑΒ.`,en:`Let ΑΒΓ be a triangle. I say that any two sides together exceed the remaining side: ΒΑ and ΑΓ exceed ΒΓ; ΑΒ and ΒΓ exceed ΑΓ; ΒΓ and ΓΑ exceed ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[48],latinPages:[49],language:`μὲν … δὲ … δέ distributes the claim over three pairs. Latin displays the three inequalities directly. These are the goals, not results proved at the opening step.`},{title:`Continue ΒΑ beyond Α`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`inequalityExtend`,grc:`Διήχθω γὰρ ἡ ΒΑ ἐπὶ τὸ Δ σημεῖον,`,lat:`educatur enim ΒΑ ad Δ punctum,`,en:`For let ΒΑ be continued to the point Δ,`,greekPages:[48],latinPages:[49],language:`The order ΒΑ matters: continue from Β through Α. The name Δ anticipates the point whose distance from Α the next command fixes. This plate first shows the continuation as a ray.`},{title:`Copy ΓΑ to ΑΔ, then join ΔΓ`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`inequalityCopy`,grc:`καὶ κείσθω τῇ ΓΑ ἴση ἡ ΑΔ, καὶ ἐπεζεύχθω ἡ ΔΓ.`,lat:`et ponatur ΑΔ = ΓΑ, et ducatur ΔΓ.`,en:`and let ΑΔ be laid off equal to ΓΑ, and let ΔΓ be joined.`,greekPages:[48],latinPages:[49],language:`κείσθω and ἐπεζεύχθω are separate commands, as are ponatur and ducatur. One establishes an equal length; the other joins the newly fixed point to Γ.`},{title:`Use the small isosceles triangle`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityIsosceles`,grc:`Ἐπεὶ οὖν ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΔΑ τῇ ΑΓ, ἴση ἐστὶ καὶ γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΔΓ τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΓΔ·`,lat:`iam quoniam ΔΑ = ΑΓ, erit etiam ∠ ΑΔΓ = ΑΓΔ.`,en:`Since ΔΑ equals ΑΓ, angle ΑΔΓ also equals ΑΓΔ.`,greekPages:[48],latinPages:[49],language:`The repeated ἴση links a side equality to an angle equality through I.5. ΔΑ and ΑΔ name the same segment; the reversed endpoint order does not change its length.`},{title:`Compare the whole angle with its part`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityAnglePart`,grc:`μείζων ἄρα ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΓΔ τῆς ὑπὸ ΑΔΓ.`,lat:`itaque ∠ ΒΓΔ > ΑΔΓ.`,en:`Therefore ΒΓΔ is greater than ΑΔΓ.`,greekPages:[48],latinPages:[49],language:`The short sentence compresses a substitution: ΒΓΔ contains ΑΓΔ, and ΑΓΔ equals ΑΔΓ. Follow both angle names before accepting ἄρα / itaque.`},{title:`Use I.19 in the larger triangle`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityLonger`,grc:`καὶ ἐπεὶ τρίγωνόν ἐστι τὸ ΔΓΒ μείζονα ἔχον τὴν ὑπὸ ΒΓΔ γωνίαν τῆς ὑπὸ ΒΔΓ, ὑπὸ δὲ τὴν μείζονα γωνίαν ἡ μείζων πλευρὰ ὑποτείνει, ἡ ΔΒ ἄρα τῆς ΒΓ ἐστι μείζων.`,lat:`et quoniam triangulus est ΔΓΒ maiorem habens angulum ΒΓΔ angulo ΒΔΓ, sub maiore autem angulo maius latus subtendit, erit ΔΒ > ΒΓ.`,en:`Since triangle ΔΓΒ has angle ΒΓΔ greater than ΒΔΓ, and the side opposite the greater angle is greater, ΔΒ is greater than ΒΓ.`,greekPages:[48],latinPages:[49],language:`ὑπὸ δὲ τὴν μείζονα γωνίαν … repeats I.19’s conclusion inside the new proof. Track the change from triangle ΔΑΓ to triangle ΔΓΒ.`},{title:`Replace ΔΑ with its equal ΑΓ`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalitySum`,grc:`ἴση δὲ ἡ ΔΑ τῇ ΑΓ· μείζονες ἄρα αἱ ΒΑ, ΑΓ τῆς ΒΓ·`,lat:`uerum ΔΑ = ΑΓ. itaque ΒΑ + ΑΓ > ΒΓ.`,en:`But ΔΑ equals ΑΓ. Therefore ΒΑ and ΑΓ together are greater than ΒΓ.`,greekPages:[48],latinPages:[49],language:`The plural αἱ ΒΑ, ΑΓ names the two sides taken together. Latin writes the addition explicitly. The whole ΔΒ contains ΒΑ plus ΑΔ because Α lies between its endpoints.`},{title:`Construct the other two comparisons`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`inequalityRepeat`,grc:`ὁμοίως δὴ δείξομεν, ὅτι καὶ αἱ μὲν ΑΒ, ΒΓ τῆς ΓΑ μείζονές εἰσιν, αἱ δὲ ΒΓ, ΓΑ τῆς ΑΒ.`,lat:`similiter demonstrabimus, esse etiam ΑΒ + ΒΓ > ΓΑ et ΒΓ + ΓΑ > ΑΒ.`,en:`Similarly we shall show that ΑΒ and ΒΓ together exceed ΓΑ, and that ΒΓ and ΓΑ together exceed ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[48],latinPages:[49],language:`ὁμοίως / similiter requests the same reasoning with different roles. The selector redraws the copied segment and the containing angle for each pair, while leaving the source sentence unchanged.`},{title:`Conclude the triangle inequality`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`inequalityRepeat`,grc:`Παντὸς ἄρα τριγώνου αἱ δύο πλευραὶ τῆς λοιπῆς μείζονές εἰσι πάντῃ μεταλαμβανόμεναι· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo in quouis triangulo duo latera reliquo maiora sunt quoquo modo coniuncta; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Therefore, in any triangle, any two sides taken together exceed the remaining side. This is what had to be shown.`,greekPages:[50],latinPages:[51],language:`τῆς λοιπῆς leaves πλευρᾶς understood: ‘than the remaining side’. Latin reliquo likewise leaves latere understood. πάντῃ / quoquo modo covers all three choices.`}],insight:{title:`Prove the shorter route instead of assuming it`,text:`A two-side route becomes the straight segment ΒΔ by copying ΑΓ beyond Α. The comparison ΔΒ > ΒΓ follows from angles in the larger triangle, using I.5 and I.19. The other two inequalities require the same construction at the other vertices; the selector draws them explicitly.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI20.html`},question:{prompt:`What justifies ΔΒ > ΒΓ before replacing ΔΑ by ΑΓ?`,options:[`Assuming that every direct route is shorter`,`Measuring the two lines on the screen`,`I.19 applied to the greater angle in triangle ΔΓΒ`],correct:2,explanations:[`That would assume the geometric comparison we are working to establish.`,`The readout checks this particular drawing, not every triangle.`,`Yes. I.5 and the proper-part relation supply the angle inequality; I.19 turns it into a side inequality.`]}}},o={rightPairCase:{title:`Definition 10 · first handle equal adjacent angles`,text:`If the two adjacent angles are equal, definition 10 already calls each right. The remaining argument concerns the unequal case. Selecting 90° follows this first branch rather than inventing a nonzero angle between coincident rays.`,kind:`definition`},raiseForPair:{title:`I.11 · construct a pair of right angles`,text:`Raise ΒΕ perpendicular to ΓΔ at Β using I.11. This provides a known pair of right angles to compare with the original pair. The plate uses the arrangement in the printed diagram, with ΒΑ between ΒΓ and ΒΕ.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:11},addFirstAngles:{title:`Common notion 2 · add the same angle to equals`,text:`Angle ΓΒΕ consists of ΓΒΑ and ΑΒΕ. Add ΕΒΔ to both descriptions. The two right angles now equal the three parts ΓΒΑ, ΑΒΕ and ΕΒΔ. Their containment is visible on this plate; it is not obtained by adding arbitrary angles that overlap.`,kind:`deduction`},addSecondAngles:{title:`Common notion 2 · compare the original pair with the same three parts`,text:`Angle ΔΒΑ consists of ΔΒΕ and ΕΒΑ. Add ΑΒΓ. This expresses the original pair as the same three parts, with reversed point names and a different ordering. The shared vertex remains Β.`,kind:`deduction`},equalAngleSums:{title:`Common notion 1 · two sums equal the same sum`,text:`Both the constructed right-angle pair and the original pair equal the same three angle parts. Therefore the two pairs are equal. ‘Equal to two right angles’ refers to their total; it does not make each original angle right.`,kind:`deduction`},converseSupposition:{title:`Suppose a different continuation exists`,text:`The proof supposes ΒΔ is not the continuation of ΓΒ, and names a different continuation ΒΕ. That supposition cannot be drawn together with every original premise. The printed-style sketch keeps the given angle pair and draws Ε separately; it does not actually make Γ, Β and Ε collinear. Its numerical table shows that distinction.`,kind:`assumption`},conditionalLinearPair:{title:`I.13 · the consequence if ΓΒΕ were one line`,text:`Under the temporary claim that Γ, Β and Ε are collinear, I.13 would make angles ΑΒΓ and ΑΒΕ equal to two right angles together. This is a conditional assertion in the proof, not a measured property of the hypothetical sketch, whose Ε is deliberately drawn off the line.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:13},compareGivenPair:{title:`The original premise and common notion 1`,text:`The original hypothesis gives ΑΒΓ + ΑΒΔ equal to two right angles. The temporary continuation would give ΑΒΓ + ΑΒΕ the same total. Common notion 1 therefore makes those pairs equal under the supposition.`,kind:`deduction`},removeCommonAngle:{title:`Common notion 3 · subtract the shared angle`,text:`Remove ΓΒΑ from both conditionally equal pairs. The remainders would be ΑΒΕ and ΑΒΔ. The diagram's measured remainders remain unequal; the proof is showing why the temporary claim cannot coexist with the given premise.`,kind:`deduction`},impossibleAnglePart:{title:`Common notion 5 · a smaller angle cannot equal the larger`,text:`In the printed arrangement ΑΒΕ is a proper part of ΑΒΔ. The supposed continuation forces them equal, contradicting the whole being greater than its part. The same reasoning excludes any different continuation; ΒΔ is the straight continuation of ΓΒ.`,kind:`deduction`}},s={13:{id:13,numeral:`I.13`,greekNumeral:`ιγʹ`,title:`Compare a linear pair with two right angles`,shortTitle:`A linear pair`,introduction:`Do not begin by assuming a straight angle measures 180°. Construct a perpendicular, split the surrounding angles into shared parts, and compare the two totals.`,statement:{grc:`Ἐὰν εὐθεῖα ἐπ᾽ εὐθεῖαν σταθεῖσα γωνίας ποιῇ, ἤτοι δύο ὀρθὰς ἢ δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς ἴσας ποιήσει.`,lat:`Si recta super rectam lineam erecta angulos effecerit, aut duos rectos aut duobus rectis aequales angulos efficiet.`,en:`If a line standing on a line makes angles, it makes either two right angles or angles equal to two right angles together.`,greekPages:[36],latinPages:[37]},steps:[{title:`Take the two angles made on a straight line`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Εὐθεῖα γάρ τις ἡ ΑΒ ἐπ᾽ εὐθεῖαν τὴν ΓΔ σταθεῖσα γωνίας ποιείτω τὰς ὑπὸ ΓΒΑ, ΑΒΔ· λέγω, ὅτι αἱ ὑπὸ ΓΒΑ, ΑΒΔ γωνίαι ἤτοι δύο ὀρθαί εἰσιν ἢ δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς ἴσαι.`,lat:`nam recta aliqua ΑΒ super rectam ΓΔ erecta angulos efficiat ΓΒΑ, ΑΒΔ. dico, angulos ΓΒΑ, ΑΒΔ aut duos rectos esse aut duobus rectis aequales.`,en:`Let ΑΒ stand on the straight line ΓΔ and make angles ΓΒΑ and ΑΒΔ. I claim that they are either two right angles or equal to two right angles together.`,greekPages:[36],latinPages:[37],language:`ἤτοι … ἤ / aut … aut separates two cases. In the second, δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς ἴσαι compares the pair's total with two right angles; it does not say the two original angles are equal to one another.`},{title:`If the adjacent angles are equal, use the definition`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`rightPairCase`,grc:`Εἰ μὲν οὖν ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ὑπὸ ΓΒΑ τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΒΔ, δύο ὀρθαί εἰσιν.`,lat:`iam si ΓΒΑ = ΑΒΔ, duo recti sunt.`,en:`If ΓΒΑ equals ΑΒΔ, they are two right angles.`,greekPages:[36],latinPages:[37],language:`εἰ μέν introduces the first conditional branch. Set ΓΒΑ to 90° to inspect it. The definition requires both equality and adjacency; the following unequal-angle branch is unnecessary in that case.`},{title:`Otherwise construct a perpendicular at Β`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`raiseForPair`,grc:`εἰ δὲ οὔ, ἤχθω ἀπὸ τοῦ Β σημείου τῇ ΓΔ [εὐθείᾳ] πρὸς ὀρθὰς ἡ ΒΕ· αἱ ἄρα ὑπὸ ΓΒΕ, ΕΒΔ δύο ὀρθαί εἰσιν·`,lat:`sin minus, a Β puncto ad rectam ΓΔ perpendicularis ducatur ΒΕ. itaque ΓΒΕ, ΕΒΔ duo recti sunt.`,en:`If not, draw ΒΕ from Β at right angles to ΓΔ. Thus ΓΒΕ and ΕΒΔ are two right angles.`,greekPages:[36],latinPages:[37],language:`εἰ δὲ οὔ / sin minus means ‘if not’. Heiberg brackets εὐθείᾳ in the Greek; those brackets are retained. ΒΕ is constructed by I.11 rather than chosen by a protractor.`},{title:`Express the right-angle pair as three parts`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`addFirstAngles`,grc:`καὶ ἐπεὶ ἡ ὑπὸ ΓΒΕ δυσὶ ταῖς ὑπὸ ΓΒΑ, ΑΒΕ ἴση ἐστίν, κοινὴ προσκείσθω ἡ ὑπὸ ΕΒΔ· αἱ ἄρα ὑπὸ ΓΒΕ, ΕΒΔ τρισὶ ταῖς ὑπὸ ΓΒΑ, ΑΒΕ, ΕΒΔ ἴσαι εἰσίν.`,lat:`et quoniam ΓΒΕ = ΓΒΑ + ΑΒΕ, communis adiciatur ΕΒΔ. itaque ΓΒΕ + ΕΒΔ = ΓΒΑ + ΑΒΕ + ΕΒΔ.`,en:`ΓΒΕ equals the two parts ΓΒΑ and ΑΒΕ. Add the common angle ΕΒΔ. Thus ΓΒΕ and ΕΒΔ together equal ΓΒΑ, ΑΒΕ and ΕΒΔ together.`,greekPages:[36],latinPages:[37],language:`προσκείσθω / adiciatur commands addition to both equal descriptions. δυσί names two parts and τρισί three. Latin writes their sum with plus signs where Greek names the angles in prose.`},{title:`Express the original pair as those same parts`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`addSecondAngles`,grc:`πάλιν, ἐπεὶ ἡ ὑπὸ ΔΒΑ δυσὶ ταῖς ὑπὸ ΔΒΕ, ΕΒΑ ἴση ἐστίν, κοινὴ προσκείσθω ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ· αἱ ἄρα ὑπὸ ΔΒΑ, ΑΒΓ τρισὶ ταῖς ὑπὸ ΔΒΕ, ΕΒΑ, ΑΒΓ ἴσαι εἰσίν.`,lat:`rursus quoniam ΔΒΑ = ΔΒΕ + ΕΒΑ, communis adiiciatur ΑΒΓ. itaque ΔΒΑ + ΑΒΓ = ΔΒΕ + ΕΒΑ + ΑΒΓ.`,en:`Again, ΔΒΑ equals ΔΒΕ and ΕΒΑ together. Add the common angle ΑΒΓ. The original pair ΔΒΑ and ΑΒΓ therefore equals the three parts ΔΒΕ, ΕΒΑ and ΑΒΓ.`,greekPages:[36],latinPages:[37],language:`πάλιν / rursus repeats the addition strategy. Reversing an angle's outer letters leaves the same angle: ΔΒΕ is ΕΒΔ, ΕΒΑ is ΑΒΕ, and ΑΒΓ is ΓΒΑ. These are the same three parts in another order.`},{title:`Connect the two totals through the shared three parts`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`equalAngleSums`,grc:`ἐδείχθησαν δὲ καὶ αἱ ὑπὸ ΓΒΕ, ΕΒΔ τρισὶ ταῖς αὐταῖς ἴσαι· τὰ δὲ τῷ αὐτῷ ἴσα καὶ ἀλλήλοις ἐστὶν ἴσα· καὶ αἱ ὑπὸ ΓΒΕ, ΕΒΔ ἄρα ταῖς ὑπὸ ΔΒΑ, ΑΒΓ ἴσαι εἰσίν·`,lat:`sed demonstratum est, etiam ΓΒΕ + ΕΒΔ isdem tribus aequales esse. quae autem eidem aequalia sunt, etiam inter se aequalia sunt. quare etiam ΓΒΕ + ΕΒΔ = ΔΒΑ + ΑΒΓ.`,en:`The right-angle pair ΓΒΕ and ΕΒΔ was also shown equal to those same three parts. Things equal to the same thing are equal to one another. Therefore ΓΒΕ and ΕΒΔ together equal ΔΒΑ and ΑΒΓ together.`,greekPages:[36],latinPages:[37],language:`ἐδείχθησαν / demonstratum est recalls a result already proved. ταῖς αὐταῖς / isdem means ‘the same’, not merely ‘equal-looking’. This is the point where common notion 1 connects the two sums.`},{title:`The original pair equals two right angles`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`equalAngleSums`,grc:`ἀλλὰ αἱ ὑπὸ ΓΒΕ, ΕΒΔ δύο ὀρθαί εἰσιν· καὶ αἱ ὑπὸ ΔΒΑ, ΑΒΓ ἄρα δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς ἴσαι εἰσίν.`,lat:`uerum ΓΒΕ + ΕΒΔ duo recti sunt. itaque etiam ΔΒΑ + ΑΒΓ duobus rectis sunt aequales.`,en:`ΓΒΕ and ΕΒΔ are two right angles. Hence ΔΒΑ and ΑΒΓ together equal two right angles.`,greekPages:[36],latinPages:[37],language:`ἀλλά / uerum returns to the known right-angle pair. The numerical 180° check is a modern way to measure the resulting total, not an extra premise used in the source argument.`},{title:`The two cases establish the proposition`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`finish`,grc:`Ἐὰν ἄρα εὐθεῖα ἐπ᾽ εὐθεῖαν σταθεῖσα γωνίας ποιῇ, ἤτοι δύο ὀρθὰς ἢ δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς ἴσας ποιήσει· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo si recta super rectam lineam erecta angulos effecerit, aut duos rectos aut duobus rectis aequales angulos efficiet; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Therefore a line standing on a line makes either two right angles or angles equal to two right angles together. This is what had to be shown.`,greekPages:[36,38],latinPages:[37,39],language:`The conclusion crosses a page break in both languages. δεῖξαι / demonstrandum closes a theorem, in contrast with the construction endings ποιῆσαι / fieri in I.11 and I.12.`}],insight:{title:`A shared sum can prove an angle total`,text:`The perpendicular divides the original pair into three pieces. Group them once as two right angles and again as the original pair. Their common sum connects the totals without assuming the theorem in advance. At the 90° setting the source's first branch applies directly.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI13.html`},question:{prompt:`What does ‘equal to two right angles’ describe in the unequal case?`,options:[`The sum of the two adjacent angles`,`Each angle individually`,`The angle between two parallel lines`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. Each original angle may differ from a right angle, while their total equals the constructed pair.`,`That would force the equal-angle case. The second branch allows one acute and one obtuse angle.`,`No parallel lines or parallel postulate are used here.`]}},14:{id:14,numeral:`I.14`,greekNumeral:`ιδʹ`,title:`Recover a straight line from its angle condition`,shortTitle:`The converse`,introduction:`Now reverse the implication. Adjacent angles totaling two right angles force the two outer rays to form one straight line. A different supposed continuation leads to unequal angles being declared equal.`,statement:{grc:`Ἐὰν πρός τινι εὐθείᾳ καὶ τῷ πρὸς αὐτῇ σημείῳ δύο εὐθεῖαι μὴ ἐπὶ τὰ αὐτὰ μέρη κείμεναι τὰς ἐφεξῆς γωνίας δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς ἴσας ποιῶσιν, ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας ἔσονται ἀλλήλαις αἱ εὐθεῖαι.`,lat:`Si duae rectae ad rectam aliquam et punctum eius non in eadem parte positae angulos deinceps positos duobus rectis aequales effecerint, in eadem erunt linea recta.`,en:`If two lines at a point on a line, lying on different sides of it, make adjacent angles equal to two right angles together, then those two lines lie in one straight line.`,greekPages:[38],latinPages:[39]},steps:[{title:`Keep the two-right-angle premise`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Πρὸς γάρ τινι εὐθείᾳ τῇ ΑΒ καὶ τῷ πρὸς αὐτῇ σημείῳ τῷ Β δύο εὐθεῖαι αἱ ΒΓ, ΒΔ μὴ ἐπὶ τὰ αὐτὰ μέρη κείμεναι τὰς ἐφεξῆς γωνίας τὰς ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ, ΑΒΔ δύο ὀρθαῖς ἴσας ποιείτωσαν· λέγω, ὅτι ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας ἐστὶ τῇ ΓΒ ἡ ΒΔ.`,lat:`Nam ad rectam aliquam ΑΒ et punctum eius Β duae rectae ΒΓ, ΒΔ non in eadem parte positae angulos deinceps positos ΑΒΓ, ΑΒΔ duobus rectis aequales efficiant. dico, ΓΒ et ΒΔ in eadem recta esse.`,en:`Let ΒΓ and ΒΔ stand on different sides of ΑΒ at Β, making adjacent angles ΑΒΓ and ΑΒΔ equal to two right angles together. I claim that ΓΒ and ΒΔ lie in one straight line.`,greekPages:[38],latinPages:[39],language:`μὴ ἐπὶ τὰ αὐτὰ μέρη / non in eadem parte excludes placing both outer rays on the same side of ΑΒ. Heiberg's Greek body text prints δύο ὀρθαῖς, while the proposition statement uses δυσίν; the printed forms are retained.`},{title:`Suppose a different straight continuation ΒΕ`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`converseSupposition`,grc:`Εἰ γὰρ μή ἐστι τῇ ΒΓ ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας ἡ ΒΔ, ἔστω τῇ ΓΒ ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας ἡ ΒΕ.`,lat:`nam si ΒΓ et ΒΔ non sunt in eadem recta, ΓΒ et ΒΕ in eadem recta sint.`,en:`If ΒΔ is not in a straight line with ΒΓ, let ΒΕ be the straight continuation of ΓΒ instead.`,greekPages:[38],latinPages:[39],language:`εἰ … μή / si … non opens a temporary negative supposition. ἔστω / sint introduces its alternative. The hypothetical sketch cannot realize both the given angle condition and a different straight continuation; the plate states that limit explicitly.`},{title:`Apply I.13 under that temporary claim`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`conditionalLinearPair`,grc:`Ἐπεὶ οὖν εὐθεῖα ἡ ΑΒ ἐπ᾽ εὐθεῖαν τὴν ΓΒΕ ἐφέστηκεν, αἱ ἄρα ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ, ΑΒΕ γωνίαι δύο ὀρθαῖς ἴσαι εἰσίν·`,lat:`iam quoniam recta ΑΒ super rectam ΓΒΕ erecta est, ∠ ΑΒΓ + ΑΒΕ duobus rectis aequales sunt.`,en:`Under the supposition that ΑΒ stands on the straight line ΓΒΕ, angles ΑΒΓ and ΑΒΕ together equal two right angles.`,greekPages:[38],latinPages:[39],language:`ἐφέστηκεν / erecta est describes the standing-on relationship assumed in this branch. The English marks its conditional status; the source need not repeat ‘if’ at each consequence of the supposition.`},{title:`Compare with the originally given angle pair`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`compareGivenPair`,grc:`εἰσὶ δὲ καὶ αἱ ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ, ΑΒΔ δύο ὀρθαῖς ἴσαι· αἱ ἄρα ὑπὸ ΓΒΑ, ΑΒΕ ταῖς ὑπὸ ΓΒΑ, ΑΒΔ ἴσαι εἰσίν.`,lat:`uerum etiam ΑΒΓ + ΑΒΔ duobus rectis aequales sunt. itaque ΓΒΑ + ΑΒΕ = ΓΒΑ + ΑΒΔ.`,en:`The given pair ΑΒΓ and ΑΒΔ also equals two right angles. Thus ΓΒΑ and ΑΒΕ together would equal ΓΒΑ and ΑΒΔ together.`,greekPages:[38],latinPages:[39],language:`καί / etiam means ‘also’: it links the conditional total to the original premise. The source changes ΑΒΓ to ΓΒΑ; the outer-letter reversal leaves the same angle.`},{title:`Remove the common angle`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`removeCommonAngle`,grc:`κοινὴ ἀφῃρήσθω ἡ ὑπὸ ΓΒΑ· λοιπὴ ἄρα ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΒΕ λοιπῇ τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΒΔ ἐστιν ἴση,`,lat:`subtrahatur, qui communis est, ∠ ΓΒΑ. itaque ∠ ΑΒΕ = ΑΒΔ,`,en:`Subtract the common angle ΓΒΑ. The remainder ΑΒΕ would therefore equal the remainder ΑΒΔ.`,greekPages:[38],latinPages:[39],language:`ἀφῃρήσθω / subtrahatur commands subtraction. λοιπή names what remains after removing the same angle from both sums. The resulting equality is still under the supposition, not a measured equality of the sketch.`},{title:`The smaller cannot equal the larger`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`impossibleAnglePart`,grc:`ἡ ἐλάσσων τῇ μείζονι· ὅπερ ἐστὶν ἀδύνατον.`,lat:`minor maiori; quod fieri non potest.`,en:`That would make the smaller equal to the larger, which is impossible.`,greekPages:[38],latinPages:[39],language:`ἡ ἐλάσσων / minor identifies the smaller angle as the subject; τῇ μείζονι / maiori names the larger one it would equal. ἀδύνατον is negative: impossible. The actual sketch shows the proper-part inequality, not the conditional equality.`},{title:`Exclude other continuations and retain ΒΔ`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`impossibleAnglePart`,grc:`οὐκ ἄρα ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας ἐστὶν ἡ ΒΕ τῇ ΓΒ. ὁμοίως δὴ δείξομεν, ὅτι οὐδὲ ἄλλη τις πλὴν τῆς ΒΔ· ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας ἄρα ἐστὶν ἡ ΓΒ τῇ ΒΔ.`,lat:`quare ΒΕ et ΓΒ non sunt in eadem recta. similiter idem de quauis alia recta praeter ΒΔ demonstrabimus. itaque ΓΒ et ΒΔ in eadem recta sunt.`,en:`Therefore ΒΕ cannot be the straight continuation of ΓΒ. Likewise any other line except ΒΔ is excluded. Hence ΓΒ and ΒΔ lie in one straight line.`,greekPages:[38],latinPages:[39],language:`πλὴν / praeter means ‘except’. The rejection is not merely of the one drawn Ε: ὁμοίως / similiter extends it to any different proposed continuation.`},{title:`The angle condition forces a straight line`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`finish`,grc:`Ἐὰν ἄρα πρός τινι εὐθείᾳ καὶ τῷ πρὸς αὐτῇ σημείῳ δύο εὐθεῖαι μὴ ἐπὶ τὰ αὐτὰ μέρη κείμεναι τὰς ἐφεξῆς γωνίας δυσὶν ὀρθαῖς ἴσας ποιῶσιν, ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας ἔσονται ἀλλήλαις αἱ εὐθεῖαι· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo si duae rectae ad rectam aliquam et punctum eius non in eadem parte positae angulos deinceps positos duobus rectis aequales effecerint, in eadem erunt linea recta; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Therefore two lines on different sides of a line at its point, making adjacent angles equal to two right angles together, lie in one straight line. This is what had to be shown.`,greekPages:[40],latinPages:[41],language:`The conclusion restores the full placement condition. I.13 went from a straight line to an angle total; I.14 has proved the converse rather than simply reading the earlier implication backward.`}],insight:{title:`A contradiction sketch cannot satisfy everything`,text:`The source supposes a different continuation while retaining the original angle premise. No plane figure can realize that combination. The plate follows the printed arrangement, labels its temporary sketch, and keeps measured angles distinct from the equality that the supposition would force. The original given figure returns when that supposition is rejected.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI14.html`},question:{prompt:`Why does the plate not display the hypothetical remainders as numerically equal?`,options:[`Their forced equality contradicts the smaller/larger relationship; no such plane figure realizes all the premises`,`The numerical calculation is being used as Euclid's proof`,`I.14 allows two different straight continuations`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. The conditional equality belongs to the contradiction argument. The sketch must not pretend to realize it.`,`The logical contradiction uses the established angle relations and the whole–part principle. Measurements only check the displayed sketch.`,`The contradiction excludes every different continuation under the stated angle and placement conditions.`]}}},c={nestedExtend:{title:`Postulate 2 · continue ΒΔ to the opposite side`,kind:`construction`,text:`Because Δ is strictly inside ΑΒΓ, the ray from Β through Δ meets ΑΓ at an interior point Ε. The point ordering and intersection are geometric assumptions used by the source. A modern treatment must justify them; the drawing does not supply a separate proof.`},nestedFirst:{title:`I.20 · the two sides of ΑΒΕ exceed the third`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:20,text:`Apply the triangle inequality in ΑΒΕ: ΑΒ + ΑΕ > ΒΕ. These are three sides of one auxiliary triangle, not the two routes that the proposition ultimately compares.`},nestedAdd:{title:`Add the common segment ΕΓ`,kind:`assumption`,text:`Adding the same positive length preserves a strict inequality. Since Ε lies between Α and Γ, ΑΕ + ΕΓ = ΑΓ. Thus the outer route Β–Α–Γ exceeds the intermediate route Β–Ε–Γ. Heiberg cites expanded common notion 4 here; it is not the usual common notion 4 about coincidence.`},nestedSecond:{title:`I.20 again · use ΓΕΔ and add ΔΒ`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:20,text:`In ΓΕΔ, ΓΕ + ΕΔ > ΓΔ. Add ΔΒ to both sides. Because Δ lies between Β and Ε, ΕΔ + ΔΒ = ΕΒ. Therefore the intermediate route Γ–Ε–Β exceeds the inner route Γ–Δ–Β. Its reversed reading direction changes no length.`},nestedLengths:{title:`Chain the two strict length inequalities`,kind:`deduction`,text:`The outer route exceeds the intermediate route, which exceeds the inner route. Strict order is transitive. The total shortening equals the sum of the two positive triangle-inequality excesses; the measurements expose that identity as an additional modern observation.`},nestedFirstAngle:{title:`I.16 · the exterior angle at Δ`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:16,text:`In triangle ΓΔΕ, continue ΕΔ past Δ toward Β. The exterior angle ΒΔΓ exceeds the opposite interior angle ΓΕΔ. Since ΕΔ and ΕΒ are the same ray, ΓΕΔ equals ΓΕΒ. No theorem about the sum of three triangle angles is used.`},nestedSecondAngle:{title:`I.16 again · the exterior angle at Ε`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:16,text:`In triangle ΑΒΕ, continue ΑΕ past Ε toward Γ. Its exterior angle ΓΕΒ exceeds the opposite interior angle ΒΑΕ, which is ΒΑΓ because ΑΕ and ΑΓ are the same ray.`},nestedAngles:{title:`Chain the angle comparisons`,kind:`deduction`,text:`The inner angle ΒΔΓ exceeds the intermediate angle ΓΕΒ, which exceeds the outer angle ΒΑΓ. This proves the angle claim independently of the length claim. A shorter pair of segments does not by itself prove a greater included angle.`},nestedConclude:{title:`Both parts of the proposition are proved`,kind:`deduction`,text:`For the given interior meeting point, the inner two sides together are shorter, but their included angle is greater. The inequalities compare sums of lengths, not each corresponding side individually. The controls preserve the strict interior hypothesis.`}},l={21:{id:21,numeral:`I.21`,greekNumeral:`καʹ`,title:`Shorten the route, enlarge the angle`,shortTitle:`Inside the triangle`,introduction:`Two segments meet inside a triangle. Their total length falls below the outer two sides, yet their included angle exceeds the outer angle. One intermediate route explains both comparisons.`,statement:{grc:`Ἐὰν τριγώνου ἐπὶ μιᾶς τῶν πλευρῶν ἀπὸ τῶν περάτων δύο εὐθεῖαι ἐντὸς συσταθῶσιν, αἱ συσταθεῖσαι τῶν λοιπῶν τοῦ τριγώνου δύο πλευρῶν ἐλάττονες μὲν ἔσονται, μείζονα δὲ γωνίαν περιέξουσιν.`,lat:`Si in uno latere trianguli a terminis duae rectae intus coniunguntur, rectae coniunctae reliquis duobus lateribus trianguli minores erunt, maiorem autem angulum comprehendent.`,en:`If two straight lines from the endpoints of one side of a triangle meet inside it, the lines so joined will together be less than the remaining two sides of the triangle, but will contain a greater angle.`,greekPages:[50],latinPages:[51]},steps:[{title:`Join the endpoints to an interior point`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Τριγώνου γὰρ τοῦ ΑΒΓ ἐπὶ μιᾶς τῶν πλευρῶν τῆς ΒΓ ἀπὸ τῶν περάτων τῶν Β, Γ δύο εὐθεῖαι ἐντὸς συνεστάτωσαν αἱ ΒΔ, ΔΓ· λέγω, ὅτι αἱ ΒΔ, ΔΓ τῶν λοιπῶν τοῦ τριγώνου δύο πλευρῶν τῶν ΒΑ, ΑΓ ἐλάσσονες μέν εἰσιν, μείζονα δὲ γωνίαν περιέχουσι τὴν ὑπὸ ΒΔΓ τῆς ὑπὸ ΒΑΓ.`,lat:`In triangulo enim ΑΒΓ in uno latere ΒΓ a terminis Β, Γ duae rectae intus coniungantur ΒΔ, ΔΓ. dico, esse ΒΔ + ΔΓ < ΒΑ + ΑΓ et ∠ ΒΔΓ > ΒΑΓ.`,en:`In triangle ΑΒΓ, let ΒΔ and ΔΓ meet inside it from the endpoints Β and Γ of side ΒΓ. I say that ΒΔ and ΔΓ together are less than ΒΑ and ΑΓ together, but contain an angle ΒΔΓ greater than ΒΑΓ.`,greekPages:[50],latinPages:[51],language:`ἐντός / intus means inside: it is a hypothesis, not decoration. μέν … δέ balances the two surprising claims: smaller lengths, but a larger angle. Heiberg makes the sums explicit with + signs in Latin.`},{title:`Continue ΒΔ to Ε on ΑΓ`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`nestedExtend`,grc:`Διήχθω γὰρ ἡ ΒΔ ἐπὶ τὸ Ε.`,lat:`educatur enim ΒΔ ad Ε.`,en:`For let ΒΔ be continued to Ε [on ΑΓ].`,greekPages:[50],latinPages:[51],language:`Διήχθω is a third-person perfect passive imperative: let it have been carried through. Latin educatur is a passive subjunctive used as an instruction. The English bracket explains the intersection supplied by the figure.`},{title:`Compare the first auxiliary triangle`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`nestedFirst`,grc:`καὶ ἐπεὶ παντὸς τριγώνου αἱ δύο πλευραὶ τῆς λοιπῆς μείζονές εἰσιν, τοῦ ΑΒΕ ἄρα τριγώνου αἱ δύο πλευραὶ αἱ ΑΒ, ΑΕ τῆς ΒΕ μείζονές εἰσιν·`,lat:`et quoniam in quouis triangulo duo latera reliquo maiora sunt, in triangulo ΑΒΕ erunt ΑΒ + ΑΕ > ΒΕ.`,en:`Since any two sides of a triangle together are greater than the remaining side, the sides ΑΒ and ΑΕ of triangle ΑΒΕ are together greater than ΒΕ.`,greekPages:[50],latinPages:[51],language:`τῆς λοιπῆς is genitive after μείζονες: greater than the remaining side. The noun πλευρᾶς is understood. Latin reliquo is ablative of comparison, with latere understood.`},{title:`Add the same remaining piece`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`nestedAdd`,grc:`κοινὴ προσκείσθω ἡ ΕΓ· αἱ ἄρα ΒΑ, ΑΓ τῶν ΒΕ, ΕΓ μείζονές εἰσιν.`,lat:`communis adiciatur ΕΓ. itaque ΒΑ + ΑΓ > ΒΕ + ΕΓ.`,en:`Let ΕΓ be added in common. Therefore ΒΑ and ΑΓ together are greater than ΒΕ and ΕΓ together.`,greekPages:[50],latinPages:[51],language:`κοινή / communis says the same segment is added to both sides of the inequality. ΑΕ and ΕΓ become the single whole ΑΓ. ΒΑ and ΑΒ name the same length.`},{title:`Shorten the intermediate route once more`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`nestedSecond`,grc:`πάλιν, ἐπεὶ τοῦ ΓΕΔ τριγώνου αἱ δύο πλευραὶ αἱ ΓΕ, ΕΔ τῆς ΓΔ μείζονές εἰσιν, κοινὴ προσκείσθω ἡ ΔΒ· αἱ ΓΕ, ΕΒ ἄρα τῶν ΓΔ, ΔΒ μείζονές εἰσιν.`,lat:`rursus quoniam in ΓΕΔ triangulo ΓΕ + ΕΔ > ΓΔ, communis adiciatur ΔΒ. itaque ΓΕ + ΕΒ > ΓΔ + ΔΒ.`,en:`Again, since ΓΕ and ΕΔ in triangle ΓΕΔ are together greater than ΓΔ, let ΔΒ be added in common. Therefore ΓΕ and ΕΒ together are greater than ΓΔ and ΔΒ together.`,greekPages:[50],latinPages:[51],language:`πάλιν / rursus announces a second application of the same idea to a different triangle. ΕΔ plus ΔΒ becomes ΕΒ because the points occur in the order Ε, Δ, Β.`},{title:`Chain the length comparisons`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`nestedLengths`,grc:`ἀλλὰ τῶν ΒΕ, ΕΓ μείζονες ἐδείχθησαν αἱ ΒΑ, ΑΓ· πολλῷ ἄρα αἱ ΒΑ, ΑΓ τῶν ΒΔ, ΔΓ μείζονές εἰσιν.`,lat:`sed demonstratum est ΒΑ + ΑΓ > ΒΕ + ΕΓ. itaque multo magis ΒΑ + ΑΓ > ΒΔ + ΔΓ.`,en:`But ΒΑ and ΑΓ together were shown to exceed ΒΕ and ΕΓ together. Therefore, all the more, ΒΑ and ΑΓ together exceed ΒΔ and ΔΓ together.`,greekPages:[50],latinPages:[51],language:`ἐδείχθησαν is aorist passive: they were shown. πολλῷ and multo strengthen the comparison—by much, or all the more—without claiming a fixed numerical margin.`},{title:`Compare the exterior angle at Δ`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`nestedFirstAngle`,grc:`Πάλιν, ἐπεὶ παντὸς τριγώνου ἡ ἐκτὸς γωνία τῆς ἐντὸς καὶ ἀπεναντίον μείζων ἐστίν, τοῦ ΓΔΕ ἄρα τριγώνου ἡ ἐκτὸς γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΔΓ μείζων ἐστὶ τῆς ὑπὸ ΓΕΔ.`,lat:`rursus quoniam in quouis triangulo angulus extrinsecus positus maior est angulo interiore et opposito, in triangulo ΓΔΕ erit ∠ ΒΔΓ > ΓΕΔ.`,en:`Again, since an exterior angle of any triangle is greater than an interior and opposite angle, the exterior angle ΒΔΓ of triangle ΓΔΕ is greater than ΓΕΔ.`,greekPages:[52],latinPages:[53],language:`ἐκτός now means outside the small triangle ΓΔΕ, even though Δ is inside the original triangle. interiore et opposito preserves both qualifications: interior and opposite, not adjacent.`},{title:`Compare the exterior angle at Ε`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`nestedSecondAngle`,grc:`διὰ ταὐτὰ τοίνυν καὶ τοῦ ΑΒΕ τριγώνου ἡ ἐκτὸς γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΓΕΒ μείζων ἐστὶ τῆς ὑπὸ ΒΑΓ.`,lat:`eadem de causa igitur etiam in triangulo ΑΒΕ erit ∠ ΓΕΒ > ΒΑΓ.`,en:`For the same reason, the exterior angle ΓΕΒ of triangle ΑΒΕ is also greater than ΒΑΓ.`,greekPages:[52],latinPages:[53],language:`διὰ ταὐτά means for the same reasons; ταὐτά contracts τὰ αὐτά. Latin eadem de causa uses the singular cause. The unchanged direction of rays ΕΔ and ΕΒ identifies the intermediate angle.`},{title:`Chain the angle comparisons`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`nestedAngles`,grc:`ἀλλὰ τῆς ὑπὸ ΓΕΒ μείζων ἐδείχθη ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΔΓ· πολλῷ ἄρα ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΔΓ μείζων ἐστὶ τῆς ὑπὸ ΒΑΓ.`,lat:`uerum demonstratum est ∠ ΒΔΓ > ΓΕΒ. multo igitur magis ΒΔΓ > ΒΑΓ.`,en:`But ΒΔΓ was shown to be greater than ΓΕΒ. Therefore, all the more, ΒΔΓ is greater than ΒΑΓ.`,greekPages:[52],latinPages:[53],language:`ἐδείχθη is singular here because its subject is one angle; the length argument used plural ἐδείχθησαν. The same rhetorical pattern carries a separate mathematical comparison.`},{title:`Keep both conclusions together`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`nestedConclude`,grc:`Ἐὰν ἄρα τριγώνου ἐπὶ μιᾶς τῶν πλευρῶν ἀπὸ τῶν περάτων δύο εὐθεῖαι ἐντὸς συσταθῶσιν, αἱ συσταθεῖσαι τῶν λοιπῶν τοῦ τριγώνου δύο πλευρῶν ἐλάττονες μὲν ἔσονται, μείζονα δὲ γωνίαν περιέξουσιν· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo si in uno latere trianguli a terminis duae rectae intus coniunguntur, rectae coniunctae reliquis duobus lateribus trianguli minores erunt, maiorem autem angulum comprehendent; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Therefore, if two straight lines from the endpoints of one side of a triangle meet inside it, they will together be less than the remaining two sides, but contain a greater angle; which was to be proved.`,greekPages:[52],latinPages:[53],language:`συσταθῶσιν follows ἐάν as an aorist passive subjunctive. The futures ἔσονται and περιέξουσιν state what will follow. Latin minores erunt and maiorem … comprehendent reproduce the two consequences.`}],insight:{title:`One intermediate route, two different proofs`,text:`Β–Ε–Γ sits between the outer and inner routes in length. The angle at Ε also sits between their included angles, in the reverse order. I.20 proves the length chain; I.16 proves the angle chain. Neither conclusion is inferred just by watching the controls. The numeric shortening is the sum of the two auxiliary triangles’ positive excesses.`,link:`https://archive.org/details/euclidisoperaomn01eucluoft/page/50/mode/2up`},question:{prompt:`What does I.21 prove about the two inner segments?`,options:[`Each inner segment is shorter than its corresponding outer side.`,`Their sum is smaller, while their included angle is greater.`,`Their sum is smaller, so the angle claim needs no separate proof.`],correct:1,explanations:[`The source compares the sums. Individual corresponding sides need not both decrease; move Δ near Β to explore this.`,`Yes. Two uses of I.20 establish the length result, and two uses of I.16 establish the angle result.`,`The angle result has its own proof. A comparison of sums alone does not establish this included-angle order.`]}}},u={perpendicularCopy:{title:`I.2 · place an equal distance at Γ`,text:`Choose Δ between Α and Γ. Obtain ΓΕ equal to ΓΔ on the other ray of ΑΒ. Heiberg's Latin margin cites I.2 here. The model obtains the copied distance through I.2, then intersects a circle at Γ with the given line to locate Ε. Equal-looking spacing is not the premise.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:2},perpendicularEquilateral:{title:`I.1 · an equilateral triangle on ΔΕ`,text:`Construct ΖΔΕ with the two equal-radius circles of I.1. ΖΔ = ΖΕ will supply the third side pair for the two triangles beside ΓΖ.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:1},perpendicularSidePairs:{title:`The copied lengths and one common side`,text:`ΔΓ = ΓΕ is the earlier construction's result. ΓΖ is a single side shared by both triangles. These give the first two corresponding side pairs without assuming any right angle.`,kind:`given`},perpendicularSSS:{title:`I.8 · equality of adjacent angles`,text:`Compare ΔΓΖ with ΕΓΖ. The copied sides ΔΓ and ΕΓ are equal, ΓΖ is common, and ΔΖ = ΕΖ comes from the equilateral triangle. I.8 proves equality of the two angles at Γ. Their adjacency follows from Δ, Γ and Ε lying on the given line.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:8},rightDefinition:{title:`Definition 10 · equal adjacent angles are right`,text:`A line standing on a line makes equal adjacent angles. Definition 10 calls each right and the standing line perpendicular. The construction earns the angle equality first; the measured 90° readout is a check, not the reason.`,kind:`definition`},oppositeSide:{title:`Choose the auxiliary point on the other side`,text:`Δ must lie across ΑΒ from Γ. Then the distance ΓΔ is greater than the perpendicular distance from Γ to the line, so the circle has two distinct crossings in the Euclidean plane. Euclid uses that crossing without separately proving a continuity axiom. A same-side choice loses the guarantee: the circle can miss, touch, or still cross the line.`,kind:`assumption`},chordMidpoint:{title:`I.10 · bisect the chord ΕΗ`,text:`Ε and Η are the two intersections of the circle with the unbounded line. Apply I.10 to their segment: its equilateral and bisector constructions locate Θ. The model calls that construction rather than assigning the perpendicular foot by projection.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:10},chordPairs:{title:`The constructed halves and their common side`,text:`I.10 supplied ΗΘ = ΘΕ. ΘΓ belongs to both triangles. No angle equality has been used to place Θ.`,kind:`given`,lesson:10},chordSSS:{title:`I.8 · equal radii complete the side comparison`,text:`ΓΗ and ΓΕ are radii of the same circle. Together with ΗΘ = ΘΕ and common ΘΓ, I.8 gives equal angles ΓΘΗ and ΕΘΓ. Their other rays run in opposite directions on the given straight line.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:8}},d={11:{id:11,numeral:`I.11`,greekNumeral:`ιαʹ`,title:`Raise a perpendicular from the line`,shortTitle:`Raise a perpendicular`,introduction:`Two equal distances and an equilateral triangle give equal adjacent angles. Only then does the definition name those angles right.`,statement:{grc:`Τῇ δοθείσῃ εὐθείᾳ ἀπὸ τοῦ πρὸς αὐτῇ δοθέντος σημείου πρὸς ὀρθὰς γωνίας εὐθεῖαν γραμμὴν ἀγαγεῖν.`,lat:`Ad datam rectam a dato puncto in ea sito rectam perpendicularem erigere.`,en:`Draw a straight line at right angles to a given straight line, from a given point on it.`,greekPages:[32],latinPages:[33]},steps:[{title:`Take the line and a point on it`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω ἡ μὲν δοθεῖσα εὐθεῖα ἡ ΑΒ τὸ δὲ δοθὲν σημεῖον ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς τὸ Γ· δεῖ δὴ ἀπὸ τοῦ Γ σημείου τῇ ΑΒ εὐθείᾳ πρὸς ὀρθὰς γωνίας εὐθεῖαν γραμμὴν ἀγαγεῖν.`,lat:`Sit data recta ΑΒ, punctum autem datum in ea situm Γ. oportet igitur a Γ puncto rectae ΑΒ perpendicularem rectam erigere.`,en:`Let ΑΒ be the given straight line and Γ the given point on it. From Γ we must draw a line at right angles to ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[32],latinPages:[33],language:`ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς / in ea situm places Γ on the given line. This condition distinguishes the task from I.12, where the given point is outside the line. πρὸς ὀρθὰς γωνίας means ‘at right angles’, not ‘toward the right-hand side’.`},{title:`Choose Δ and obtain an equal distance`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`perpendicularCopy`,grc:`Εἰλήφθω ἐπὶ τῆς ΑΓ τυχὸν σημεῖον τὸ Δ, καὶ κείσθω τῇ ΓΔ ἴση ἡ ΓΕ,`,lat:`sumatur in ΑΓ quoduis punctum Δ, et ponatur ΓΕ = ΓΔ,`,en:`Choose any point Δ on ΑΓ, and place ΓΕ equal to ΓΔ.`,greekPages:[32],latinPages:[33],language:`τυχὸν / quoduis permits a chosen point. κείσθω / ponatur commands an equal placement; it does not assert that Γ was given as the midpoint of ΑΒ. The printed figure places Ε on the other ray from Γ. Heiberg's margin cites I.2, not I.3.`},{title:`Construct an equilateral triangle on ΔΕ`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`perpendicularEquilateral`,grc:`καὶ συνεστάτω ἐπὶ τῆς ΔΕ τρίγωνον ἰσόπλευρον τὸ ΖΔΕ,`,lat:`et in ΔΕ triangulus aequilaterus construatur ΖΔΕ,`,en:`On ΔΕ construct the equilateral triangle ΖΔΕ.`,greekPages:[32],latinPages:[33],language:`The same construction command συνεστάτω / construatur now reuses I.1 on a different base. The new vertex Ζ is not chosen by looking for a right angle above Γ.`},{title:`Join Ζ to Γ and announce the claim`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p1`,grc:`καὶ ἐπεζεύχθω ἡ ΖΓ· λέγω, ὅτι τῇ δοθείσῃ εὐθείᾳ τῇ ΑΒ ἀπὸ τοῦ πρὸς αὐτῇ δοθέντος σημείου τοῦ Γ πρὸς ὀρθὰς γωνίας εὐθεῖα γραμμὴ ἧκται ἡ ΖΓ.`,lat:`et ducatur ΖΓ. dico, ad datam rectam ΑΒ a dato puncto in ea sito Γ perpendicularem erectam esse rectam lineam ΖΓ.`,en:`Join ΖΓ. I claim that ΖΓ has been drawn from Γ at right angles to the given line ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[32],latinPages:[33],language:`ἧκται / erectam esse describes the claimed completed construction. After λέγω / dico it still needs the argument that follows. Joining the points has not itself established perpendicularity.`},{title:`Identify two equal side pairs`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`perpendicularSidePairs`,grc:`Ἐπεὶ γὰρ ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΔΓ τῇ ΓΕ, κοινὴ δὲ ἡ ΓΖ, δύο δὴ αἱ ΔΓ, ΓΖ δυσὶ ταῖς ΕΓ, ΓΖ ἴσαι εἰσὶν ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ·`,lat:`nam quoniam ΔΓ = ΓΕ et communis ΓΖ, duae rectae ΔΓ, ΓΖ duabus ΕΓ, ΓΖ aequales sunt altera alteri;`,en:`ΔΓ equals ΓΕ, and ΓΖ is common. Thus ΔΓ and ΓΖ equal ΕΓ and ΓΖ respectively.`,greekPages:[32],latinPages:[33],language:`ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ / altera alteri pairs the sides in order. ΕΓ and ΓΕ name the same segment with reversed endpoints; the reversal follows the triangle correspondence.`},{title:`Use the third side pair to prove equal adjacent angles`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`perpendicularSSS`,grc:`καὶ βάσις ἡ ΔΖ βάσει τῇ ΖΕ ἴση ἐστίν· γωνία ἄρα ἡ ὑπὸ ΔΓΖ γωνίᾳ τῇ ὑπὸ ΕΓΖ ἴση ἐστίν· καί εἰσιν ἐφεξῆς.`,lat:`et basis ΔΖ basi ΖΕ aequalis est. itaque ∠ ΔΓΖ = ΕΓΖ; et deinceps sunt positi.`,en:`The base ΔΖ also equals ΖΕ. Therefore angle ΔΓΖ equals angle ΕΓΖ, and these angles are adjacent.`,greekPages:[32],latinPages:[33],language:`ἐφεξῆς / deinceps says ‘adjacent’ or ‘in succession’. Equality alone would not make two arbitrary angles right: their position together on the straight line is essential.`},{title:`Apply the definition of a right angle`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`rightDefinition`,grc:`ὅταν δὲ εὐθεῖα ἐπ᾽ εὐθεῖαν σταθεῖσα τὰς ἐφεξῆς γωνίας ἴσας ἀλλήλαις ποιῇ, ὀρθὴ ἑκατέρα τῶν ἴσων γωνιῶν ἐστιν· ὀρθὴ ἄρα ἐστὶν ἑκατέρα τῶν ὑπὸ ΔΓΖ, ΖΓΕ.`,lat:`ubi autem recta super rectam lineam erecta angulos deinceps positos inter se aequales efficit, rectus est uterque angulus aequalis. itaque ΔΓΖ, ΖΓΕ recti sunt.`,en:`When a line standing on a line makes the adjacent angles equal, each of those equal angles is right. Therefore each of ΔΓΖ and ΖΓΕ is right.`,greekPages:[32],latinPages:[33],language:`ὅταν with ποιῇ gives a general ‘whenever’ condition. ἑκατέρα and Latin uterque distribute the conclusion to each of the two angles. The definition, not a degree measurement, supplies ‘right’.`},{title:`The required perpendicular is constructed`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`finish`,grc:`Τῇ ἄρα δοθείσῃ εὐθείᾳ τῇ ΑΒ ἀπὸ τοῦ πρὸς αὐτῇ δοθέντος σημείου τοῦ Γ πρὸς ὀρθὰς γωνίας εὐθεῖα γραμμὴ ἧκται ἡ ΓΖ· ὅπερ ἔδει ποιῆσαι.`,lat:`Ergo ad datam rectam ΑΒ a dato puncto in ea sito Γ perpendicularis recta linea ducta est ΓΖ; quod oportebat fieri.`,en:`Thus ΓΖ has been drawn at right angles to the given line ΑΒ from its given point Γ. The requested construction is complete.`,greekPages:[32],latinPages:[33],language:`The conclusion returns to the given point and line. ποιῆσαι / fieri closes a construction problem. ΓΖ reverses the earlier ΖΓ without changing the line that was constructed.`}],insight:{title:`The right angle is a conclusion`,text:`The diagram may look perpendicular as soon as ΖΓ is joined. The proof earns that description only after three side pairs establish equal adjacent angles. Move Γ along ΑΒ: Γ need not bisect the original given segment, because the construction makes its own equal distances ΔΓ and ΓΕ.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI11.html`},question:{prompt:`What makes the equal angles at Γ right?`,options:[`They are adjacent angles made by a line standing on a straight line`,`Every pair of equal angles is right`,`Γ is the midpoint of the originally given ΑΒ`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. I.8 establishes equality, and their adjacent position lets definition 10 apply.`,`Equal angles could both be acute or both obtuse. Their position on the straight line is required.`,`Γ is given anywhere on ΑΒ. The construction makes it the midpoint of ΔΕ, not necessarily of ΑΒ.`]}},12:{id:12,numeral:`I.12`,greekNumeral:`ιβʹ`,title:`Drop a perpendicular from outside the line`,shortTitle:`Drop a perpendicular`,introduction:`A circle cuts the line twice, and the chord's constructed midpoint supplies the foot. The short instruction ‘on the other side’ makes those two crossings available.`,statement:{grc:`Ἐπὶ τὴν δοθεῖσαν εὐθεῖαν ἄπειρον ἀπὸ τοῦ δοθέντος σημείου, ὃ μή ἐστιν ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς, κάθετον εὐθεῖαν γραμμὴν ἀγαγεῖν.`,lat:`Ad datam rectam infinitam a dato puncto extra eam sito perpendicularem rectam lineam ducere.`,en:`Draw a perpendicular straight line to a given unbounded straight line from a given point not on it.`,greekPages:[34],latinPages:[35]},steps:[{title:`Take the line and an exterior point`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω ἡ μὲν δοθεῖσα εὐθεῖα ἄπειρος ἡ ΑΒ τὸ δὲ δοθὲν σημεῖον, ὃ μή ἐστιν ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς, τὸ Γ· δεῖ δὴ ἐπὶ τὴν δοθεῖσαν εὐθεῖαν ἄπειρον τὴν ΑΒ ἀπὸ τοῦ δοθέντος σημείου τοῦ Γ, ὃ μή ἐστιν ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς, κάθετον εὐθεῖαν γραμμὴν ἀγαγεῖν.`,lat:`Sit data recta infinita ΑΒ punctum autem datum extra eam situm Γ. oportet igitur ad datam rectam infinitam ΑΒ a dato puncto extra eam sito Γ perpendicularem rectam ducere.`,en:`Let ΑΒ be the given unbounded line and Γ the given point outside it. We must draw a perpendicular from Γ to ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[34],latinPages:[35],language:`ἄπειρον / infinitam means the line is not limited to the displayed endpoints. ὃ μή ἐστιν ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς is a relative clause: ‘which is not on it’. Latin compresses that condition to extra eam situm.`},{title:`Choose Δ on the other side`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`oppositeSide`,grc:`Εἰλήφθω γὰρ ἐπὶ τὰ ἕτερα μέρη τῆς ΑΒ εὐθείας τυχὸν σημεῖον τὸ Δ,`,lat:`sumatur enim in altera parte rectae ΑΒ quoduis punctum Δ,`,en:`Choose a point Δ on the other side of the straight line ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[34],latinPages:[35],language:`τυχὸν / quoduis means ‘any’, but the following placement restriction still applies. ἐπὶ τὰ ἕτερα μέρη / in altera parte puts Δ across the line from Γ. It does not mean anywhere in the plane.`},{title:`Draw the circle through Δ`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p3`,grc:`καὶ κέντρῳ μὲν τῷ Γ διαστήματι δὲ τῷ ΓΔ κύκλος γεγράφθω ὁ ΕΖΗ,`,lat:`et centro Γ radio autem ΓΔ circulus describatur ΕΖΗ,`,en:`With center Γ and distance ΓΔ, draw the circle ΕΖΗ. Its crossings with ΑΒ are Ε and Η.`,greekPages:[34],latinPages:[35],language:`κέντρῳ μὲν … διαστήματι δέ distinguishes center from distance. ΕΖΗ names the circle through points on it; Ζ is not another center. The English makes the two crossings explicit, as the source's next command and diagram require them.`},{title:`Construct the chord's midpoint`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`chordMidpoint`,grc:`καὶ τετμήσθω ἡ ΕΗ εὐθεῖα δίχα κατὰ τὸ Θ,`,lat:`et recta ΕΗ in duas partes aequales secetur in Θ,`,en:`Bisect the straight line ΕΗ at Θ.`,greekPages:[34],latinPages:[35],language:`τετμήσθω is a command to have the bisection completed. κατὰ τὸ Θ / in Θ names where it is made. I.10 supplies that construction; the foot is not selected by measuring a right angle.`},{title:`Join the radii and the proposed perpendicular`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p1`,grc:`καὶ ἐπεζεύχθωσαν αἱ ΓΗ, ΓΘ, ΓΕ εὐθεῖαι· λέγω, ὅτι ἐπὶ τὴν δοθεῖσαν εὐθεῖαν ἄπειρον τὴν ΑΒ ἀπὸ τοῦ δοθέντος σημείου τοῦ Γ, ὃ μή ἐστιν ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς, κάθετος ἧκται ἡ ΓΘ.`,lat:`et ducantur rectae ΓΗ, ΓΘ, ΓΕ. dico, ad datam rectam infinitam ΑΒ a dato puncto Γ extra eam sito perpendicularem ductam esse ΓΘ.`,en:`Join ΓΗ, ΓΘ and ΓΕ. I claim that ΓΘ is perpendicular to the given line ΑΒ from its exterior point Γ.`,greekPages:[34],latinPages:[35],language:`The plural command ἐπεζεύχθωσαν / ducantur joins three pairs of points. Two resulting lines are radii; ΓΘ is the line whose perpendicularity still needs proof.`},{title:`Use the constructed halves and common side`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`chordPairs`,grc:`Ἐπεὶ γὰρ ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΗΘ τῇ ΘΕ, κοινὴ δὲ ἡ ΘΓ, δύο δὴ αἱ ΗΘ, ΘΓ δύο ταῖς ΕΘ, ΘΓ ἴσαι εἰσὶν ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ·`,lat:`nam cum ΗΘ = ΘΕ, et communis sit ΘΓ, duae rectae ΗΘ, ΘΓ duabus ΕΘ, ΘΓ aequales sunt altera alteri.`,en:`ΗΘ equals ΘΕ, and ΘΓ is common. Thus ΗΘ and ΘΓ equal ΕΘ and ΘΓ respectively.`,greekPages:[34],latinPages:[35],language:`Heiberg prints δύο ταῖς here, with δυσί recorded in the apparatus for other witnesses; the main-text wording is retained. Latin duabus makes the comparison with the second pair explicit. The equality of the halves comes from I.10.`},{title:`Complete the three-side comparison`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`chordSSS`,grc:`καὶ βάσις ἡ ΓΗ βάσει τῇ ΓΕ ἐστιν ἴση· γωνία ἄρα ἡ ὑπὸ ΓΘΗ γωνίᾳ τῇ ὑπὸ ΕΘΓ ἐστιν ἴση. καί εἰσιν ἐφεξῆς.`,lat:`et basis ΓΗ basi ΓΕ aequalis est. itaque ∠ ΓΘΗ = ΕΘΓ. et deinceps positi sunt.`,en:`The base ΓΗ equals ΓΕ. Therefore angle ΓΘΗ equals angle ΕΘΓ, and these angles are adjacent.`,greekPages:[34],latinPages:[35],language:`The middle letter Θ identifies the common vertex of both angles. ΓΗ and ΓΕ are equal because they are radii, not because Θ was already assumed to be the perpendicular foot.`},{title:`Name the right angles and perpendicular`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`rightDefinition`,grc:`ὅταν δὲ εὐθεῖα ἐπ᾽ εὐθεῖαν σταθεῖσα τὰς ἐφεξῆς γωνίας ἴσας ἀλλήλαις ποιῇ, ὀρθὴ ἑκατέρα τῶν ἴσων γωνιῶν ἐστιν, καὶ ἡ ἐφεστηκυῖα εὐθεῖα κάθετος καλεῖται ἐφ᾽ ἣν ἐφέστηκεν.`,lat:`ubi autem recta super rectam lineam erecta angulos deinceps positos inter se aequales efficit, rectus est uterque angulus aequalis, et recta linea erecta perpendicularis adpellatur ad eam, super quam erecta est.`,en:`When a line standing on a line makes adjacent angles equal, each is right, and the standing line is called perpendicular to the line on which it stands.`,greekPages:[34],latinPages:[35],language:`καλεῖται / adpellatur means ‘is called’: the definition names a relationship now established. ἐφεστηκυῖα is the perfect participle ‘having stood upon’; Latin erecta plays the corresponding descriptive role.`},{title:`The perpendicular has been obtained`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`finish`,grc:`Ἐπὶ τὴν δοθεῖσαν ἄρα εὐθεῖαν ἄπειρον τὴν ΑΒ ἀπὸ τοῦ δοθέντος σημείου τοῦ Γ, ὃ μή ἐστιν ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς, κάθετος ἧκται ἡ ΓΘ· ὅπερ ἔδει ποιῆσαι.`,lat:`Ergo ad datam rectam infinitam ΑΒ a dato puncto Γ extra eam sito perpendicularis ducta est ΓΘ. quod oportebat fieri.`,en:`Thus ΓΘ has been drawn perpendicular to the given unbounded line ΑΒ from the given point Γ outside it. The requested construction is complete.`,greekPages:[34],latinPages:[35],language:`The conclusion repeats the exterior-point condition. Changing the auxiliary Δ changes the circle and chord; with the source's opposite-side choice, their constructed midpoint still yields the same perpendicular from Γ.`}],insight:{title:`‘Any point’ still has a condition`,text:`Move Δ while keeping it on the other side of ΑΒ from Γ. The radius and two intersections change, but the foot stays fixed. Deliberately move Δ to the same side: a circle can miss the line, touch it once, or still cross twice. Only two distinct intersections supply a nonzero chord for I.10. The source's placement condition guarantees that case.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI12.html`},question:{prompt:`Why does the instruction put Δ on the other side of ΑΒ?`,options:[`It ensures the circle through Δ crosses the line at two distinct points`,`It says Δ must be directly beneath Γ`,`It makes every same-side choice impossible`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. The radius must reach beyond the line, providing the two points needed for the chord and its bisection.`,`Δ may move sideways anywhere on the other side. It need not lie on the desired perpendicular.`,`Some same-side choices also cross twice. The source's restriction guarantees crossings without needing another test.`]}}},f={superposition:{title:`Superposition · an additional assumption`,text:`Euclid applies one triangle to the other while keeping its sides and angles unchanged. His five postulates do not explicitly license that operation. The movable plate illustrates a rigid motion in the real plane; it does not fill this axiomatic gap. Reflected correspondences need a reflection too.`,kind:`assumption`},noEnclosure:{title:`Two straight lines cannot enclose a region`,text:`Heiberg’s text explicitly rules out two different straight segments enclosing a region between the same endpoints. This is an additional straight-line uniqueness assumption. It is printed as common notion 9 in this edition; it is absent from the usual modern list of five common notions.`,kind:`assumption`},coincide:{title:`Common notion 4 · coincidence gives equality`,text:`Things that coincide with one another are equal. This is common notion 7 in Heiberg’s expanded list. Once the correspondence is justified, it applies to the base, the triangle and the remaining angles. Equal triangles here includes equality of area.`,kind:`deduction`},i3:{title:`I.3 · cut off an equal part`,text:`A shorter length can be cut off from a longer one. In I.5, extend ΑΕ far enough to exceed ΑΖ before cutting ΑΗ equal to ΑΖ. In I.6, the supposition ΑΒ > ΑΓ permits ΔΒ = ΑΓ, with Δ inside ΑΒ.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:3},i4:{title:`I.4 · two sides and their included angle`,text:`Match the vertices in order. Two pairs of equal sides and the equal angle between them give equal bases, equal triangles and corresponding remaining angles. An equal angle elsewhere would not supply this particular argument.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:4},commonAngle:{title:`A common angle and the given equal sides`,text:`ΑΖ and ΑΒ lie on the same ray; ΑΗ and ΑΓ lie on the other. Thus the larger triangles use the same angle at Α, even though the order of the two rays is reversed. A common angle is one object used twice.`,kind:`given`},subtractLengths:{title:`Common notion 3 · equal length remainders`,text:`From ΑΖ = ΑΗ subtract ΑΒ = ΑΓ. The remaining segments are ΒΖ and ΓΗ. They are needed for the second application of I.4, to the two triangles below the base.`,kind:`deduction`},subtractAngles:{title:`Common notion 3 · equal angle remainders`,text:`At Β, remove ∠ΓΒΗ from the whole ∠ΑΒΗ. At Γ, remove ∠ΒΓΖ from the whole ∠ΑΓΖ. The whole angles are equal by the first triangle comparison and the removed angles by the second. What remains is ∠ΑΒΓ = ∠ΑΓΒ.`,kind:`deduction`},trichotomy:{title:`Suppose the opposite · compare the lengths`,text:`If ΑΒ and ΑΓ are unequal, one is greater. Call the greater one ΑΒ; the other case is obtained by exchanging Β and Γ. Euclid uses this ordering of lengths without listing it among his five postulates. The extra inequality is temporary, not another given of the theorem.`,kind:`assumption`},hypotheticalSas:{title:`I.4 applied inside the temporary supposition`,text:`The cut supplies ΔΒ = ΑΓ; ΒΓ is common. Because Δ lies on ΒΑ, ∠ΔΒΓ is the given ∠ΑΒΓ, assumed equal to ∠ΑΓΒ. Thus I.4 would give equality of triangles ΔΒΓ and ΑΓΒ. The displayed scalene diagram cannot also satisfy the given angle equality.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:4},wholePart:{title:`Common notion 5 · the whole is greater than the part`,text:`Triangle ΔΒΓ is a proper part of ΑΓΒ, yet the supposition and I.4 have made them equal. Both claims cannot hold. Heiberg’s edition numbers the whole–part statement as common notion 8. The contradiction rejects the extra supposition of unequal sides.`,kind:`deduction`},theorem:{title:`The theorem is proved`,text:`The conclusion follows from the stated givens. ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι and quod erat demonstrandum close a proof: ‘which was to be shown’. Compare ποιῆσαι, ‘to make’, at the end of the first three construction problems.`,kind:`deduction`}},p={4:{id:4,numeral:`I.4`,greekNumeral:`δʹ`,title:`Match two sides and the angle between`,shortTitle:`Match triangles`,introduction:`Two lengths and their included angle determine the rest. Follow Euclid’s superposition argument, and inspect the assumption behind moving one triangle onto another.`,statement:{grc:`Ἐὰν δύο τρίγωνα τὰς δύο πλευρὰς [ταῖς] δυσὶ πλευραῖς ἴσας ἔχῃ ἑκατέραν ἑκατέρᾳ καὶ τὴν γωνίαν τῇ γωνίᾳ ἴσην ἔχῃ τὴν ὑπὸ τῶν ἴσων εὐθειῶν περιεχομένην, καὶ τὴν βάσιν τῇ βάσει ἴσην ἕξει, καὶ τὸ τρίγωνον τῷ τριγώνῳ ἴσον ἔσται, καὶ αἱ λοιπαὶ γωνίαι ταῖς λοιπαῖς γωνίαις ἴσαι ἔσονται ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ, ὑφ’ ἃς αἱ ἴσαι πλευραὶ ὑποτείνουσιν.`,lat:`Si duo trianguli duo latera duobus lateribus alterum alteri aequalia habent et angulos rectis aequalibus comprehensos aequales, etiam basim basi aequalem habebunt, et triangulus triangulo aequalis erit, et reliqui anguli reliquis aequales alter alteri, ii scilicet, sub quibus aequalia latera subtendunt.`,en:`If two triangles have two sides respectively equal to two sides, and the angles contained by the equal sides equal, they will also have equal bases; the triangles will be equal, and the remaining angles opposite the equal sides will be respectively equal.`,greekPages:[16],latinPages:[17]},steps:[{title:`Identify the three given equalities`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω δύο τρίγωνα τὰ ΑΒΓ, ΔΕΖ τὰς δύο πλευρὰς τὰς ΑΒ, ΑΓ ταῖς δυσὶ πλευραῖς ταῖς ΔΕ, ΔΖ ἴσας ἔχοντα ἑκατέραν ἑκατέρᾳ τὴν μὲν ΑΒ τῇ ΔΕ τὴν δὲ ΑΓ τῇ ΔΖ καὶ γωνίαν τὴν ὑπὸ ΒΑΓ γωνίᾳ τῇ ὑπὸ ΕΔΖ ἴσην.`,lat:`Sint duo trianguli ΑΒΓ, ΔΕΖ duo latera ΑΒ, ΑΓ duobus lateribus ΔΕ, ΔΖ aequalia habentes alterum alteri, ΑΒ = ΔΕ et ΑΓ = ΔΖ, et ∠ ΒΑΓ = ΕΔΖ.`,en:`Let ΑΒΓ and ΔΕΖ be two triangles with ΑΒ equal to ΔΕ, ΑΓ equal to ΔΖ, and the angle ΒΑΓ equal to ΕΔΖ.`,greekPages:[16],latinPages:[17],language:`ἑκατέραν ἑκατέρᾳ means ‘each to each’, here ‘respectively’. Latin alterum alteri makes the same pairing. In a three-letter angle name, the middle letter is its vertex: ΒΑΓ is at Α, not Β.`},{title:`State what still needs proof`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`given`,grc:`λέγω, ὅτι καὶ βάσις ἡ ΒΓ βάσει τῇ ΕΖ ἴση ἐστίν, καὶ τὸ ΑΒΓ τρίγωνον τῷ ΔΕΖ τριγώνῳ ἴσον ἔσται, καὶ αἱ λοιπαὶ γωνίαι ταῖς λοιπαῖς γωνίαις ἴσαι ἔσονται ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ, ὑφ’ ἃς αἱ ἴσαι πλευραὶ ὑποτείνουσιν, ἡ μὲν ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ τῇ ὑπὸ ΔΕΖ, ἡ δὲ ὑπὸ ΑΓΒ τῇ ὑπὸ ΔΖΕ.`,lat:`dico, etiam esse ΒΓ = ΕΖ et △ ΑΒΓ = ΔΕΖ, et reliquos angulos reliquis aequales alterum alteri, sub quibus aequalia latera subtendunt, ∠ ΑΒΓ = ΔΕΖ et ΑΓΒ = ΔΖΕ.`,en:`I say that ΒΓ equals ΕΖ, triangle ΑΒΓ equals triangle ΔΕΖ, and the remaining corresponding angles are equal: ΑΒΓ to ΔΕΖ, and ΑΓΒ to ΔΖΕ.`,greekPages:[16],latinPages:[17],language:`λέγω, ὅτι introduces the claim: ‘I say that’. Latin dico takes an infinitive clause, etiam esse. The future ἔσται (‘will be’) announces what the argument will establish; it is not a new given.`},{title:`Apply Α to Δ and ΑΒ to ΔΕ`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`superposition`,grc:`Ἐφαρμοζομένου γὰρ τοῦ ΑΒΓ τριγώνου ἐπὶ τὸ ΔΕΖ τρίγωνον καὶ τιθεμένου τοῦ μὲν Α σημείου ἐπὶ τὸ Δ σημεῖον τῆς δὲ ΑΒ εὐθείας ἐπὶ τὴν ΔΕ, ἐφαρμόσει καὶ τὸ Β σημεῖον ἐπὶ τὸ Ε διὰ τὸ ἴσην εἶναι τὴν ΑΒ τῇ ΔΕ·`,lat:`Nam si triangulum ΑΒΓ triangulo ΔΕΖ adplicuerimus et punctum Α in Δ puncto posuerimus, rectam autem ΑΒ in ΔΕ, etiam Β punctum in Ε cadet, quia ΑΒ = ΔΕ.`,en:`For if triangle ΑΒΓ is applied to triangle ΔΕΖ, with Α placed on Δ and ΑΒ on ΔΕ, then Β also coincides with Ε, because ΑΒ equals ΔΕ.`,greekPages:[16,18],latinPages:[17,19],language:`Ἐφαρμοζομένου … τοῦ … τριγώνου is a genitive absolute: ‘when the triangle is applied’. Latin recasts it as si … adplicuerimus, ‘if we have applied’. Notice the shift from Greek passive phrasing to Latin ‘we’.`},{title:`Use the included angle to align the next side`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`superposition`,grc:`ἐφαρμοσάσης δὴ τῆς ΑΒ ἐπὶ τὴν ΔΕ ἐφαρμόσει καὶ ἡ ΑΓ εὐθεῖα ἐπὶ τὴν ΔΖ διὰ τὸ ἴσην εἶναι τὴν ὑπὸ ΒΑΓ γωνίαν τῇ ὑπὸ ΕΔΖ·`,lat:`adplicata iam ΑΒ rectae ΔΕ etiam ΑΓ recta cum ΔΖ congruet, quia ∠ ΒΑΓ = ΕΔΖ.`,en:`With ΑΒ applied to ΔΕ, the straight line ΑΓ will also fall on ΔΖ, because angle ΒΑΓ equals angle ΕΔΖ.`,greekPages:[18],latinPages:[19],language:`διὰ τὸ … εἶναι means ‘because of … being’. The article τὸ turns the infinitive clause into a noun-like expression. Latin uses the finite clause quia, ‘because’. congruet here means ‘will coincide’.`},{title:`The second length fixes the third vertex`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`superposition`,grc:`ὥστε καὶ τὸ Γ σημεῖον ἐπὶ τὸ Ζ σημεῖον ἐφαρμόσει διὰ τὸ ἴσην πάλιν εἶναι τὴν ΑΓ τῇ ΔΖ.`,lat:`quare etiam punctum Γ in Ζ punctum cadet, quia rursus ΑΓ = ΔΖ.`,en:`Consequently Γ also coincides with Ζ, since ΑΓ again equals ΔΖ.`,greekPages:[18],latinPages:[19],language:`ὥστε marks a consequence, like Latin quare. πάλιν / rursus (‘again’) recalls the second given length equality. Equal direction alone would not fix the endpoint without this length.`},{title:`The bases must coincide`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`noEnclosure`,grc:`ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ τὸ Β ἐπὶ τὸ Ε ἐφηρμόκει· ὥστε βάσις ἡ ΒΓ ἐπὶ βάσιν τὴν ΕΖ ἐφαρμόσει. εἰ γὰρ τοῦ μὲν Β ἐπὶ τὸ Ε ἐφαρμόσαντος τοῦ δὲ Γ ἐπὶ τὸ Ζ ἡ ΒΓ βάσις ἐπὶ τὴν ΕΖ οὐκ ἐφαρμόσει, δύο εὐθεῖαι χωρίον περιέξουσιν· ὅπερ ἐστὶν ἀδύνατον. ἐφαρμόσει ἄρα ἡ ΒΓ βάσις ἐπὶ τὴν ΕΖ καὶ ἴση αὐτῇ ἔσται·`,lat:`uerum etiam Β in Ε ceciderat; quare basis ΒΓ in basim ΕΖ cadet. nam, cum Β in Ε, Γ uero in Ζ ceciderit, si ita basis ΒΓ cum ΕΖ non congruet, duae rectae spatium comprehendent; quod fieri non potest. itaque basis ΒΓ cum ΕΖ congruet et aequalis ei erit.`,en:`But Β had already coincided with Ε, so base ΒΓ coincides with ΕΖ. If their endpoints coincided but the bases did not, two straight lines would enclose a region, which is impossible. The bases therefore coincide and are equal.`,greekPages:[18],latinPages:[19],language:`ἐφηρμόκει and ceciderat are pluperfects: the earlier endpoint match ‘had’ happened. ἀδύνατον / fieri non potest marks impossibility. This extra straight-line argument is present in Heiberg’s text and is kept here.`},{title:`Read off the remaining equalities`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`coincide`,grc:`ὥστε καὶ ὅλον τὸ ΑΒΓ τρίγωνον ἐπὶ ὅλον τὸ ΔΕΖ τρίγωνον ἐφαρμόσει καὶ ἴσον αὐτῷ ἔσται, καὶ αἱ λοιπαὶ γωνίαι ἐπὶ τὰς λοιπὰς γωνίας ἐφαρμόσουσι καὶ ἴσαι αὐταῖς ἔσονται, ἡ μὲν ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ τῇ ὑπὸ ΔΕΖ ἡ δὲ ὑπὸ ΑΓΒ τῇ ὑπὸ ΔΖΕ.`,lat:`quare etiam totus triangulus ΑΒΓ cum toto triangulo ΔΕΖ congruet et ei aequalis erit, et reliqui anguli cum reliquis congruent et aequales iis erunt, ∠ ΑΒΓ = ΔΕΖ et ∠ ΑΓΒ = ΔΖΕ.`,en:`The whole triangle ΑΒΓ therefore coincides with the whole triangle ΔΕΖ and is equal to it. The remaining angles also coincide and are equal: ΑΒΓ with ΔΕΖ, and ΑΓΒ with ΔΖΕ.`,greekPages:[18],latinPages:[19],language:`ὅλον / totus says ‘whole’. λοιπαί / reliqui means ‘remaining’: these angles were not among the givens. The repeated μὲν … δὲ pairs one angle with its counterpart, then the other.`},{title:`Close the theorem`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`theorem`,grc:`Ἐὰν ἄρα δύο τρίγωνα τὰς δύο πλευρὰς [ταῖς] δύο πλευραῖς ἴσας ἔχῃ ἑκατέραν ἑκατέρᾳ καὶ τὴν γωνίαν τῇ γωνίᾳ ἴσην ἔχῃ τὴν ὑπὸ τῶν ἴσων εὐθειῶν περιεχομένην, καὶ τὴν βάσιν τῇ βάσει ἴσην ἕξει, καὶ τὸ τρίγωνον τῷ τριγώνῳ ἴσον ἔσται, καὶ αἱ λοιπαὶ γωνίαι ταῖς λοιπαῖς γωνίαις ἴσαι ἔσονται ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ, ὑφ’ ἃς αἱ ἴσαι πλευραὶ ὑποτείνουσιν· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo si duo trianguli duo latera duobus lateribus alterum alteri aequalia habent et angulos rectis aequalibus comprehensos aequales, etiam basim basi aequalem habebunt, et triangulus triangulo aequalis erit, et reliqui anguli reliquis aequales alter alteri, ii scilicet, sub quibus aequalia latera subtendunt; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Therefore two triangles with two respectively equal sides and equal included angles also have equal bases, equal triangles and equal remaining corresponding angles. Which was to be shown.`,greekPages:[18],latinPages:[19],language:`The closing δεῖξαι (‘to show’) replaces ποιῆσαι (‘to make’) from I.1–3. Latin quod erat demonstrandum is the source of Q.E.D. The square brackets around [ταῖς] belong to Heiberg’s edition.`}],insight:{title:`Can a proof move a triangle?`,text:`The slider preserves every length by a calculated rotation and translation. That makes the correspondence visible. Euclid’s text, however, has not postulated such movements or justified why they preserve the figure. Treat the motion as an explanation of his argument, with its assumption exposed. Equal area alone would not give the three angle and side correspondences proved here.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI4.html`},question:{prompt:`Which angle must accompany the two given equal sides in I.4?`,options:[`The angle between those two sides`,`Any angle of the triangle`,`Only an angle opposite the longer side`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. The included angle aligns the second side after the first side is placed.`,`The placement argument uses the included angle specifically. An arbitrary equal angle is insufficient for this argument.`,`The theorem does not select an angle by the length opposite it. It uses the angle between the two given sides.`]}},5:{id:5,numeral:`I.5`,greekNumeral:`εʹ`,title:`Cross the isosceles triangle’s bridge`,shortTitle:`Prove equal angles`,introduction:`Equal sides give equal base angles. Euclid reaches them through two pairs of overlapping triangles and an angle subtraction, proving the exterior angles equal along the way.`,statement:{grc:`Τῶν ἰσοσκελῶν τριγώνων αἱ πρὸς τῇ βάσει γωνίαι ἴσαι ἀλλήλαις εἰσίν, καὶ προσεκβληθεισῶν τῶν ἴσων εὐθειῶν αἱ ὑπὸ τὴν βάσιν γωνίαι ἴσαι ἀλλήλαις ἔσονται.`,lat:`In triangulis aequicruriis anguli ad basim positi inter se aequales sunt, et productis rectis aequalibus anguli sub basi positi inter se aequales erunt.`,en:`In isosceles triangles the angles at the base are equal to one another; when the equal sides are extended, the angles below the base will also be equal to one another.`,greekPages:[20],latinPages:[21]},steps:[{title:`Begin with equal sides and extend them`,phase:`Given`,reason:`p2`,grc:`Ἔστω τρίγωνον ἰσοσκελὲς τὸ ΑΒΓ ἴσην ἔχον τὴν ΑΒ πλευρὰν τῇ ΑΓ πλευρᾷ, καὶ προσεκβεβλήσθωσαν ἐπ’ εὐθείας ταῖς ΑΒ, ΑΓ εὐθεῖαι αἱ ΒΔ, ΓΕ·`,lat:`Sit triangulus aequicrurius ΑΒΓ habens ΑΒ = ΑΓ, et producantur ΑΒ, ΑΓ in directum, ut fiant ΒΔ, ΓΕ.`,en:`Let ΑΒΓ be an isosceles triangle with side ΑΒ equal to ΑΓ. Let ΒΔ and ΓΕ continue ΑΒ and ΑΓ in straight lines.`,greekPages:[20],latinPages:[21],language:`ἰσοσκελής combines ‘equal’ with σκέλος, ‘leg’. Latin aequicrurius likewise combines equal with crus, ‘leg’. The instruction extends the legs beyond Β and Γ; the apex remains Α.`},{title:`There are two angle claims`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`given`,grc:`λέγω, ὅτι ἡ μὲν ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ γωνία τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΓΒ ἴση ἐστίν, ἡ δὲ ὑπὸ ΓΒΔ τῇ ὑπὸ ΒΓΕ.`,lat:`dico, esse ∠ ΑΒΓ = ΑΓΒ et ∠ ΓΒΔ = ΒΓΕ.`,en:`I say that angle ΑΒΓ equals ΑΓΒ, and angle ΓΒΔ equals ΒΓΕ.`,greekPages:[20],latinPages:[21],language:`μὲν … δὲ divides the claim into two parts. πρὸς τῇ βάσει in the heading means ‘at the base’; ὑπὸ τὴν βάσιν means ‘below the base’. The prepositions distinguish the interior and exterior angles.`},{title:`Choose Ζ, cut ΑΗ and join the cross-lines`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`i3`,grc:`εἰλήφθω γὰρ ἐπὶ τῆς ΒΔ τυχὸν σημεῖον τὸ Ζ, καὶ ἀφῃρήσθω ἀπὸ τῆς μείζονος τῆς ΑΕ τῇ ἐλάσσονι τῇ ΑΖ ἴση ἡ ΑΗ, καὶ ἐπεζεύχθωσαν αἱ ΖΓ, ΗΒ εὐθεῖαι.`,lat:`Sumatur enim in ΒΔ quoduis punctum Ζ, et a maiore ΑΕ minori ΑΖ aequalis abscindatur ΑΗ, et ducantur ΖΓ, ΗΒ rectae.`,en:`Take any point Ζ on ΒΔ. From ΑΕ, the greater, cut off ΑΗ equal to the lesser ΑΖ, and join ΖΓ and ΗΒ.`,greekPages:[20],latinPages:[21],language:`τυχὸν σημεῖον / quoduis punctum means ‘any point’. The next clause still requires ΑΕ to be longer than ΑΖ. The plate extends ΑΕ far enough; changing Ζ is not permission to ignore that hypothesis.`},{title:`Match the two larger triangles`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`commonAngle`,grc:`ἐπεὶ οὖν ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ μὲν ΑΖ τῇ ΑΗ ἡ δὲ ΑΒ τῇ ΑΓ, δύο δὴ αἱ ΖΑ, ΑΓ δυσὶ ταῖς ΗΑ, ΑΒ ἴσαι εἰσὶν ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ· καὶ γωνίαν κοινὴν περιέχουσι τὴν ὑπὸ ΖΑΗ·`,lat:`iam quoniam ΑΖ = ΑΗ et ΑΒ = ΑΓ, duae rectae ΖΑ, ΑΓ duabus ΗΑ, ΑΒ aequales sunt altera alteri; et angulum communem comprehendunt ΖΑΗ.`,en:`Since ΑΖ equals ΑΗ and ΑΒ equals ΑΓ, the pairs ΖΑ, ΑΓ and ΗΑ, ΑΒ are respectively equal. They contain the common angle ΖΑΗ.`,greekPages:[20],latinPages:[21],language:`κοινή / communis means ‘common’ or ‘shared’. Read the triangle names in correspondence: ΑΖΓ matches ΑΗΒ, so Α matches Α, Ζ matches Η, and Γ matches Β. The order carries the proof.`},{title:`Use I.4 for the larger pair`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`i4`,grc:`βάσις ἄρα ἡ ΖΓ βάσει τῇ ΗΒ ἴση ἐστίν, καὶ τὸ ΑΖΓ τρίγωνον τῷ ΑΗΒ τριγώνῳ ἴσον ἔσται, καὶ αἱ λοιπαὶ γωνίαι ταῖς λοιπαῖς γωνίαις ἴσαι ἔσονται ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ, ὑφ’ ἃς αἱ ἴσαι πλευραὶ ὑποτείνουσιν, ἡ μὲν ὑπὸ ΑΓΖ τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΒΗ, ἡ δὲ ὑπὸ ΑΖΓ τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΗΒ.`,lat:`itaque ΖΓ = ΗΒ et △ ΑΖΓ = ΑΗΒ, et reliqui anguli reliquis aequales erunt alter alteri, sub quibus aequalia latera subtendunt, ∠ ΑΓΖ = ΑΒΗ et ∠ ΑΖΓ = ΑΗΒ.`,en:`Therefore ΖΓ equals ΗΒ, and triangle ΑΖΓ equals ΑΗΒ. The remaining corresponding angles are equal: ΑΓΖ to ΑΒΗ, and ΑΖΓ to ΑΗΒ.`,greekPages:[20],latinPages:[21],language:`βάσις (‘base’) here names ΖΓ and ΗΒ, not the original base ΒΓ. A triangle’s base is the side selected for that comparison; it need not be horizontal or below the other sides.`},{title:`Subtract the equal upper lengths`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`subtractLengths`,grc:`καὶ ἐπεὶ ὅλη ἡ ΑΖ ὅλῃ τῇ ΑΗ ἐστιν ἴση, ὧν ἡ ΑΒ τῇ ΑΓ ἐστιν ἴση, λοιπὴ ἄρα ἡ ΒΖ λοιπῇ τῇ ΓΗ ἐστιν ἴση.`,lat:`et quoniam ΑΖ = ΑΗ, quarum partes ΑΒ, ΑΓ aequales, erit ΒΖ = ΓΗ.`,en:`Since the whole ΑΖ equals the whole ΑΗ, and the parts ΑΒ and ΑΓ are equal, the remaining ΒΖ equals the remaining ΓΗ.`,greekPages:[20],latinPages:[21],language:`ὧν (‘of which’) links the parts to the previously named wholes. Heiberg’s Latin makes ‘parts’ explicit with partes. λοιπή identifies what remains after the subtraction, as it did in I.2.`},{title:`Match the two lower triangles`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`i4`,grc:`ἐδείχθη δὲ καὶ ἡ ΖΓ τῇ ΗΒ ἴση· δύο δὴ αἱ ΒΖ, ΖΓ δυσὶ ταῖς ΓΗ, ΗΒ ἴσαι εἰσὶν ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ· καὶ γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΖΓ γωνίᾳ τῇ ὑπὸ ΓΗΒ ἴση, καὶ βάσις αὐτῶν κοινὴ ἡ ΒΓ·`,lat:`sed demonstratum est etiam ΖΓ = ΗΒ. itaque duae rectae ΒΖ, ΖΓ duabus ΓΗ, ΗΒ aequales sunt altera alteri; et ∠ ΒΖΓ = ΓΗΒ et basis eorum communis ΒΓ.`,en:`ΖΓ was also proved equal to ΗΒ. Thus ΒΖ, ΖΓ equal ΓΗ, ΗΒ respectively, angle ΒΖΓ equals ΓΗΒ, and their base ΒΓ is common.`,greekPages:[20,22],latinPages:[21,23],language:`ἐδείχθη (‘was shown’) retrieves a result from the first triangle comparison. The angle at Ζ can be named ΑΖΓ or ΒΖΓ because Α and Β lie on the same ray from Ζ. The same applies at Η.`},{title:`Use I.4 again below the base`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`i4`,grc:`καὶ τὸ ΒΖΓ ἄρα τρίγωνον τῷ ΓΗΒ τριγώνῳ ἴσον ἔσται, καὶ αἱ λοιπαὶ γωνίαι ταῖς λοιπαῖς γωνίαις ἴσαι ἔσονται ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ, ὑφ’ ἃς αἱ ἴσαι πλευραὶ ὑποτείνουσιν· ἴση ἄρα ἐστὶν ἡ μὲν ὑπὸ ΖΒΓ τῇ ὑπὸ ΗΓΒ ἡ δὲ ὑπὸ ΒΓΖ τῇ ὑπὸ ΓΒΗ.`,lat:`itaque etiam △ ΒΖΓ = ΓΗΒ, et reliqui anguli reliquis aequales erunt alter alteri, sub quibus aequalia latera subtendunt. itaque ∠ ΖΒΓ = ΗΓΒ et ΒΓΖ = ΓΒΗ.`,en:`The triangles ΒΖΓ and ΓΗΒ are therefore equal, with their remaining corresponding angles equal. Hence ΖΒΓ equals ΗΓΒ, and ΒΓΖ equals ΓΒΗ.`,greekPages:[22],latinPages:[23],language:`ἄρα / itaque marks the conclusion from I.4. The new correspondence is Β ↔ Γ, Ζ ↔ Η, Γ ↔ Β. A repeated point can change its partner when the compared triangles change.`},{title:`Subtract equal angles to expose the base angles`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`subtractAngles`,grc:`ἐπεὶ οὖν ὅλη ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΒΗ γωνία ὅλῃ τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΓΖ γωνίᾳ ἐδείχθη ἴση, ὧν ἡ ὑπὸ ΓΒΗ τῇ ὑπὸ ΒΓΖ ἴση, λοιπὴ ἄρα ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ λοιπῇ τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΓΒ ἐστιν ἴση· καί εἰσι πρὸς τῇ βάσει τοῦ ΑΒΓ τριγώνου.`,lat:`iam quoniam ∠ ΑΒΗ = ΑΓΖ, ut demonstratum est, quorum partes ΓΒΗ, ΒΓΖ aequales, erit ∠ ΑΒΓ = ΑΓΒ. et sunt ad basim positi trianguli ΑΒΓ.`,en:`The whole angles ΑΒΗ and ΑΓΖ were proved equal, and their parts ΓΒΗ and ΒΓΖ are equal. The remaining angles ΑΒΓ and ΑΓΒ are therefore equal; these are at the base of triangle ΑΒΓ.`,greekPages:[22],latinPages:[23],language:`The syntax repeats the length subtraction: whole, equal parts, remaining. Here ὅλη and λοιπή describe angles. Common notion 3 can compare magnitudes other than lengths.`},{title:`Recover the angles below the base`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`i4`,grc:`ἐδείχθη δὲ καὶ ἡ ὑπὸ ΖΒΓ τῇ ὑπὸ ΗΓΒ ἴση· καί εἰσιν ὑπὸ τὴν βάσιν.`,lat:`uerum etiam demonstratum est ∠ ΖΒΓ = ΗΓΒ; et sub basi sunt.`,en:`Angle ΖΒΓ was also proved equal to ΗΓΒ; these are below the base.`,greekPages:[22],latinPages:[23],language:`The second claim is already available: ἐδείχθη / demonstratum est (‘was proved’). Ζ and Δ lie on the same ray from Β; Η and Ε lie on the same ray from Γ. Thus these are the exterior angles named at the start.`},{title:`Both angle claims are established`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`theorem`,grc:`Τῶν ἄρα ἰσοσκελῶν τριγώνων αἱ πρὸς τῇ βάσει γωνίαι ἴσαι ἀλλήλαις εἰσίν, καὶ προσεκβληθεισῶν τῶν ἴσων εὐθειῶν αἱ ὑπὸ τὴν βάσιν γωνίαι ἴσαι ἀλλήλαις ἔσονται· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo in triangulis aequicruriis anguli ad basim positi inter se aequales sunt, et productis rectis aequalibus anguli sub basi positi inter se aequales erunt; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Therefore an isosceles triangle has equal angles at its base, and extending its equal sides gives equal angles below the base. Which was to be shown.`,greekPages:[22],latinPages:[23],language:`προσεκβληθεισῶν τῶν ἴσων εὐθειῶν is a genitive absolute, ‘when the equal lines have been extended’. Latin productis rectis aequalibus is an ablative absolute. Both compress a condition into a participial phrase.`}],insight:{title:`Why all the extra lines?`,text:`The proof needs both interior and exterior angle equalities using tools already earned. The first comparison supplies equal whole angles; the second supplies equal parts to subtract. A later theorem about angles on a straight line would shorten the argument, but using it here would reverse Euclid’s dependency order. Follow the two correspondences before looking at the final symmetry.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI5.html`},question:{prompt:`At the last subtraction, what does the second triangle comparison provide?`,options:[`The equal small angles removed from the equal whole angles`,`The original given equality ΑΒ = ΑΓ`,`A measurement of the base angles`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. The first comparison gives equal whole angles; the second gives equal parts. Subtraction leaves the base angles equal.`,`ΑΒ = ΑΓ is given at the beginning. The lower triangles provide the angle parts needed later.`,`The proof never measures an angle. Its conclusion follows by subtracting equal parts from equal wholes.`]}},6:{id:6,numeral:`I.6`,greekNumeral:`ϛʹ`,title:`Reject an impossible inequality`,shortTitle:`Reverse the theorem`,introduction:`Now equal angles are given and equal sides must be proved. Euclid supposes unequal sides and forces a proper part to equal its whole. Learn to read the contradiction without mistaking its diagram for a possible triangle.`,statement:{grc:`Ἐὰν τριγώνου αἱ δύο γωνίαι ἴσαι ἀλλήλαις ὦσιν, καὶ αἱ ὑπὸ τὰς ἴσας γωνίας ὑποτείνουσαι πλευραὶ ἴσαι ἀλλήλαις ἔσονται.`,lat:`Si in triangulo duo anguli inter se aequales sunt, etiam latera sub aequalibus angulis subtendentia inter se aequalia erunt.`,en:`If two angles of a triangle are equal to one another, the sides opposite those equal angles will also be equal to one another.`,greekPages:[22],latinPages:[23]},steps:[{title:`Give the equal angles; claim the equal sides`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω τρίγωνον τὸ ΑΒΓ ἴσην ἔχον τὴν ὑπὸ ΑΒΓ γωνίαν τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΓΒ γωνίᾳ· λέγω, ὅτι καὶ πλευρὰ ἡ ΑΒ πλευρᾷ τῇ ΑΓ ἐστιν ἴση.`,lat:`Sit triangulus ΑΒΓ habens ∠ ΑΒΓ = ΑΓΒ. dico, esse etiam ΑΒ = ΑΓ.`,en:`Let ΑΒΓ be a triangle with angle ΑΒΓ equal to ΑΓΒ. I say that side ΑΒ also equals ΑΓ.`,greekPages:[22],latinPages:[23],language:`The same nouns now exchange logical roles: γωνία (‘angle’) belongs to the given, πλευρά (‘side’) to the claim. This reverses the first conclusion of I.5; a converse requires its own proof.`},{title:`Suppose the sides are unequal`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`trichotomy`,grc:`εἰ γὰρ ἄνισός ἐστιν ἡ ΑΒ τῇ ΑΓ, ἡ ἑτέρα αὐτῶν μείζων ἐστίν. ἔστω μείζων ἡ ΑΒ,`,lat:`Si enim ΑΒ rectae ΑΓ inaequalis est, alterutra earum maior est. sit ΑΒ maior,`,en:`For if ΑΒ is unequal to ΑΓ, one of them is greater. Let ΑΒ be the greater.`,greekPages:[22],latinPages:[23],language:`εἰ / si (‘if’) opens a temporary supposition. The prefix ἀν- in ἄνισος and in- in inaequalis negate equality. The new ἔστω does not replace the original equal-angle given.`},{title:`Cut ΔΒ equal to ΑΓ and join ΔΓ`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`i3`,grc:`καὶ ἀφῃρήσθω ἀπὸ τῆς μείζονος τῆς ΑΒ τῇ ἐλάττονι τῇ ΑΓ ἴση ἡ ΔΒ, καὶ ἐπεζεύχθω ἡ ΔΓ.`,lat:`et a maiore ΑΒ minori ΑΓ aequalis abscindatur ΔΒ, et ducatur ΔΓ.`,en:`From the greater ΑΒ cut off ΔΒ equal to the lesser ΑΓ, and join ΔΓ.`,greekPages:[22],latinPages:[23],language:`ΔΒ begins at Β: Δ lies inside ΑΒ. The source here uses ἐλάττονι, a variant of the ἐλάσσονι seen in I.3 and I.5. The cutting instruction has the same grammar and invokes the same earlier construction.`},{title:`Apply I.4 under the supposition`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`hypotheticalSas`,grc:`Ἐπεὶ οὖν ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΔΒ τῇ ΑΓ κοινὴ δὲ ἡ ΒΓ, δύο δὴ αἱ ΔΒ, ΒΓ δύο ταῖς ΑΓ, ΓΒ ἴσαι εἰσὶν ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ, καὶ γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΔΒΓ γωνίᾳ τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΓΒ ἐστιν ἴση·`,lat:`iam cum ΔΒ = ΑΓ, et ΒΓ communis sit, duae rectae ΔΒ, ΒΓ duabus ΑΓ, ΓΒ aequales sunt altera alteri, et ∠ ΔΒΓ = ΑΓΒ.`,en:`Since ΔΒ equals ΑΓ and ΒΓ is common, the pairs ΔΒ, ΒΓ and ΑΓ, ΓΒ are respectively equal. Angle ΔΒΓ also equals ΑΓΒ by the given angle equality.`,greekPages:[24],latinPages:[25],language:`κοινὴ δὲ ἡ ΒΓ leaves ‘is’ understood: ‘and ΒΓ [is] common’. Latin writes communis sit. The angle equality is carried from the theorem’s given into the hypothetical comparison; it is not a measurement of the sketch.`},{title:`A proper part would equal its whole`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`wholePart`,grc:`βάσις ἄρα ἡ ΔΓ βάσει τῇ ΑΒ ἴση ἐστίν, καὶ τὸ ΔΒΓ τρίγωνον τῷ ΑΓΒ τριγώνῳ ἴσον ἔσται, τὸ ἔλασσον τῷ μείζονι· ὅπερ ἄτοπον·`,lat:`itaque ΔΓ = ΑΒ et △ ΔΒΓ = ΑΓΒ, minus maiori; quod absurdum est.`,en:`Thus ΔΓ would equal ΑΒ, and triangle ΔΒΓ would equal ΑΓΒ: the lesser equal to the greater. That is absurd.`,greekPages:[24],latinPages:[25],language:`τὸ ἔλασσον τῷ μείζονι is compressed: ‘the lesser [equal] to the greater’. Latin minus maiori is equally brief. ἄτοπον (‘out of place; absurd’) signals the contradiction, not an approximate mismatch.`},{title:`Discharge the extra assumption`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`wholePart`,grc:`οὐκ ἄρα ἄνισός ἐστιν ἡ ΑΒ τῇ ΑΓ· ἴση ἄρα.`,lat:`itaque ΑΒ rectae ΑΓ inaequalis non est; aequalis igitur.`,en:`Therefore ΑΒ is not unequal to ΑΓ. It is therefore equal.`,greekPages:[24],latinPages:[25],language:`οὐκ … ἄνισος / inaequalis non est literally denies inequality. Then ἴση ἄρα / aequalis igitur gives the positive conclusion in just two words. The temporary supposition has been rejected.`},{title:`The converse is proved`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`theorem`,grc:`Ἐὰν ἄρα τριγώνου αἱ δύο γωνίαι ἴσαι ἀλλήλαις ὦσιν, καὶ αἱ ὑπὸ τὰς ἴσας γωνίας ὑποτείνουσαι πλευραὶ ἴσαι ἀλλήλαις ἔσονται· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo si in triangulo duo anguli inter se aequales sunt, etiam latera sub aequalibus angulis subtendentia inter se aequalia erunt; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Therefore, if two angles of a triangle are equal, the sides opposite those angles are also equal. Which was to be shown.`,greekPages:[24],latinPages:[25],language:`Ἐὰν … ὦσιν uses the subjunctive ‘if … are’ for a general condition, followed by future ἔσονται (‘will be’). Latin uses si … sunt followed by erunt. The heading’s conditional structure returns at the conclusion.`}],insight:{title:`An impossible picture can still explain an argument`,text:`The middle steps use a scalene sketch to locate Δ and distinguish a part from its whole. Such a sketch cannot also have the two equal base angles required by the theorem. The plate labels that incompatibility and shows actual measured angles separately. The proof rejects the supposition of unequal sides; no impossible triangle has been constructed.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI6.html`},question:{prompt:`Which claim does the contradiction reject?`,options:[`The temporary supposition that the sides are unequal`,`The original given that the base angles are equal`,`The rule that a whole is greater than its proper part`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. Retain the given equal angles and reject the added inequality. The opposite sides must be equal.`,`Equal base angles are the theorem’s hypothesis. The proof introduces unequal sides temporarily and rejects that addition.`,`The whole–part rule exposes the impossibility. It is not discarded to preserve the supposition.`]}}},m={i5:{title:`I.5 · equal sides give equal base angles`,text:`Use the triangle named in this sentence. ΑΓ = ΑΔ gives equal angles in ΑΓΔ. The second application, to ΓΒΔ, is conditional on ΓΒ = ΔΒ, which the distinct same-side sketch cannot also satisfy.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:5},anglePart:{title:`Whole and part in the pictured configuration`,text:`Here ∠ΔΓΒ is a proper part of ∠ΑΓΔ. I.5 made ∠ΑΓΔ equal to ∠ΑΔΓ, so ∠ΑΔΓ is greater than ∠ΔΓΒ. This uses the drawn placement of Δ outside ΑΒΓ and compatibility of equality with order. The printed proof does not discuss every possible placement.`,kind:`assumption`},angleOrder:{title:`Order passes through the two comparisons`,text:`In this configuration ∠ΓΔΒ contains ∠ΑΔΓ, which was already greater than ∠ΔΓΒ. Thus ∠ΓΔΒ is greater still. Transitivity of strict comparison is used here without being separately stated in the usual five common notions.`,kind:`assumption`},impossibleOrder:{title:`The same two angles cannot be both equal and unequal`,text:`The first radius equality and the pictured placement give ∠ΓΔΒ > ∠ΔΓΒ. The added equality ΓΒ = ΔΒ would give ∠ΓΔΒ = ∠ΔΓΒ by I.5. The conjunction is impossible. A reflected point escapes this contradiction by failing the same-side hypothesis.`,kind:`deduction`},i7:{title:`I.7 · one meeting point on the same side`,text:`After the equal bases have been aligned, two distinct meeting points on the same side cannot have the same two distances from the corresponding base endpoints. The side condition matters: the two circle intersections on opposite sides are both possible.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:7},sssCoincidence:{title:`I.7 excludes the second apex; coincidence gives equality`,text:`With Β on Ε and Γ on Ζ, the remaining vertex must fall on Δ on the selected side. The angle then coincides with ∠ΕΔΖ and is equal to it. The source still relies on superposition, as I.4 did; the three numerical side checks do not justify that assumption.`,kind:`deduction`}},h={7:{id:7,numeral:`I.7`,greekNumeral:`ζʹ`,title:`Test why the same side matters`,shortTitle:`Locate one apex`,introduction:`Two distances locate a meeting point on one side of a base. Follow the pictured case, then reflect the point to see exactly which condition the second solution fails.`,statement:{grc:`Ἐπὶ τῆς αὐτῆς εὐθείας δύο ταῖς αὐταῖς εὐθείαις ἄλλαι δύο εὐθεῖαι ἴσαι ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ οὐ συσταθήσονται πρὸς ἄλλῳ καὶ ἄλλῳ σημείῳ ἐπὶ τὰ αὐτὰ μέρη τὰ αὐτὰ πέρατα ἔχουσαι ταῖς ἐξ ἀρχῆς εὐθείαις.`,lat:`In eadem recta iisdem duabus rectis aliae duae rectae aequales altera alteri non constituentur ad aliud atque aliud punctum ad eandem partem eosdem terminos, quos priores rectae, habentes.`,en:`On the same straight line, two further straight lines respectively equal to the original two cannot be set up to meet at a different point on the same side, with each starting from the same endpoint as its counterpart.`,greekPages:[24],latinPages:[25]},steps:[{title:`Suppose there is a second meeting point`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Εἰ γὰρ δυνατόν, ἐπὶ τῆς αὐτῆς εὐθείας τῆς ΑΒ δύο ταῖς αὐταῖς εὐθείαις ταῖς ΑΓ, ΓΒ ἄλλαι δύο εὐθεῖαι αἱ ΑΔ, ΔΒ ἴσαι ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ συνεστάτωσαν πρὸς ἄλλῳ καὶ ἄλλῳ σημείῳ τῷ τε Γ καὶ Δ ἐπὶ τὰ αὐτὰ μέρη τὰ αὐτὰ πέρατα ἔχουσαι, ὥστε ἴσην εἶναι τὴν μὲν ΓΑ τῇ ΔΑ τὸ αὐτὸ πέρας ἔχουσαν αὐτῇ τὸ Α, τὴν δὲ ΓΒ τῇ ΔΒ τὸ αὐτὸ πέρας ἔχουσαν αὐτῇ τὸ Β,`,lat:`Nam si fieri potest, in eadem recta ΑΒ duabus iisdem rectis ΑΓ, ΓΒ aliae duae rectae ΑΔ, ΔΒ aequales altera alteri constituantur ad aliud atque aliud punctum Γ et Δ ad eandem partem eosdem terminos habentes, ita ut ΓΑ = ΔΑ, quacum terminum habet communem Α, et ΓΒ = ΔΒ, quacum terminum habet communem Β,`,en:`Suppose this is possible. On the same base ΑΒ let ΑΔ and ΔΒ meet at Δ, distinct from Γ but on the same side. Let ΓΑ equal ΔΑ, both with endpoint Α, and ΓΒ equal ΔΒ, both with endpoint Β.`,greekPages:[24],latinPages:[25],language:`Εἰ … δυνατόν / si fieri potest introduces a possibility to be tested. The repeated αὐτός / idem keeps the base, side and paired endpoints fixed; ἄλλῳ / aliud makes the meeting points different. These conditions must be read together.`},{title:`Join the two proposed meeting points`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p1`,grc:`καὶ ἐπεζεύχθω ἡ ΓΔ.`,lat:`et ducatur ΓΔ.`,en:`And join Γ to Δ.`,greekPages:[24],latinPages:[25],language:`The singular imperative ἐπεζεύχθω asks for one joining line. It only makes sense as a new segment when Γ and Δ are distinct; moving the candidate onto Γ removes that hypothesis.`},{title:`Use the first isosceles triangle`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`i5`,grc:`Ἐπεὶ οὖν ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΑΓ τῇ ΑΔ, ἴση ἐστὶ καὶ γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΓΔ τῇ ὑπὸ ΑΔΓ·`,lat:`Iam quoniam ΑΓ = ΑΔ, etiam ∠ ΑΓΔ = ΑΔΓ.`,en:`Since ΑΓ equals ΑΔ, the angles ΑΓΔ and ΑΔΓ are equal as well.`,greekPages:[24,26],latinPages:[25,27],language:`Ἐπεὶ … / quoniam introduces the reason. Read the middle letter of each three-letter angle name as its vertex: first Γ, then Δ. The conclusion is an application of I.5, not a new given.`},{title:`Compare an angle with a proper part`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`anglePart`,grc:`μείζων ἄρα ἡ ὑπὸ ΑΔΓ τῆς ὑπὸ ΔΓΒ·`,lat:`quare ∠ ΑΔΓ > ΔΓΒ.`,en:`Therefore angle ΑΔΓ is greater than angle ΔΓΒ.`,greekPages:[26],latinPages:[27],language:`τῆς … is the genitive after the comparative μείζων: ‘greater than’. Latin compresses the comparison into the > sign. The angle containing the smaller part is the equal angle at Γ from the preceding step.`},{title:`Pass the strict comparison to the larger angle`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`angleOrder`,grc:`πολλῷ ἄρα ἡ ὑπὸ ΓΔΒ μείζων ἐστὶ τῆς ὑπὸ ΔΓΒ.`,lat:`itaque multo magis ∠ ΓΔΒ > ΔΓΒ.`,en:`Angle ΓΔΒ is therefore greater still than angle ΔΓΒ.`,greekPages:[26],latinPages:[27],language:`πολλῷ and multo express degree of difference: ‘by much’. They are not numerical angle measurements. The argument has added another whole–part comparison to the one already established.`},{title:`Apply the second supposed side equality`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`i5`,grc:`πάλιν ἐπεὶ ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΓΒ τῇ ΔΒ, ἴση ἐστὶ καὶ γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΓΔΒ γωνίᾳ τῇ ὑπὸ ΔΓΒ.`,lat:`rursus quoniam ΓΒ = ΔΒ, erit ∠ ΓΔΒ = ΔΓΒ.`,en:`Again, if ΓΒ equals ΔΒ as supposed, the angles ΓΔΒ and ΔΓΒ must be equal by I.5.`,greekPages:[26],latinPages:[27],language:`πάλιν / rursus means ‘again’, marking a second application of the same result to a different triangle. The English ‘as supposed’ makes the temporary status explicit; the Greek and Latin directly use the equality introduced at the start.`},{title:`The same angle cannot be equal and greater`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`impossibleOrder`,grc:`ἐδείχθη δὲ αὐτῆς καὶ πολλῷ μείζων· ὅπερ ἐστὶν ἀδύνατον.`,lat:`sed demonstratum est, eundem multo maiorem esse; quod fieri non potest.`,en:`But that same angle was shown to be greater than the other. The two claims cannot both hold.`,greekPages:[26],latinPages:[27],language:`αὐτῆς supplies ‘than it’ after μείζων. Latin eundem refers to the same angle already compared, with angulum understood. ἀδύνατον says ‘impossible’; compare I.6’s ἄτοπον, ‘absurd’.`},{title:`Keep every condition in the conclusion`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`theorem`,grc:`Οὐκ ἄρα ἐπὶ τῆς αὐτῆς εὐθείας δύο ταῖς αὐταῖς εὐθείαις ἄλλαι δύο εὐθεῖαι ἴσαι ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ συσταθήσονται πρὸς ἄλλῳ καὶ ἄλλῳ σημείῳ ἐπὶ τὰ αὐτὰ μέρη τὰ αὐτὰ πέρατα ἔχουσαι ταῖς ἐξ ἀρχῆς εὐθείαις· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo in eadem recta iisdem duabus rectis aliae duae rectae aequales altera alteri non constituentur ad aliud atque aliud punctum ad eandem partem eosdem terminos, quos priores rectae, habentes; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Thus the original two lengths cannot be reproduced from their respective endpoints of the same base to meet at a different point on the same side. Which was to be shown.`,greekPages:[26],latinPages:[27],language:`The future passive συσταθήσονται / constituentur returns from the heading, now with the rejection established. ‘On the same side’ remains part of the conclusion: the reflected example does not contradict this statement.`}],insight:{title:`A reflected solution shows what the hypothesis does`,text:`Reflecting Γ across ΑΒ preserves both distances to Α and Β, yet gives a different meeting point. It is excluded precisely by ‘on the same side’. The printed proof treats the configuration with Δ outside ΑΒΓ; other placements need additional case work. Our default same-side probe stays in that pictured configuration, preserves ΑΓ = ΑΔ, and measures the second pair honestly. It does not draw an impossible pair of distinct same-side circle intersections.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI7.html`},question:{prompt:`The reflected point has the same two distances. Why does it not refute I.7?`,options:[`Reflection changes one of the two distances`,`It lies on the other side of ΑΒ`,`I.7 requires the two distances to equal one another`],correct:1,explanations:[`Reflection in a line through both endpoints preserves both distances. The failed condition is the side of the base.`,`Yes. Both paired length equalities hold, but the second point is on the opposite side. I.7 explicitly excludes that configuration.`,`The two radii may differ. Each must equal its own counterpart from the same endpoint; they need not equal one another.`]}},8:{id:8,numeral:`I.8`,greekNumeral:`ηʹ`,title:`Let three sides determine the angle`,shortTitle:`Use three sides`,introduction:`Now the base is given equal instead of the included angle. Align the bases, then use I.7 to exclude a second apex on the selected side.`,statement:{grc:`Ἐὰν δύο τρίγωνα τὰς δύο πλευρὰς [ταῖς] δύο πλευραῖς ἴσας ἔχῃ ἑκατέραν ἑκατέρᾳ, ἔχῃ δὲ καὶ τὴν βάσιν τῇ βάσει ἴσην, καὶ τὴν γωνίαν τῇ γωνίᾳ ἴσην ἕξει τὴν ὑπὸ τῶν ἴσων εὐθειῶν περιεχομένην.`,lat:`Si duo trianguli duo latera duobus lateribus aequalia habent alterum alteri et praeterea basim basi aequalem habent, etiam angulos aequalibus rectis comprehensos aequales habebunt.`,en:`When two triangles have two respective sides equal and their bases equal too, the angles enclosed by those equal sides will also be equal.`,greekPages:[26],latinPages:[27]},steps:[{title:`Name the three side pairs and the angle to prove`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω δύο τρίγωνα τὰ ΑΒΓ, ΔΕΖ τὰς δύο πλευρὰς τὰς ΑΒ, ΑΓ ταῖς δύο πλευραῖς ταῖς ΔΕ, ΔΖ ἴσας ἔχοντα ἑκατέραν ἑκατέρᾳ, τὴν μὲν ΑΒ τῇ ΔΕ τὴν δὲ ΑΓ τῇ ΔΖ· ἐχέτω δὲ καὶ βάσιν τὴν ΒΓ βάσει τῇ ΕΖ ἴσην· λέγω, ὅτι καὶ γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΑΓ γωνίᾳ τῇ ὑπὸ ΕΔΖ ἐστιν ἴση.`,lat:`Sint duo trianguli ΑΒΓ, ΔΕΖ duo latera ΑΒ, ΑΓ duobus lateribus ΔΕ, ΔΖ aequalia habentes alterum alteri, ΑΒ = ΔΕ et ΑΓ = ΔΖ, et praeterea habeant ΒΓ = ΕΖ. dico, etiam esse ∠ ΒΑΓ = ΕΔΖ.`,en:`Let ΑΒΓ and ΔΕΖ have ΑΒ = ΔΕ, ΑΓ = ΔΖ and ΒΓ = ΕΖ. I claim that angle ΒΑΓ also equals angle ΕΔΖ.`,greekPages:[26],latinPages:[27],language:`ἐχέτω / habeant adds the equal-base condition. Compare I.4: there the included angle was given and the equal base was proved. Here those roles are exchanged. λέγω / dico introduces a claim still to be justified.`},{title:`Apply one equal base to the other`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`superposition`,grc:`Ἐφαρμοζομένου γὰρ τοῦ ΑΒΓ τριγώνου ἐπὶ τὸ ΔΕΖ τρίγωνον καὶ τιθεμένου τοῦ μὲν Β σημείου ἐπὶ τὸ Ε σημεῖον τῆς δὲ ΒΓ εὐθείας ἐπὶ τὴν ΕΖ ἐφαρμόσει καὶ τὸ Γ σημεῖον ἐπὶ τὸ Ζ διὰ τὸ ἴσην εἶναι τὴν ΒΓ τῇ ΕΖ·`,lat:`nam triangulo ΑΒΓ ad triangulum ΔΕΖ adplicato et puncto Β in Ε puncto posito recta autem ΒΓ in ΕΖ etiam Γ punctum in Ζ cadet, quia ΒΓ = ΕΖ.`,en:`Apply ΑΒΓ to ΔΕΖ, placing Β on Ε and ΒΓ along ΕΖ. Then Γ falls on Ζ because the bases are equal.`,greekPages:[26],latinPages:[27],language:`The Greek genitive absolutes ἐφαρμοζομένου and τιθεμένου set up the placement. Heiberg’s Latin uses ablative absolutes adplicato and posito. The corresponding endpoints are now Β → Ε and Γ → Ζ.`},{title:`Claim that the remaining sides must coincide`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`i7`,grc:`ἐφαρμοσάσης δὴ τῆς ΒΓ ἐπὶ τὴν ΕΖ ἐφαρμόσουσι καὶ αἱ ΒΑ, ΓΑ ἐπὶ τὰς ΕΔ, ΔΖ.`,lat:`adplicata iam ΒΓ rectae ΕΖ etiam ΒΑ, ΓΑ cum ΕΔ, ΔΖ congruent.`,en:`Once ΒΓ is on ΕΖ, the sides ΒΑ and ΓΑ must also coincide with ΕΔ and ΔΖ. The next step explains why.`,greekPages:[26,28],latinPages:[27,29],language:`The future ἐφαρμόσουσι / congruent states what must follow from applying the base. ΓΑ is the same segment as ΑΓ, named from the base endpoint here. The English last sentence marks the source’s claim-then-justification structure.`},{title:`Suppose the sides meet at another apex`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`i7`,grc:`εἰ γὰρ βάσις μὲν ἡ ΒΓ ἐπὶ βάσιν τὴν ΕΖ ἐφαρμόσει, αἱ δὲ ΒΑ, ΑΓ πλευραὶ ἐπὶ τὰς ΕΔ, ΔΖ οὐκ ἐφαρμόσουσιν ἀλλὰ παραλλάξουσιν ὡς αἱ ΕΗ, ΗΖ, συσταθήσονται ἐπὶ τῆς αὐτῆς εὐθείας δύο ταῖς αὐταῖς εὐθείαις ἄλλαι δύο εὐθεῖαι ἴσαι ἑκατέρα ἑκατέρᾳ πρὸς ἄλλῳ καὶ ἄλλῳ σημείῳ ἐπὶ τὰ αὐτὰ μέρη τὰ αὐτὰ πέρατα ἔχουσαι.`,lat:`nam si basis ΒΓ cum basi ΕΖ congruet, latera autem ΒΑ, ΑΓ cum ΕΔ, ΔΖ non congruent, uerum extra cadent, ut ΕΗ, ΗΖ, in eadem recta iisdem duabus rectis aliae duae rectae aequales altera alteri constituentur ad aliud atque aliud punctum ad eandem partem eosdem terminos habentes.`,en:`Suppose the bases coincide but the other sides fall elsewhere, along ΕΗ and ΗΖ. There would then be a different meeting point Η on the same side of ΕΖ, with the same two lengths from the same respective endpoints.`,greekPages:[28],latinPages:[29],language:`μὲν … δὲ separates what coincides (the base) from what is supposed not to coincide (the other sides). παραλλάξουσιν / extra cadent introduces a displaced candidate. The long final clause deliberately repeats the conditions of I.7.`},{title:`Exclude that candidate by I.7`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`i7`,grc:`οὐ συνίστανται δέ·`,lat:`sed non constituuntur.`,en:`But I.7 rules out such a second pair on the same side.`,greekPages:[28],latinPages:[29],language:`This brief denial relies on the entire description just given. The English names I.7 to make the reference explicit; Heiberg prints the proposition reference beside the Latin.`},{title:`Unpack the double negative`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`i7`,grc:`οὐκ ἄρα ἐφαρμοζομένης τῆς ΒΓ βάσεως ἐπὶ τὴν ΕΖ βάσιν οὐκ ἐφαρμόσουσι καὶ αἱ ΒΑ, ΑΓ πλευραὶ ἐπὶ τὰς ΕΔ, ΔΖ. ἐφαρμόσουσιν ἄρα·`,lat:`itaque fieri non potest, ut basi ΒΓ ad basim ΕΖ adplicata non congruant etiam latera ΒΑ, ΑΓ cum ΕΔ, ΔΖ. congruent igitur.`,en:`Therefore, with the bases aligned, it cannot be that the remaining sides fail to coincide with ΕΔ and ΔΖ. They must coincide.`,greekPages:[28],latinPages:[29],language:`The outer οὐκ denies the proposed failure expressed by the inner οὐκ. Latin separates the scopes clearly: non potest … non congruant, ‘it cannot happen that they do not coincide’. The final short clause gives the positive result.`},{title:`Conclude equality of the included angles`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`sssCoincidence`,grc:`ὥστε καὶ γωνία ἡ ὑπὸ ΒΑΓ ἐπὶ γωνίαν τὴν ὑπὸ ΕΔΖ ἐφαρμόσει καὶ ἴση αὐτῇ ἔσται.`,lat:`quare etiam angulus ΒΑΓ cum angulo ΕΔΖ congruet et ei aequalis erit.`,en:`Consequently angle ΒΑΓ coincides with angle ΕΔΖ and is equal to it.`,greekPages:[28],latinPages:[29],language:`αὐτῇ and ei are datives referring to the angle ΕΔΖ: ‘equal to it’. The angle equality is the result, not an extra premise supplied by the drawing.`},{title:`Three side pairs have determined the angle`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`theorem`,grc:`Ἐὰν ἄρα δύο τρίγωνα τὰς δύο πλευρὰς [ταῖς] δύο πλευραῖς ἴσας ἔχῃ ἑκατέραν ἑκατέρᾳ καὶ τὴν βάσιν τῇ βάσει ἴσην ἔχῃ, καὶ τὴν γωνίαν τῇ γωνίᾳ ἴσην ἕξει τὴν ὑπὸ τῶν ἴσων εὐθειῶν περιεχομένην· ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.`,lat:`Ergo si duo trianguli duo latera duobus lateribus aequalia habent alterum alteri et basim basi aequalem habent, etiam angulos aequalibus rectis comprehensos aequales habebunt; quod erat demonstrandum.`,en:`Thus two triangles with those three respective side pairs equal have equal angles between the first two sides. Which was to be shown.`,greekPages:[28],latinPages:[29],language:`The bracketed [ταῖς] remains as in Heiberg’s printed Greek. The result’s future ἕξει / habebunt returns to the general statement, beyond the particular labelled triangles.`}],insight:{title:`The angle is earned, not set as a hidden given`,text:`The model reconstructs an apex from three lengths. You can enter lengths that do not close into a triangle; the reader then withholds the triangle. When they do close, I.7 supplies the source argument for the apex after the equal bases are applied. The motion still illustrates an additional superposition assumption. Changing the data is a numerical exploration, not a replacement proof of I.7.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI8.html`},question:{prompt:`What prevents a distinct apex Η after the bases have been aligned?`,options:[`The included angle was already given equal`,`The sketch looks symmetric`,`I.7, with the same-side and corresponding-endpoint conditions`],correct:2,explanations:[`That was I.4’s premise. In I.8 the included angle is what must be proved equal.`,`The triangles need not be isosceles or symmetric. The exclusion uses the two distances from the same endpoints.`,`Yes. After fixing the base, I.7 excludes a different meeting point with those two distances on the selected side.`]}}},g={...f,...m,...n,...u,...o,...e,...i,...c,given:{title:`The task and its given objects`,text:`A given object is part of the problem. It has not been constructed by this proof. A finite straight line here means a line segment.`,kind:`given`},p1:{title:`Postulate 1 · join two points`,text:`A straight line may be drawn from any point to any point. The straightedge is unmarked: this operation does not measure a length.`,kind:`construction`},p2:{title:`Postulate 2 · extend a segment`,text:`A finite straight line may be continued in a straight line. In I.2 the extensions go away from Δ, past Α and Β. No new length is measured here.`,kind:`construction`},p3:{title:`Postulate 3 · draw a circle`,text:`A circle may be described with a centre and a distance. Here the distance is from the centre to an already available point; drawing this circle does not silently carry a length to a different centre.`,kind:`construction`},intersection:{title:`Postulate 1 + an intersection assumption`,text:`Join Γ to Α and Β. Euclid takes the meeting of the two circles for granted. A modern axiomatic account must justify that intersection too; the picture alone is not a proof that a point exists.`,kind:`assumption`},d15:{title:`Definition 15 · radii of one circle`,text:`The straight lines from the centre to the circumference are equal. Identify the centre and the two points on that circle before asserting a length equality.`,kind:`definition`},cn1:{title:`Common notion 1 · equality through a shared equal`,text:`Things equal to the same thing are also equal to one another. This connects equalities already established; no measurement of the picture is needed.`,kind:`deduction`},cn3:{title:`Common notion 3 · subtract equal parts`,text:`If equals are subtracted from equals, the remainders are equal. Here ΔΛ = ΔΗ and ΔΑ = ΔΒ. Subtract the second pair from the first to obtain ΑΛ = ΒΗ.`,kind:`deduction`},d20:{title:`Definition 20 · equilateral triangle`,text:`An equilateral triangle has three equal sides. The two circle equalities and common notion 1 supply all three; how the figure happens to look is irrelevant.`,kind:`definition`},i1:{title:`I.1 · reuse the equilateral construction`,text:`The previous proposition constructs an equilateral triangle on ΑΒ. Its equal sides ΔΑ and ΔΒ will later be subtracted. This plate shows the completed subconstruction; I.1 shows its two circles.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:1},i2:{title:`I.2 · copy the reference length to Α`,text:`The previous proposition gives a segment at Α equal to the separate reference Γ. Its result is named ΑΔ here. This is a complete earlier construction, not an unexplained compass transfer.`,kind:`construction`,lesson:2},equalSides:{title:`Definition 15 + I.1`,text:`The larger circle gives ΔΛ = ΔΗ. The equilateral triangle from I.1 gives ΔΑ = ΔΒ. Equal whole lengths and equal parts are now available for the next step.`,kind:`deduction`,lesson:1},finish:{title:`The construction is complete`,text:`The requested object has been constructed and its defining equality has been proved. The closing phrase says that the task was to make something, rather than merely state a theorem.`,kind:`deduction`}},_={...p,...h,...r,...d,...s,...t,...a,...l,1:{id:1,numeral:`I.1`,greekNumeral:`αʹ`,title:`Construct an equilateral triangle`,shortTitle:`Make equal sides`,introduction:`Two circles turn a given line into three equal sides. Read how the commands become a construction, and how the construction becomes a proof.`,statement:{grc:`Ἐπὶ τῆς δοθείσης εὐθείας πεπερασμένης τρίγωνον ἰσόπλευρον συστήσασθαι.`,lat:`In data recta terminata triangulum aequilaterum construere.`,en:`To construct an equilateral triangle on a given finite straight line.`,greekPages:[10],latinPages:[11]},steps:[{title:`Begin with the given line`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω ἡ δοθεῖσα εὐθεῖα πεπερασμένη ἡ ΑΒ. Δεῖ δὴ ἐπὶ τῆς ΑΒ εὐθείας τρίγωνον ἰσόπλευρον συστήσασθαι.`,lat:`Sit data recta terminata ΑΒ. oportet igitur in recta ΑΒ terminata triangulum aequilaterum construere.`,en:`Let ΑΒ be the given finite straight line. We must construct an equilateral triangle on ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[10],latinPages:[11],language:`Ἔστω means ‘let … be’: a third-person imperative. Latin uses sit, a subjunctive with the same instructional role. Neither word is a claim that the reader has proved something.`},{title:`Draw a circle centred at Α`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p3`,grc:`Κέντρῳ μὲν τῷ Α διαστήματι δὲ τῷ ΑΒ κύκλος γεγράφθω ὁ ΒΓΔ,`,lat:`centro Α et radio ΑΒ circulus describatur ΒΓΔ,`,en:`With centre Α and radius ΑΒ, let the circle ΒΓΔ be drawn.`,greekPages:[10,12],latinPages:[11],language:`Κέντρῳ and διαστήματι are datives: ‘with centre’ and ‘with distance’. γεγράφθω asks for a completed drawing: ‘let it have been drawn’. Latin describatur gives the corresponding command.`},{title:`Draw the second circle`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p3`,grc:`καὶ πάλιν κέντρῳ μὲν τῷ Β διαστήματι δὲ τῷ ΒΑ κύκλος γεγράφθω ὁ ΑΓΕ,`,lat:`et rursus centro Β radio autem ΒΑ circulus describatur ΑΓΕ,`,en:`Again, with centre Β and radius ΒΑ, let the circle ΑΓΕ be drawn.`,greekPages:[12],latinPages:[13],language:`πάλιν and Latin rursus both mean ‘again’. ΑΒ and ΒΑ name the same length, but the order helps you see which endpoint is now the centre.`},{title:`Join the intersection to the endpoints`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`intersection`,grc:`καὶ ἀπὸ τοῦ Γ σημείου, καθ᾽ ὃ τέμνουσιν ἀλλήλους οἱ κύκλοι, ἐπὶ τὰ Α, Β σημεῖα ἐπεζεύχθωσαν εὐθεῖαι αἱ ΓΑ, ΓΒ.`,lat:`et a puncto Γ, in quo circuli inter se secant, ad puncta Α, Β ducantur rectae ΓΑ, ΓΒ.`,en:`From Γ, where the circles meet, draw the straight lines ΓΑ and ΓΒ to Α and Β.`,greekPages:[12],latinPages:[13],language:`ἀπό … ἐπί marks ‘from … to’; Latin uses a … ad. The plural command ἐπεζεύχθωσαν agrees with the two lines to be joined. Γ is gamma, the third letter, corresponding to C in many English editions.`},{title:`Read the first pair of radii`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`d15`,grc:`Καὶ ἐπεὶ τὸ Α σημεῖον κέντρον ἐστὶ τοῦ ΓΔΒ κύκλου, ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΑΓ τῇ ΑΒ·`,lat:`iam quoniam punctum Α centrum est circuli ΓΔΒ, erit ΑΓ = ΑΒ.`,en:`Since Α is the centre of circle ΓΔΒ, ΑΓ is equal to ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[12],latinPages:[13],language:`ἐπεὶ and quoniam introduce ‘since’. ἴση is feminine because the understood noun εὐθεῖα (‘straight line’) is feminine. τῇ ΑΒ means ‘to ΑΒ’ after ‘equal’.`},{title:`Read the second pair of radii`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`d15`,grc:`πάλιν, ἐπεὶ τὸ Β σημεῖον κέντρον ἐστὶ τοῦ ΓΑΕ κύκλου, ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΒΓ τῇ ΒΑ.`,lat:`rursus quoniam Β punctum centrum est circuli ΓΑΕ, est ΒΓ = ΒΑ.`,en:`Again, since Β is the centre of circle ΓΑΕ, ΒΓ is equal to ΒΑ.`,greekPages:[12],latinPages:[13],language:`The wording repeats the first radius argument with Β as centre. Repetition in a proof is useful reading practice: only the point names change.`},{title:`Find the shared equal length`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`cn1`,grc:`ἐδείχθη δὲ καὶ ἡ ΓΑ τῇ ΑΒ ἴση· ἑκατέρα ἄρα τῶν ΓΑ, ΓΒ τῇ ΑΒ ἐστιν ἴση.`,lat:`sed demonstratum est etiam ΓΑ = ΑΒ. quare utraque ΓΑ, ΓΒ rectae ΑΒ aequalis est.`,en:`But ΓΑ was also shown equal to ΑΒ. Thus each of ΓΑ and ΓΒ is equal to ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[12],latinPages:[13],language:`ἐδείχθη means ‘was shown’. demonstratum est is likewise passive. ἄρα and quare signal that a conclusion follows; they are signposts in the argument.`},{title:`Connect the equalities`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`cn1`,grc:`τὰ δὲ τῷ αὐτῷ ἴσα καὶ ἀλλήλοις ἐστὶν ἴσα· καὶ ἡ ΓΑ ἄρα τῇ ΓΒ ἐστιν ἴση· αἱ τρεῖς ἄρα αἱ ΓΑ, ΑΒ, ΒΓ ἴσαι ἀλλήλαις εἰσίν.`,lat:`quae autem eidem aequalia sunt, etiam inter se aequalia sunt. itaque etiam ΓΑ = ΓΒ. itaque ΓΑ, ΑΒ, ΒΓ aequales sunt.`,en:`Things equal to the same thing are also equal to one another. Hence ΓΑ equals ΓΒ, and the three lines ΓΑ, ΑΒ and ΒΓ are equal to one another.`,greekPages:[12],latinPages:[13],language:`τὰ … ἴσα (‘the equal things’) is neuter plural: the statement applies generally to things, not just lines. Latin quae … aequalia uses the same general form.`},{title:`Name what has been made`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`d20`,grc:`ἰσόπλευρον ἄρα ἐστὶ τὸ ΑΒΓ τρίγωνον. καὶ συνέσταται ἐπὶ τῆς δοθείσης εὐθείας πεπερασμένης τῆς ΑΒ.`,lat:`quare triangulus ΑΒΓ aequilaterus est; et in data recta terminata ΑΒ constructus est.`,en:`Therefore ΑΒΓ is an equilateral triangle, constructed on the given finite straight line ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[12],latinPages:[13],language:`ἰσόπλευρον combines ἴσος (‘equal’) and πλευρά (‘side’). aequilaterus combines aequus and latus. The Greek and Latin describe the same geometric property.`},{title:`Which was to be done`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`finish`,grc:`[Ἐπὶ τῆς δοθείσης ἄρα εὐθείας πεπερασμένης τρίγωνον ἰσόπλευρον συνέσταται] ὅπερ ἔδει ποιῆσαι.`,lat:`quod oportebat fieri.`,en:`[Thus an equilateral triangle has been constructed on the given finite straight line:] which was to be done.`,greekPages:[12],latinPages:[13],language:`ὅπερ ἔδει ποιῆσαι closes a construction. Latin quod oportebat fieri means ‘which had to be done’. Heiberg brackets the repeated Greek conclusion; the Latin omits that repetition. The brackets are retained here.`}],insight:{title:`An intersection the picture takes for granted`,text:`You can see where the two circles cross, but a drawing does not establish that an intersection exists. Euclid uses it without an explicit circle-intersection axiom. Our coordinates use the real plane; this supplies a modern setting, rather than filling that gap in Euclid’s stated postulates. Flip Γ to see the other valid intersection.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI1.html`},question:{prompt:`Why does ΑΓ equal ΑΒ?`,options:[`They are radii of the circle centred at Α.`,`The two circles have the same centre.`,`The drawing makes the sides look equal.`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. Γ and Β lie on the circle centred at Α, so definition 15 gives ΑΓ = ΑΒ. This does not depend on measuring the screen.`,`The centres are different: Α and Β. Look at the single circle centred at Α and identify its two radii.`,`A picture can suggest an equality. The reason is that Γ and Β are on one circle with centre Α.`]}},2:{id:2,numeral:`I.2`,greekNumeral:`βʹ`,title:`Carry a length without carrying the compass`,shortTitle:`Move a length`,introduction:`A reference line lies away from Α. Construct an equal length at Α, using an equilateral triangle and subtraction. The surprising part is what the two equal sides let you remove.`,statement:{grc:`Πρὸς τῷ δοθέντι σημείῳ τῇ δοθείσῃ εὐθείᾳ ἴσην εὐθεῖαν θέσθαι.`,lat:`Ad datum punctum datae rectae aequalem rectam constituere.`,en:`To place, at a given point, a straight line equal to a given straight line.`,greekPages:[12],latinPages:[13]},steps:[{title:`A point and a separate length`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστω τὸ μὲν δοθὲν σημεῖον τὸ Α, ἡ δὲ δοθεῖσα εὐθεῖα ἡ ΒΓ· δεῖ δὴ πρὸς τῷ Α σημείῳ τῇ δοθείσῃ εὐθείᾳ τῇ ΒΓ ἴσην εὐθεῖαν θέσθαι.`,lat:`Sit datum punctum Α, data autem recta ΒΓ. oportet igitur ad punctum Α datae rectae ΒΓ aequalem rectam constituere.`,en:`Let Α be the given point and ΒΓ the given straight line. We must place at Α a straight line equal to ΒΓ.`,greekPages:[12],latinPages:[13],language:`μέν … δέ pairs two items: the given point on the one hand, the given line on the other. δοθέν and δοθεῖσα both mean ‘given’; their forms agree with the different nouns.`},{title:`Join Α to Β`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p1`,grc:`Ἐπεζεύχθω γὰρ ἀπὸ τοῦ Α σημείου ἐπὶ τὸ Β σημεῖον εὐθεῖα ἡ ΑΒ,`,lat:`ducatur enim a puncto Α ad Β punctum recta ΑΒ,`,en:`Draw the straight line ΑΒ from point Α to point Β.`,greekPages:[12],latinPages:[13],language:`ἐπεζεύχθω is singular: one line is to be joined. Compare the plural ἐπεζεύχθωσαν in I.1, where two lines were joined.`},{title:`Use I.1 on the new line`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`i1`,grc:`καὶ συνεστάτω ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς τρίγωνον ἰσόπλευρον τὸ ΔΑΒ.`,lat:`et in ea construatur triangulus aequilaterus ΔΑΒ,`,en:`On it construct the equilateral triangle ΔΑΒ.`,greekPages:[12],latinPages:[13],language:`ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς and in ea both mean ‘on it’, referring back to ΑΒ. The point name Δ is delta; this triangle supplies the equality ΔΑ = ΔΒ.`},{title:`Extend past Α and Β`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p2`,grc:`καὶ ἐκβεβλήσθωσαν ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας ταῖς ΔΑ, ΔΒ εὐθεῖαι αἱ ΑΕ, ΒΖ,`,lat:`et producantur in directum rectae ΔΑ, ΔΒ, ut fiant ΑΕ, ΒΖ,`,en:`Continue ΔΑ and ΔΒ in straight lines beyond Α and Β, forming ΑΕ and ΒΖ.`,greekPages:[12,14],latinPages:[13,15],language:`ἐκβεβλήσθωσαν commands that the lines be extended. Latin producantur in directum says ‘let them be extended straight on’. The directions matter: go away from Δ.`},{title:`Mark the reference length from Β`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p3`,grc:`καὶ κέντρῳ μὲν τῷ Β διαστήματι δὲ τῷ ΒΓ κύκλος γεγράφθω ὁ ΓΗΘ.`,lat:`et centro Β radio autem ΒΓ circulus describatur ΓΗΘ,`,en:`With centre Β and radius ΒΓ, draw circle ΓΗΘ. Η lies on the extension beyond Β.`,greekPages:[14],latinPages:[15],language:`Η is eta, not the Latin letter H. The circle is named by points on its circumference; Θ is another such point in the source figure. This plate labels the points needed for the argument.`},{title:`Reach both extended sides from Δ`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p3`,grc:`καὶ πάλιν κέντρῳ τῷ Δ καὶ διαστήματι τῷ ΔΗ κύκλος γεγράφθω ὁ ΗΚΛ.`,lat:`et rursus centro Δ radio autem ΔΗ circulus describatur ΗΚΛ.`,en:`Again, with centre Δ and radius ΔΗ, draw circle ΗΚΛ. Λ lies on the extension beyond Α.`,greekPages:[14],latinPages:[15],language:`Λ is lambda. The names ΗΚΛ identify the second circle. Κ is not needed in the subtraction argument, so the plate shows the working arc through Η and Λ.`},{title:`Identify the first equal radii`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`d15`,grc:`Ἐπεὶ οὖν τὸ Β σημεῖον κέντρον ἐστὶ τοῦ ΓΗΘ, ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΒΓ τῇ ΒΗ.`,lat:`iam quoniam Β punctum centrum est circuli ΓΗΘ, erit ΒΓ = ΒΗ.`,en:`Since Β is the centre of circle ΓΗΘ, ΒΓ equals ΒΗ.`,greekPages:[14],latinPages:[15],language:`The radius argument from I.1 appears again. ΒΓ is the given length, and ΒΗ is now an equal length on the extended side.`},{title:`Identify equal wholes and equal parts`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`equalSides`,grc:`πάλιν, ἐπεὶ τὸ Δ σημεῖον κέντρον ἐστὶ τοῦ ΗΚΛ κύκλου, ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΔΛ τῇ ΔΗ, ὧν ἡ ΔΑ τῇ ΔΒ ἴση ἐστίν.`,lat:`rursus quoniam Δ punctum centrum est circuli ΗΚΛ, erit ΔΛ = ΔΗ, quarum partes ΔΑ, ΔΒ aequales.`,en:`Since Δ is the centre of circle ΗΚΛ, ΔΛ equals ΔΗ; within them, the parts ΔΑ and ΔΒ are equal.`,greekPages:[14],latinPages:[15],language:`ὧν means ‘of which’. Heiberg’s Latin makes the implied relationship explicit with quarum partes: ‘whose parts’. The equal parts come from the equilateral triangle.`},{title:`Subtract the equal parts`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`cn3`,grc:`λοιπὴ ἄρα ἡ ΑΛ λοιπῇ τῇ ΒΗ ἐστιν ἴση.`,lat:`itaque ΑΛ = ΒΗ.`,en:`Therefore the remainder ΑΛ is equal to the remainder ΒΗ.`,greekPages:[14],latinPages:[15],language:`λοιπή means ‘remaining’. Greek names the remainders, while the Latin prints an equation. The language encodes an operation: remove ΔΑ and ΔΒ, leaving ΑΛ and ΒΗ.`},{title:`Connect the remainder to the reference`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`cn1`,grc:`ἐδείχθη δὲ καὶ ἡ ΒΓ τῇ ΒΗ ἴση· ἑκατέρα ἄρα τῶν ΑΛ, ΒΓ τῇ ΒΗ ἐστιν ἴση. τὰ δὲ τῷ αὐτῷ ἴσα καὶ ἀλλήλοις ἐστὶν ἴσα· καὶ ἡ ΑΛ ἄρα τῇ ΒΓ ἐστιν ἴση.`,lat:`sed demonstratum est ΒΓ = ΒΗ. itaque utraque ΑΛ, ΒΓ rectae ΒΗ aequalis est. uerum quae eidem aequalia sunt, etiam inter se aequalia sunt. ergo etiam ΑΛ = ΒΓ.`,en:`ΒΓ was also shown equal to ΒΗ. Since ΑΛ and ΒΓ are each equal to ΒΗ, they are equal to one another: ΑΛ = ΒΓ.`,greekPages:[14],latinPages:[15],language:`ἑκατέρα means ‘each of the two’; Latin utraque has the same role. The proof repeats common notion 1 rather than relying on the appearance of the diagram.`},{title:`The new length starts at Α`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`finish`,grc:`Πρὸς ἄρα τῷ δοθέντι σημείῳ τῷ Α τῇ δοθείσῃ εὐθείᾳ τῇ ΒΓ ἴση εὐθεῖα κεῖται ἡ ΑΛ· ὅπερ ἔδει ποιῆσαι.`,lat:`Ergo ad datum punctum Α datae rectae ΒΓ aequalis constituta est recta ΑΛ; quod oportebat fieri.`,en:`At the given point Α, the straight line ΑΛ has been placed equal to the given straight line ΒΓ: which was to be done.`,greekPages:[14],latinPages:[15],language:`κεῖται (‘lies’ or ‘has been placed’) answers the opening task θέσθαι (‘to place’). Latin constituere is answered by constituta est. The end echoes the beginning.`}],insight:{title:`The length survives because equal parts disappear`,text:`The equilateral triangle looks like a detour. Its purpose is to create equal removable parts. ΔΛ and ΔΗ are radii of one circle; removing equal ΔΑ and ΔΒ leaves equal ΑΛ and ΒΗ. ‘Collapsing compass’ is a modern description of the restricted operation, not evidence about an actual ancient instrument. Change ΒΓ or flip the triangle: the subtraction still works.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI2.html`},question:{prompt:`What allows ΑΛ = ΒΗ after the two circles have been drawn?`,options:[`Any two circles give equal remainders.`,`Subtract equal ΔΑ and ΔΒ from equal ΔΛ and ΔΗ.`,`Α and Β are the same point.`],correct:1,explanations:[`Circle radii alone give equal wholes, ΔΛ and ΔΗ. You also need the equal parts ΔΑ and ΔΒ from I.1.`,`Yes. Common notion 3 applies because I.1 supplies the equal parts. Then ΒΗ = ΒΓ gives the required copied length.`,`Α and Β are different. Their separation is why I.1 can construct the equilateral triangle used in the argument.`]}},3:{id:3,numeral:`I.3`,greekNumeral:`γʹ`,title:`Cut a smaller length from a larger one`,shortTitle:`Cut a length`,introduction:`The first two propositions now become tools. Copy the separate reference to Α, then let a circle locate exactly where to cut the longer line.`,statement:{grc:`Δύο δοθεισῶν εὐθειῶν ἀνίσων ἀπὸ τῆς μείζονος τῇ ἐλάσσονι ἴσην εὐθεῖαν ἀφελεῖν.`,lat:`Datis duabus rectis inaequalibus rectam minori aequalem a maiore abscindere.`,en:`Given two unequal straight lines, to cut from the greater a straight line equal to the lesser.`,greekPages:[14],latinPages:[15]},steps:[{title:`Name the greater and the lesser`,phase:`Given`,reason:`given`,grc:`Ἔστωσαν αἱ δοθεῖσαι δύο εὐθεῖαι ἄνισοι αἱ ΑΒ, Γ, ὧν μείζων ἔστω ἡ ΑΒ· δεῖ δὴ ἀπὸ τῆς μείζονος τῆς ΑΒ τῇ ἐλάσσονι τῇ Γ ἴσην εὐθεῖαν ἀφελεῖν.`,lat:`Sint duae datae rectae inaequales ΑΒ, Γ, quarum maior sit ΑΒ. oportet igitur a maiore ΑΒ minori Γ aequalem rectam abscindere.`,en:`Let ΑΒ and Γ be the two given unequal straight lines, with ΑΒ the greater. We must cut from ΑΒ a straight line equal to Γ, the lesser.`,greekPages:[14],latinPages:[15],language:`Γ names an entire segment here, not a point. μείζων / maior mean ‘greater’; ἐλάσσων / minor mean ‘lesser’. The hypothesis matters: try a reference length of 100% or more.`},{title:`Place the reference length at Α`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`i2`,grc:`Κείσθω πρὸς τῷ Α σημείῳ τῇ Γ εὐθείᾳ ἴση ἡ ΑΔ·`,lat:`constituatur ad Α punctum rectae Γ aequalis ΑΔ,`,en:`At point Α, place ΑΔ equal to the straight line Γ.`,greekPages:[14],latinPages:[15],language:`Κείσθω (‘let it be placed’) is a command using the same verb as κεῖται in I.2’s conclusion. A completed result has become an available instruction.`},{title:`Draw the circle that locates the cut`,phase:`Construct`,reason:`p3`,grc:`καὶ κέντρῳ μὲν τῷ Α διαστήματι δὲ τῷ ΑΔ κύκλος γεγράφθω ὁ ΔΕΖ.`,lat:`et centro Α radio autem ΑΔ describatur circulus ΔΕΖ.`,en:`With centre Α and radius ΑΔ, draw circle ΔΕΖ. Ε is its intersection with ΑΒ.`,greekPages:[14],latinPages:[15],language:`The centre–radius sentence from I.1 is now familiar. The same Greek command γεγράφθω recurs. Ζ is another point naming the circle in the source figure; the plate labels the working points.`},{title:`Connect the cut to the reference`,phase:`Prove`,reason:`cn1`,grc:`Καὶ ἐπεὶ τὸ Α σημεῖον κέντρον ἐστὶ τοῦ ΔΕΖ κύκλου, ἴση ἐστὶν ἡ ΑΕ τῇ ΑΔ· ἀλλὰ καὶ ἡ Γ τῇ ΑΔ ἐστιν ἴση. ἑκατέρα ἄρα τῶν ΑΕ, Γ τῇ ΑΔ ἐστιν ἴση· ὥστε καὶ ἡ ΑΕ τῇ Γ ἐστιν ἴση.`,lat:`Et quoniam punctum Α centrum est circuli ΔΕΖ, est ΑΕ = ΑΔ; uerum etiam Γ = ΑΔ. itaque utraque ΑΕ, Γ rectae ΑΔ aequalis est; ergo etiam ΑΕ = Γ.`,en:`Because Α is the centre of circle ΔΕΖ, ΑΕ = ΑΔ. But Γ = ΑΔ as well. Both are equal to ΑΔ, so ΑΕ = Γ.`,greekPages:[16],latinPages:[17],language:`ὥστε and ergo introduce the consequence. The equality ΑΕ = ΑΔ comes from the circle, while Γ = ΑΔ comes from I.2. Common notion 1 links those two different reasons.`},{title:`The required part has been cut off`,phase:`Conclude`,reason:`finish`,grc:`Δύο ἄρα δοθεισῶν εὐθειῶν ἀνίσων τῶν ΑΒ, Γ ἀπὸ τῆς μείζονος τῆς ΑΒ τῇ ἐλάσσονι τῇ Γ ἴση ἀφῄρηται ἡ ΑΕ· ὅπερ ἔδει ποιῆσαι.`,lat:`Ergo datis duabus rectis inaequalibus ΑΒ, Γ a maiore ΑΒ minori Γ aequalis abscisa est ΑΕ; quod oportebat fieri.`,en:`From ΑΒ, the greater of the two given unequal straight lines, ΑΕ has been cut off equal to Γ, the lesser: which was to be done.`,greekPages:[16],latinPages:[17],language:`ἀφελεῖν at the start was ‘to take away’; ἀφῄρηται now says ‘has been taken away’. Latin abscindere becomes abscisa est. This change from task to completed result is a recurring pattern.`}],insight:{title:`A result becomes an instruction`,text:`I.3 can say ‘place an equal line at Α’ because I.2 has already earned that operation. I.2 in turn used I.1. The chain turns a few primitive permissions into more capable tools. Move the reference to 100% or beyond: the stated greater–lesser hypothesis fails, so this workbench withholds the cut rather than putting it beyond Β.`,link:`https://mathcs.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/propI3.html`},question:{prompt:`Why is I.2 invoked before drawing the final circle?`,options:[`To establish an equal radius starting at Α.`,`To make the reference line longer.`,`To measure the line with a marked ruler.`],correct:0,explanations:[`Yes. The reference is separate from Α. I.2 constructs ΑΔ equal to it, so the circle at Α can be drawn through Δ.`,`The reference length stays the same. I.2 relocates an equal segment to the needed starting point.`,`The construction uses an unmarked straightedge and circles. No marked ruler measurement is part of the proof.`]}}},v=(e,t)=>({x:e.x+t.x,y:e.y+t.y}),y=(e,t)=>({x:e.x-t.x,y:e.y-t.y}),b=(e,t)=>({x:e.x*t,y:e.y*t}),x=(e,t)=>Math.hypot(e.x-t.x,e.y-t.y);function S(...e){if(e.some(e=>!Number.isFinite(e.x)||!Number.isFinite(e.y)))throw RangeError(`Points must have finite coordinates.`)}function C(e,t){S(e,t);let n=x(e,t);if(n===0)throw RangeError(`The two endpoints must be different.`);return b(y(t,e),1/n)}function w(e,t,n=1){C(e,t);let r=y(t,e);return v(b(v(e,t),.5),b({x:-r.y,y:r.x},n*Math.sqrt(3)/2))}function T(e,t,n,r=1){S(e,t,n);let i=x(t,n);if(i===0)throw RangeError(`The reference segment must have positive length.`);let a=w(e,t,r),o=v(t,b(C(a,t),i)),s=x(a,o);return{a:e,b:t,c:n,d:a,g:o,l:v(a,b(C(a,e),s)),reference:i,whole:s}}function E(e,t,n,r,i=1){S(e,t,n,r);let a=x(e,t),o=x(n,r);if(!(o>0&&o<a))throw RangeError(`I.3 requires two positive unequal lengths; the reference must be shorter.`);let s=x(e,n)===0?T(e,r,n,i):T(e,n,r,i),c=x(e,s.l);return{a:e,b:t,c:n,d:r,copy:s,e:v(e,b(C(e,t),c)),radius:c}}export{b as a,_ as c,w as i,g as l,E as n,y as o,x as r,T as s,v as t};